\ shell.4 - a minimal POSIX-flavored shell built on RelF.
\
\ Load on top of an already-bootstrapped kernel.img:
\   S" shell.4" INCLUDED
\   SH
\
\ *** IMPORTANT for anyone editing this file: use only "\" line
\ *** comments for anything spanning more than one physical line.
\ *** A "(...)" comment that spans multiple physical lines can
\ *** silently corrupt parsing once enough code precedes it earlier
\ *** in this file, surfacing as a cascade of unrelated "Undefined
\ *** word" errors when the file is next loaded - confirmed
\ *** empirically in Iteration 33 (see PROGRESS.md), where an
\ *** unmodified, pre-existing six-line "(...)" comment broke only
\ *** after unrelated new code was added earlier in the file; 200
\ *** trivial filler word definitions inserted before it reproduced
\ *** the identical failure in an otherwise pristine copy. Single-line
\ *** "(...)" comments, however long, are fine. If you see this
\ *** symptom (unrelated "Undefined word" errors right after an edit
\ *** that "should" be safe), check for multi-line "(...)" comments
\ *** before assuming a logic bug in whatever was just changed.

\ Scope (v0.1), deliberately limited - see PROGRESS.md for the phased
\ plan this fits into:
\   - word-splitting on whitespace only (no quoting/escaping)
\   - PATH-searched external commands via FORK/EXECVE/WAITPID
\   - builtins: cd, exit, export, pwd
\ NOT implemented yet (planned as separate, later increments):
\   pipes, redirection, $VAR expansion, $(...) command substitution,
\   control structures, functions, job control, quoting of any kind.

\ ---- sizes ----

256 CONSTANT LINE-MAX
64  CONSTANT MAX-ARGS      \ what the word arrays START at: see ARGS-BUFFER:

\ ==================================================================
\ The word arrays grow (Iteration 250)
\ ==================================================================
\ ARGV and about thirty arrays like it - copies of it, and arrays
\ parallel to it - hold one entry per word of a line, and every one of
\ them is indexed up to ARGC without a check of its own: the only bound
\ was that ARGC never passed MAX-ARGS. So they must all grow TOGETHER.
\ ARGS-BUFFER: declares one and chains it; GROW-ARGS walks the chain,
\ so an array declared this way cannot be forgotten. ARGS-CAP is the
\ entries every one of them has room for, and replaces MAX-ARGS
\ wherever the code asks "is there room".
\
\ Growth copies and retires (pool.4's BUF-ENSURE), and nothing keeps an
\ array's ADDRESS: everything names it, so after a growth everything
\ sees the new block with the same contents.
\
\ ARGS-HARD-MAX is the ceiling: 28 arrays of up to a cell each per
\ word, so 65,536 words is at most about 15 MB. Past it the words are
\ dropped and the line is refused, as every line was past 64 before.

65536 CONSTANT ARGS-HARD-MAX
VARIABLE ARGS-CAP     MAX-ARGS ARGS-CAP !
VARIABLE ARGS-ARRAYS  0 ARGS-ARRAYS !   \ newest chain entry, as an offset

: ARGS-BUFFER: ( entry-bytes "name" --- )
\ A BUFFER: of MAX-ARGS entries that GROW-ARGS keeps at ARGS-CAP. The
\ chain entry follows the descriptor: [link][descriptor][entry-bytes],
\ all offsets from START, so they survive a save and reload.
  DUP MAX-ARGS * BUFFER:
  HERE                                  ( n entry )
  ARGS-ARRAYS @ ,
  BUF-BODY @ START @ - ,
  SWAP ,
  START @ - ARGS-ARRAYS ! ;

: GROW-ARGS ( n --- )
\ Make every word array hold at least n entries - at least doubling, up
\ to ARGS-HARD-MAX.
  DUP ARGS-CAP @ > 0= IF DROP EXIT THEN
  ARGS-CAP @ 2* MAX ARGS-HARD-MAX MIN
  ARGS-ARRAYS @
  BEGIN ?DUP WHILE
    START @ +                           ( n entry )
    2DUP 2 CELLS + @ *                  ( n entry bytes )
    OVER CELL+ @ START @ + BUF-ENSURE
    @                                   ( n link )
  REPEAT
  ARGS-CAP ! ;

: ROOM-FOR-ARG? ( i --- f )
\ Can entry i+1 be used? Grows the arrays if it can; false only at the
\ ceiling. i is the count so far, and one entry is kept for a NUL.
  2 + DUP ARGS-HARD-MAX > IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  GROW-ARGS -1 ;

VARIABLE ARGS-OVERFLOW?
\ Set whenever a word had to be dropped because ARGV is full, and
\ reported once per line by RUN-LINE. Until Iteration 154 hitting
\ MAX-ARGS was completely silent: 70 arguments came back as 62 plus a
\ spurious empty one, and `set -- <70 words>; echo $#` printed 9. The
\ command then RAN, with a quietly different argument list than the
\ script asked for, which is the worst of the available behaviours.
\ Iteration 90 gave diagnostics to SET-SHVAR, SET-FUNC and the
\ positional-parameter stack but never reached this path.
256 CONSTANT PATH-MAX

\ ---- buffers (fixed, allocated once - no dynamic sizing in v0) ----

\ Declared here rather than beside NORMALIZE-OPERATORS: TOKENIZE
\ reads the normalized line straight out of this buffer (Iteration
\ 111), and this file is one linear source where a word must be
\ defined before it is used.
1 CELLS ARGS-BUFFER: ARGV

\ ==================================================================
\ Newline
\ ==================================================================
\ This kernel's own CR emits CRLF (13 then 10), which is right for a
\ Forth console and wrong for a shell: every builtin that printed a
\ line - pwd, command -v, readonly -p, every diagnostic - emitted a
\ stray carriage return, so the output differed from every other
\ shell by one byte per line. Found by diffing command.sh against
\ bash, where the ONLY remaining difference was "\r".
\
\ Redefined here rather than spelled "10 EMIT" at each site: every
\ later definition in this file then gets it automatically, and
\ nothing outside shell.4 is affected.
: CR ( --- )  10 EMIT ;

: ARGV@ ( i --- c-addr )  CELLS ARGV + @ ;
PATH-MAX BUFFER: PATHBUF
PATH-MAX BUFFER: CWDBUF
64 BUFFER: ENVNAMBUF

VARIABLE ARGC          ( number of tokens in the current command line )
VARIABLE SHELL-PID   \ $$ - set once, in MAIN
VARIABLE JOB#           \ how many are recorded
VARIABLE JOB-CONTROL?   \ process groups and the terminal are in play
VARIABLE SHELL-PGID     \ the shell's own group, to take the terminal back
VARIABLE BG-PID   \ pid of the most recent background command, for $!
VARIABLE LAST-STATUS   ( exit status of the last foreground command, decoded to a plain 0-255 code regardless of whether it came from a builtin or WAITPID )

\ !XT / @XT (storing an execution token as a START-relative offset,
\ so it survives being saved into an image) live in shadow.4, which
\ this file already requires for SHADOW{ itself.

DEFER TREE-EVAL-CALL  \ eval's text, parsed and run as a tree
VARIABLE SUPPRESS-EXEC?  \ true when an enclosing if's own then/else
                          \ branch has already determined this whole
                          \ region shouldn't execute anything (either
                          \ because its own condition didn't permit
                          \ this branch, or because an even further
                          \ enclosing if was itself already
                          \ suppressed). A suppressed region still
                          \ needs to be *read* line by line, so a
                          \ nested if/while inside it correctly
                          \ consumes its own then/body/fi rather than
                          \ having its "fi" mistaken for the enclosing
                          \ if's own - it just must not actually run
                          \ anything while doing so. Checked at every
                          \ point that would otherwise execute a
                          \ command (TRY-ASSIGNMENT, RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE)
                          \ rather than only gating whether
                          \ RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL is invoked at all, which
                          \ is what let a nested if inside a false
                          \ branch go unparsed and corrupt the
                          \ enclosing if's own fi/else detection -
                          \ found by testing directly, not by
                          \ inspection.
0 LAST-STATUS !        ( defensive: don't rely on a VARIABLE's initial content )

VARIABLE RETURN-PENDING?  \ true from the moment a "return" builtin
                           \ runs (see DISPATCH) until the innermost
                           \ still-executing function's own
                           \ RUN-FUNC-BODY notices and resets it.
                           \ Checked alongside SUPPRESS-EXEC? in
                           \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, so it short-
                           \ circuits any further command in the same
                           \ logical line regardless of ';'/'&&'/'||'
                           \ structure - all of those eventually run
                           \ their own piece through
                           \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE, the one place
                           \ this needs to be checked, the same reason
                           \ SUPPRESS-EXEC? is checked only there
                           \ (and TRY-ASSIGNMENT) rather than at every
                           \ splitting word individually.
VARIABLE FUNC-DEPTH        \ how many RUN-FUNC-BODY calls are
                           \ currently on the way down (0 at the top
                           \ level) - "return" outside any function is
                           \ diagnosed rather than silently ignored or
                           \ left to corrupt later execution.

VARIABLE LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING?  \ true from the moment "break" or
                           \ "continue" runs (see DISPATCH) until the
                           \ current loop iteration's own
                           \ DO-WHILE-BODY notices and resets it.
                           \ Checked in the same three places
                           \ RETURN-PENDING? is checked - the plain
                           \ RUN-SIMPLE-OR-PIPELINE guard, so nothing
                           \ further in the same logical line runs
                           \ either - PLUS both RUN-FUNC-BODY's and
                           \ DO-WHILE-BODY's own replay loops, since a
                           \ loop body can call a function, and that
                           \ function might be the one that actually
                           \ calls break/continue meant for the
                           \ *loop*, several call frames further out.
                           \ Unlike RETURN-PENDING?, this flag is only
                           \ ever reset by DO-WHILE-BODY, never by
                           \ RUN-FUNC-BODY - a function's own body
                           \ stops early when it sees this set (it has
                           \ to, the same as for a return), but must
                           \ leave the flag itself alone so it keeps
                           \ propagating outward through however many
                           \ nested function calls separate the
                           \ break/continue from the loop iteration
                           \ that actually needs to see it.
VARIABLE LOOP-LEVEL     \ how many loops a break or continue leaves
VARIABLE LOOP-BREAK?       \ true only if it was specifically "break"
                           \ (not "continue") that set
                           \ LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? - survives past
                           \ DO-WHILE-BODY's own reset of that flag
                           \ (which only marks the *iteration* as
                           \ over), so the outer while/for loop
                           \ (DO-WHILE/DO-FOR-ITERATE) can check it
                           \ right after each DO-WHILE-BODY call and
                           \ decide whether to stop iterating
                           \ entirely (break) or just proceed to the
                           \ next iteration as usual (continue, or a
                           \ body that simply ran to completion).
VARIABLE LOOP-DEPTH        \ how many while/for loops are currently
                           \ active (0 at the top level) - like
                           \ FUNC-DEPTH, lets break/continue outside
                           \ any loop be diagnosed. Never needs >R/R>
                           \ nesting the way FUNC-DEPTH's own
                           \ analogues do for recursion: a loop body
                           \ (or a function called from one) can't yet
                           \ contain another while/for construct at
                           \ all (see the existing multi-line-
                           \ construct limitation), so two loops can
                           \ never be simultaneously in progress.

0 RETURN-PENDING? !
0 FUNC-DEPTH !
0 LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? !
0 LOOP-BREAK? !
0 LOOP-DEPTH !

\ ==================================================================
\ C-string helpers
\ EXECVE/GETENV/SETENV/CHDIR all speak NUL-terminated C strings, not
\ Forth's own length-prefixed convention - these bridge the two.
\ ==================================================================

\ CSTRLEN is a kernel primitive since Iteration 260 (strlen); this
\ was a threaded loop, and the second-hottest word in the shell.

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ Writing to stderr.
\
\ Forth's `."` and TYPE go to stdout, which is wrong for every
\ diagnostic a shell produces: POSIX puts them on fd 2, and a script
\ that redirects stdout must still see its errors. Defined this early
\ because RUN-CHILD needs them, ~2400 lines above where the
\ interactive prompt (the other fd 2 writer) lives.
\
\ Note that shell.4's OLDER diagnostics - "cd: no such directory",
\ "shell: syntax error: ...", "alias: too many aliases" and the rest -
\ still use `."` and so still go to stdout. Moving them is a separate
\ change with its own test churn, recorded in PROGRESS.md rather than
\ folded in here.
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
CREATE SH-NL 10 C,   \ a single newline byte. An empty S" " does not
                     \ compile in this kernel - the definition fails
                     \ to complete and the word comes back undefined -
                     \ so a byte constant is how to write exactly one
                     \ newline and nothing else.

: ERR-TYPE ( c-addr u --- )   2 WRITE-FILE DROP ;
: ERR-CSTR ( c-addr --- )     DUP CSTRLEN ERR-TYPE ;

: CSTR," ( "text<quote>" --- )
  \ A NUL-terminated string laid down here, for the names and fixed
  \ words this shell keeps: `CREATE PATH-NM CSTR," PATH"` where it used
  \ to be four `C,`s and a hand-written 0 (Iteration 353). `S" "` cannot
  \ serve, since it yields an address and a length where every caller of
  \ these wants a C string.
  [CHAR] " PARSE                             ( c-addr u )
  DUP >R  HERE SWAP MOVE
  R> ALLOT  0 C, ;
: ERR-NL   ( --- )            SH-NL 1 ERR-TYPE ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Reading a line of any length
\ ==================================================================
\ Lines are read into GROWING buffers (pool.4's BUF-ENSURE): a reader
\ is given a buffer's descriptor and an offset rather than an address,
\ because the buffer may move while it reads - and the old block stays
\ readable until the top-level loop frees it, since code holds
\ addresses into these buffers. LINE-MAX is only the size a buffer
\ starts at (Iteration 249).
\
\ Until then every line had to fit 256 bytes. A longer one was cut and
\ its tail read as the next line, so `echo <250 characters>Z echo
\ INJECTED` ran a command nobody wrote (Iteration 154's finding, made
\ safe in 248 and lifted here). What is left is a ceiling well past any
\ real script, LINE-HARD-MAX: a line longer than that is reported, read
\ to its end and discarded - status 2, nothing of it runs - so feeding
\ the shell a binary file costs a message, not the memory.

1048576 CONSTANT LINE-HARD-MAX

VARIABLE LONG-LINE?   \ was the last line read too long?

: LINE-TOO-LONG ( --- )
  S" shell: line too long" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  2 LAST-STATUS !  -1 LONG-LINE? ! ;

LINE-MAX BUFFER: DRAIN-BUF

: DRAIN-LINE ( fid --- )
\ Read and discard up to and including the next newline.
  BEGIN
    DRAIN-BUF LINE-MAX 2 PICK READ-LINE DROP   ( fid u flag )
    SWAP LINE-MAX = AND 0=
  UNTIL DROP ;

VARIABLE GL-OFF
VARIABLE GL-BODY
VARIABLE GL-FID
VARIABLE GL-N
VARIABLE GL-K
VARIABLE GL-ANY

: GL-ROOM ( --- c-addr k )
\ Room for at least 64 more characters after what has been read, plus
\ one a caller may use to terminate the line: where it is, and how many
\ characters to ask for.
  GL-OFF @ GL-N @ + 65 + GL-BODY @ BUF-ENSURE
  GL-BODY @ @ GL-OFF @ + GL-N @ +
  GL-BODY @ CELL+ @ GL-OFF @ - GL-N @ - 1- ;

: READ-LINE-INTO ( off body fid --- u flag )
\ Read one line from fid into the growing buffer described at body,
\ starting at offset off. u excludes the newline; flag is false only at
\ end of file. READ-LINE never reads past the newline, so when a read
\ fills the room it was given, the rest of the line is still waiting:
\ grow and read again. A read that fills it exactly is followed by one
\ that returns just the newline.
  SHADOW{ GL-OFF GL-BODY GL-FID | GL-N GL-K GL-ANY }
  0 LONG-LINE? !
  BEGIN
    GL-ROOM DUP GL-K ! GL-FID @ READ-LINE DROP   ( u flag )
    GL-ANY @ OR GL-ANY !
    DUP GL-N @ + GL-N !
    GL-K @ =                                     ( full? )
    GL-OFF @ GL-N @ + LINE-HARD-MAX > IF
      \ Drain only if the line is still going: a read that stopped short
      \ has already taken the newline, and draining then swallowed the
      \ NEXT line - and, as it happened, the rest of the script.
      IF GL-FID @ DRAIN-LINE THEN
      LINE-TOO-LONG 0 -1 EXIT
    THEN
  WHILE REPEAT
  GL-N @ GL-ANY @ ;

VARIABLE PARSE-N

: PARSE-DECIMAL ( c-addr u --- n )
  \ Parses a simple non-negative decimal integer from (c-addr u) - no
  \ sign handling, matching how exit codes are conventionally
  \ unsigned small integers. An explicit multiply/accumulate loop
  \ rather than the standard >NUMBER, to keep this self-contained and
  \ easy to verify directly, matching EMIT-DECIMAL's own reasoning
  \ elsewhere. u can be 0 (e.g. `exit ""`), guarded the same way as
  \ Iteration 15's crash-hardening fixes - this kernel's DO/LOOP
  \ doesn't treat start=limit as zero iterations. Moved here (from
  \ much later in the file originally) in Iteration 35, so arithmetic
  \ expansion's own numeric-lookup logic - defined much earlier than
  \ this word's original position - can call it directly.
  0 PARSE-N !
  DUP 0 > IF
    0 DO
      DUP I + C@ 48 -
      PARSE-N @ 10 * + PARSE-N !
    LOOP
    DROP
  ELSE
    2DROP
  THEN
  PARSE-N @ ;

\ ==================================================================
\ String builder: append bytes/strings into a buffer via a cursor.
\ B-RESET points the cursor at a buffer; B-CHAR/B-CSTR/B-STR append;
\ B-FINISH NUL-terminates. Used for building "dir/cmd" PATH candidates
\ without any stack-juggling - a plain, explicit cursor is much harder
\ to get wrong than shuffling multiple addresses/lengths on the stack
\ (this session's testing of the new primitives found repeated
\ stack-order mistakes doing exactly that kind of shuffling).
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE BUILD-P
VARIABLE B-SRC

: B-RESET ( addr --- )  BUILD-P ! ;
: B-CHAR ( c --- )  BUILD-P @ C! BUILD-P @ 1+ BUILD-P ! ;
: B-CSTR ( c-addr --- )
  BEGIN DUP C@ DUP WHILE B-CHAR CHAR+ REPEAT 2DROP ;
: B-STR ( c-addr u --- )
  \ u can be 0 (e.g. TRY-DIR called for an empty PATH component,
  \ which POSIX treats as meaning "."), guarded for the same reason
  \ as HAS-SLASH? above.
  SWAP B-SRC !
  DUP 0 > IF
    0 DO B-SRC @ I + C@ B-CHAR LOOP
  ELSE
    DROP
  THEN ;
: B-FINISH ( --- )  0 B-CHAR ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Forth-string equality (used for matching builtin names)
\ ==================================================================

: STR= ( c-addr1 u1 c-addr2 u2 --- f )
\ The kernel's COMPARE, which is memcmp (Iteration 260). This was a
\ byte loop in a locals frame, and the hottest word in the shell.
  COMPARE 0= ;

\ Compare a Forth string against a NUL-terminated C string (used for
\ command-name matching, since ARGV entries are C strings post-tokenize).
: STR0= ( c-addr1 u1 c-addr2 --- f )
  DUP CSTRLEN STR= ;

: CSTR= ( c-addr1 c-addr2 --- f )  \ compares two NUL-terminated C
                                     \ strings (STR0= compares a
                                     \ length-prefixed one against a
                                     \ NUL-terminated one - this is for
                                     \ two NUL-terminated ones, e.g.
                                     \ shell-variable names below).
  SWAP DUP CSTRLEN ROT STR0= ;


: SAFE-COPY-NUL ( src-addr u dest-addr dest-max --- ok? )
  \ On the stack rather than through SHADOW{ }: four saves and four
  \ restores cost more than the copy itself in a word this hot - 1.8%
  \ of a realistic script's dispatches (Iteration 365).
  >R OVER 1+ R> > IF 2DROP DROP 0 EXIT THEN   ( src u dest )
  DUP >R  OVER >R                             \ the return stack holds dest then u
  SWAP MOVE
  R> R> + 0 SWAP C!
  -1 ;

VARIABLE SCC-SRC
VARIABLE SCC-DEST
VARIABLE SCC-MAX

: SAFE-CSTR-COPY ( src-addr dest-addr dest-max --- ok? )
  \ Same as SAFE-COPY-NUL, but src-addr is already a NUL-terminated
  \ C string (its own length is computed here) rather than a
  \ separately-given (addr u) pair.
  SHADOW{ SCC-SRC SCC-DEST SCC-MAX }
  SCC-SRC @ SCC-SRC @ CSTRLEN SCC-DEST @ SCC-MAX @ SAFE-COPY-NUL ;

\ A variable's value is still limited to SHVAR-VALUE-MAX - 1 characters.
\ Until Iteration 249 an over-long one was silently not copied, so the
\ destination kept whatever it held: `a=first; b=<300 characters>` set
\ b to "first", and `read` did the same. Now it is reported, the value
\ is EMPTY, and the command's status is 2. VALUE-FAILED? tells the
\ callers that would otherwise overwrite that status.
VARIABLE VALUE-FAILED?

: VALUE-TOO-LONG ( --- )
  S" shell: value too long" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  2 LAST-STATUS !  -1 VALUE-FAILED? ! ;

: VALUE-COPY ( src u dest max --- )
\ SAFE-COPY-NUL for a value: never leaves a stale one behind.
  OVER >R SAFE-COPY-NUL R> SWAP IF DROP ELSE 0 SWAP C! VALUE-TOO-LONG THEN ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Shell-local variables: POSIX "shell parameters" distinct from the
\ OS environment - a standalone "NAME=value" line (see ASSIGNMENT-
\ EQPOS/TRY-ASSIGNMENT further down) sets one of these without exporting
\ it, so it expands via $NAME/${NAME} (see LOOKUP-VAR, used by
\ EXPAND-VAR below) but a child process never sees it - unlike
\ `export NAME=value`, which sets both this table and calls SETENV
\ (see DO-EXPORT). A simple linear table (name/value pairs copied
\ into fixed-size buffers, not just pointers - ARGV entries point
\ into LINE-BUF, which gets overwritten on the very next line) -
\ matches how this shell already avoids hashing/dynamic structures
\ everywhere else (ARGV, the PATH search, etc).
\ ==================================================================

\ Since Iteration 252 the table and its values grow. It held 32
\ variables, and every value was a 256-byte slot: the 33rd variable was
\ refused, and a longer value was dropped (and, until 249, left the
\ PREVIOUS value in place). Now the table doubles as it fills - names,
\ value pointers, capacities and readonly flags together - and each
\ value is a heap block of its own. A value that fits its block is
\ copied in place, as the slots were; a larger one gets a new block of
\ at least twice the size, and the old block is RETIRED rather than
\ freed (pool.4), which only growth does, so a loop assigning a million
\ times keeps no more than a few. `unset` frees its block.
32  CONSTANT MAX-SHVARS          \ the table's starting size
64  CONSTANT SHVAR-NAME-MAX
256 CONSTANT SHVAR-VALUE-MAX     \ the staging buffers' starting size
65536 CONSTANT SHVARS-HARD-MAX
VARIABLE SHVAR-CAP   MAX-SHVARS SHVAR-CAP !
MAX-SHVARS SHVAR-NAME-MAX * BUFFER: SHVAR-NAMES
MAX-SHVARS CELLS BUFFER: SHVAR-VALS    \ each value's block; 0 before it is set
MAX-SHVARS CELLS BUFFER: SHVAR-VCAPS   \ that block's size
MAX-SHVARS BUFFER: SHVAR-RO   \ readonly flag per slot
MAX-SHVARS CELLS BUFFER: SHVAR-HASHES   \ each name's hash (Iteration 272)
VARIABLE SHVAR-COUNT
0 SHVAR-COUNT !
CREATE EMPTY-CSTR CSTR," "
: SHVAR-NAME-SLOT ( i --- addr )   SHVAR-NAME-MAX * SHVAR-NAMES + ;
: SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT ( i --- addr )
  CELLS SHVAR-VALS + @ ?DUP 0= IF EMPTY-CSTR THEN ;

\ ---- the index (Iteration 272) ----
\ FIND-SHVAR searched every variable, comparing names: 25 microseconds a
\ lookup with 500 variables, and every $HOME or $PATH - which live in the
\ environment, not the table - paid the whole scan before getenv. dash
\ hashes (DASH-COMPARISON.md). SHVAR-INDEX is open addressing over the
\ table: each slot holds a variable's number plus one, or 0; it is at
\ least twice the table's capacity, so never more than half full. An
\ added variable is inserted; a removal (which moves the last entry) or
\ a growing table marks the index stale, and the next lookup rebuilds
\ it. SHVAR-HASHES keeps each name's hash, so most slots are rejected
\ without comparing names.
256 CONSTANT INDEX-START
INDEX-START CELLS BUFFER: SHVAR-INDEX
VARIABLE SHVAR-INDEX-SIZE   INDEX-START SHVAR-INDEX-SIZE !
VARIABLE SHVAR-INDEX-OK?    0 SHVAR-INDEX-OK? !

: NAME-HASH ( c-addr --- h )
  \ FNV-1a with a final mix. The first version, h*31+c, gave var_0 ..
  \ var_499 nearly consecutive hashes, and linear probing turned them
  \ into one long run that most lookups walked.
  2166136261 SWAP
  BEGIN DUP C@ ?DUP WHILE ROT XOR 16777619 * SWAP 1+ REPEAT DROP
  DUP 16 RSHIFT XOR  73244475 *  DUP 16 RSHIFT XOR ;
: IDX-MASK ( --- m )   SHVAR-INDEX-SIZE @ 1- ;
: IDX-SLOT ( k --- addr )   CELLS SHVAR-INDEX + ;
: INDEX-INSERT ( i --- )
  DUP CELLS SHVAR-HASHES + @ IDX-MASK AND
  BEGIN DUP IDX-SLOT @ WHILE 1+ IDX-MASK AND REPEAT
  IDX-SLOT SWAP 1+ SWAP ! ;
: INDEX-REBUILD ( --- )
  INDEX-START BEGIN DUP SHVAR-CAP @ 2* < WHILE 2* REPEAT
  DUP CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER SHVAR-INDEX
  DUP SHVAR-INDEX-SIZE !
  SHVAR-INDEX SWAP CELLS 0 FILL
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 0 ?DO I INDEX-INSERT LOOP
  -1 SHVAR-INDEX-OK? ! ;

: GROW-SHVARS ( --- f )
\ Room for one more variable? Doubles the table if it can.
  SHVAR-COUNT @ SHVAR-CAP @ < IF -1 EXIT THEN
  SHVAR-CAP @ SHVARS-HARD-MAX < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  SHVAR-CAP @ 2*
  DUP SHVAR-NAME-MAX * ENSURE-BUFFER SHVAR-NAMES
  DUP CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER SHVAR-VALS
  DUP CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER SHVAR-VCAPS
  DUP ENSURE-BUFFER SHVAR-RO
  DUP CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER SHVAR-HASHES
  SHVAR-CAP !  0 SHVAR-INDEX-OK? !  -1 ;

VARIABLE SVV-SRC
VARIABLE SVV-I
VARIABLE SVV-LEN
VARIABLE SVV-NEW

: SET-SHVAR-VALUE ( value-addr i --- )
\ Store a NUL-terminated value in variable i. The new block is filled
\ before the old one is retired, so the value may come from the old one.
  SHADOW{ SVV-SRC SVV-I | SVV-LEN SVV-NEW }
  SVV-SRC @ CSTRLEN SVV-LEN !
  SVV-LEN @ 1+ SVV-I @ CELLS SHVAR-VCAPS + @ > IF
    SVV-LEN @ 1+ 32 MAX  SVV-I @ CELLS SHVAR-VCAPS + @ 2* MAX
    DUP ALLOCATE ABORT" shell: out of memory" SVV-NEW !
    SVV-I @ CELLS SHVAR-VCAPS + !
    SVV-SRC @ SVV-NEW @ SVV-LEN @ 1+ MOVE
    SVV-I @ CELLS SHVAR-VALS + @ ?DUP IF BUF-RETIRE THEN
    SVV-NEW @ SVV-I @ CELLS SHVAR-VALS + !
  ELSE
    SVV-SRC @ SVV-I @ CELLS SHVAR-VALS + @ SVV-LEN @ 1+ MOVE
  THEN ;


VARIABLE FSS-I
: FIND-SHVAR-SCAN ( name-addr --- i | -1 )   \ a small table: every entry
  \ A variable, not DO and I - I is a colon word, and this is hot.
  0 FSS-I !
  BEGIN FSS-I @ SHVAR-COUNT @ < WHILE
    FSS-I @ SHVAR-NAME-SLOT                  ( name slot )
    OVER C@ OVER C@ = IF
      OVER CSTR= IF FSS-I @ NIP EXIT THEN
    ELSE DROP THEN
    FSS-I @ 1+ FSS-I !
  REPEAT DROP -1 ;

: FIND-SHVAR ( name-addr --- i | -1 )   \ through the index
  \ Up to eight variables a scan is cheaper than hashing the name - the
  \ loop benchmark ran 9% slower through the index alone.
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 8 > 0= IF FIND-SHVAR-SCAN EXIT THEN
  SHVAR-INDEX-OK? @ 0= IF INDEX-REBUILD THEN
  DUP NAME-HASH                             ( name h )
  DUP IDX-MASK AND                          ( name h k )
  BEGIN DUP IDX-SLOT @ ?DUP WHILE           ( name h k i+1 )
    1- DUP CELLS SHVAR-HASHES + @ 3 PICK = IF
      DUP SHVAR-NAME-SLOT 4 PICK CSTR= IF NIP NIP NIP EXIT THEN
    THEN DROP
    1+ IDX-MASK AND
  REPEAT
  DROP 2DROP -1 ;

: GET-SHVAR ( name-addr --- value-addr | 0 )
  FIND-SHVAR
  DUP -1 = IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT ;

VARIABLE SV-NAME
VARIABLE SV-VALUE
VARIABLE SV-IDX

VARIABLE INTERACTIVE?   \ standard input is a terminal: prompts, no exit on errors

\ XCU 2.8.1: an error in a SPECIAL builtin ends a non-interactive shell.
\ This shell reported and carried on, as bash does outside its POSIX
\ mode, and GOALS.md 5k left the choice open since Iteration 326 because
\ it changes the behaviour of every script. yash's POSIX suite settles
\ it: 199 of its cases in one file test this rule, dash and mrsh both
\ follow it, and POSIX conformance is what this shell is for. Decided
\ and implemented in Iteration 376.
\
\ It is the ERROR that is fatal, not a non-zero status: `eval false` and
\ `. script-that-fails` report the command's status and are not errors
\ of the builtin, so only the usage and operand paths call this.
\ Deferred because SHELL-EXIT is defined further down.
DEFER SPECIAL-ERROR   ( status --- )

: SET-SHVAR ( name-addr value-addr --- )  \ both NUL-terminated C
                                            \ strings. Updates an
                                            \ existing slot if the
                                            \ name is already present,
                                            \ otherwise appends a new
                                            \ one - silently does
                                            \ nothing if the table is
                                            \ full, or if either
                                            \ string doesn't fit its
                                            \ slot (SAFE-CSTR-COPY's
                                            \ own bounds check),
                                            \ matching this shell's
                                            \ sparse error handling
                                            \ elsewhere. Captures both
                                            \ arguments into variables
                                            \ immediately, rather than
                                            \ carrying them through the
                                            \ FIND-SHVAR/SHVAR-COUNT
                                            \ stack logic below them -
                                            \ the first version of this
                                            \ word didn't, and ended up
                                            \ feeding SAFE-CSTR-COPY
                                            \ the wrong address (found
                                            \ by testing directly: the
                                            \ name and value slots came
                                            \ out swapped).
  SHADOW{ SV-NAME SV-VALUE | SV-IDX }
  \ A name that does not fit is refused before an entry is made: the
  \ copy below used to fail silently, and a new entry kept whatever
  \ name its slot last held (Iteration 252).
  SV-NAME @ CSTRLEN SHVAR-NAME-MAX < 0= IF
    S" shell: variable name too long" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL  2 LAST-STATUS !
    -1 VALUE-FAILED? !
    EXIT
  THEN
  SV-NAME @ FIND-SHVAR
  DUP -1 = IF
    DROP
    GROW-SHVARS 0= IF
      \ GOALS.md's memory policy: a full fixed table must never fail
      \ silently. This used to just EXIT, so the 33rd variable was
      \ never set and every later use of it expanded to empty -
      \ wrong output rather than an error (found in Iteration 44,
      \ diagnosed only now).
      ." shell: too many shell variables" CR
      EXIT
    THEN
    SHVAR-COUNT @ DUP 1+ SHVAR-COUNT !
    SV-NAME @ NAME-HASH OVER CELLS SHVAR-HASHES + !
    SHVAR-INDEX-OK? @ IF DUP INDEX-INSERT THEN
    DUP SHVAR-RO + 0 SWAP C!        \ new slots start writable
  THEN
  SV-IDX !
  \ A readonly variable refuses assignment, per POSIX. Checked here
  \ rather than in each caller, so every path that can set a variable
  \ (plain assignment, export, ${VAR:=word}, a for loop's own control
  \ variable) is covered by one test.
  SV-IDX @ SHVAR-RO + C@ IF
    \ A readonly variable cannot be assigned to: the message goes to
    \ standard error, as every other diagnostic does, and the status is
    \ 2 (Iteration 357). This wrote to standard OUTPUT and left the
    \ status at 0, so a script could not tell it had failed.
    S" shell: " ERR-TYPE SV-NAME @ ERR-CSTR
    S" : is read only" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    -1 VALUE-FAILED? !                       \ the status the caller reports
    2 SPECIAL-ERROR                          \ XCU 2.8.1 (Iteration 376)
    EXIT
  THEN
  SV-NAME @ SV-IDX @ SHVAR-NAME-SLOT SHVAR-NAME-MAX SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  SV-VALUE @ SV-IDX @ SET-SHVAR-VALUE
  \ A variable in the environment - inherited, or exported - is exported
  \ with its new value (XCU 2.5.3, 2.9.1): `PATH=/x` changed only the
  \ shell's copy, so commands kept being found along the old PATH
  \ (Iteration 269).
  SV-NAME @ GETENV IF SV-VALUE @ SV-NAME @ SETENV DROP THEN ;

: REMOVE-SHVAR ( name-addr --- )  \ shifts the last entry into the
                                    \ removed slot rather than
                                    \ shifting every following entry
                                    \ down - order doesn't matter for
                                    \ a lookup-only table. A no-op if
                                    \ the name isn't present.
  FIND-SHVAR
  DUP -1 = IF DROP EXIT THEN
  DUP CELLS SHVAR-VALS + @ ?DUP IF FREE DROP THEN
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 1- 2DUP <> IF              ( i last )
    \ The last entry moves into the hole - its readonly flag too,
    \ which until Iteration 252 stayed behind.
    DUP SHVAR-NAME-SLOT 2 PICK SHVAR-NAME-SLOT SHVAR-NAME-MAX MOVE
    DUP CELLS SHVAR-VALS + @   2 PICK CELLS SHVAR-VALS + !
    DUP CELLS SHVAR-VCAPS + @  2 PICK CELLS SHVAR-VCAPS + !
    DUP SHVAR-RO + C@          2 PICK SHVAR-RO + C!
    DUP CELLS SHVAR-HASHES + @ 2 PICK CELLS SHVAR-HASHES + !
  THEN
  0 SHVAR-INDEX-OK? !
  \ The last slot is free now: no block, so the next variable to take
  \ it allocates its own instead of freeing one it does not own.
  0 OVER CELLS SHVAR-VALS + !  0 OVER CELLS SHVAR-VCAPS + !
  0 OVER SHVAR-RO + C!
  SHVAR-COUNT !  DROP ;

DEFER POS-DIGIT-CALL   \ patched once POS-PARAM-SLOT exists, below

: LOOKUP-VAR ( name-addr --- value-addr | 0 )  \ shell-local storage
                                                 \ first (covers every
                                                 \ assigned variable,
                                                 \ exported or not),
                                                 \ falling back to
                                                 \ GETENV (covers a
                                                 \ variable this shell
                                                 \ never itself
                                                 \ assigned but
                                                 \ inherited from its
                                                 \ own environment,
                                                 \ e.g. $HOME).
  \ A single digit names a positional parameter, not a shell variable.
  \ Handled here rather than at each call site so every braced form
  \ gets it at once - ${2}, ${3:+x}, ${#2}, ${2#pat} - where before
  \ only the bare $2 spelling worked, because EXPAND-VAR special-cased
  \ digits and EXPAND-BRACED-VAR went straight to this word.
  DUP POS-DIGIT-CALL ?DUP IF SWAP DROP EXIT THEN
  DUP GET-SHVAR DUP 0= IF DROP GETENV ELSE SWAP DROP THEN ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Positional parameters ($1-$9, $#, $@, $*) - a script's own
\ command-line arguments at the top level, or a function's own call
\ arguments within its body (see SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-ARGV/
\ SAVE-POS-PARAMS/RESTORE-POS-PARAMS, used by RUN-FUNC-BODY). Scope
\ limit: single-digit access only ($1-$9, not ${10} and beyond) -
\ POS-PARAM-COUNT is itself clamped to MAX-POS-PARAMS, so $# won't
\ report a true count past 9 either; a real, if less common, gap this
\ project may want to close later given the payoff (${10}+ needs
\ EXPAND-VAR's own ${NAME} braced path to accept a decimal name, not
\ just POSIX portable name characters).
\ ==================================================================

\ Storage (Iteration 250). The parameters are one heap block: a cell
\ per parameter pointing at its string, then the strings. Until then
\ they were nine 256-byte slots, and a tenth parameter was dropped
\ without a word - `set -- 1 ... 20` gave $# = 9, and "$@" and every
\ function's arguments were cut the same way. Any number now, each any
\ length.
\
\ A function call SAVES the caller's block and builds its own; the
\ return FREES it and restores the caller's. Freed, not retired as the
\ growing buffers are: a loop calling a function a million times would
\ otherwise hold a million blocks until it ended. That is safe because
\ nothing keeps a pointer into a parameter past the command that read
\ it - expansions copy, getopts reloads its pointer on every call, and
\ shift moves the pointer cells, not the strings.
32 CONSTANT MAX-POS-PARAM-DEPTH
VARIABLE POS-BLOCK   0 POS-BLOCK !      \ 0 when there are none
VARIABLE POS-PARAM-COUNT
MAX-POS-PARAM-DEPTH 2* CELLS BUFFER: POS-SAVE   \ block, count per depth

: POS-PARAM-SLOT ( i --- addr )  \ i is 0-based: $1 is slot 0
  CELLS POS-BLOCK @ + @ ;

VARIABLE POS-FROM-SYS?   \ POS-BUILD's source: relf's argv, or ARGV
VARIABLE POS-SYS-SKIP    \ how many of relf's own arguments to pass over
1 POS-SYS-SKIP !         \ ... the script name, unless `-s` says otherwise

: POS-SRC ( i --- c-addr )   \ the i-th new parameter, 0-based
  POS-FROM-SYS? @ IF POS-SYS-SKIP @ + SYS-ARG ELSE 1+ ARGV@ THEN ;

VARIABLE PB-N
VARIABLE PB-SIZE
VARIABLE PB-I
VARIABLE PB-AT

: POS-BUILD ( n --- )
\ Replace the parameters with n strings from POS-SRC. The new block is
\ built before the old one is freed.
  SHADOW{ PB-N | PB-SIZE PB-I PB-AT }
  PB-N @ CELLS PB-SIZE !
  BEGIN PB-I @ PB-N @ < WHILE
    PB-I @ POS-SRC CSTRLEN 1+ PB-SIZE @ + PB-SIZE !
    PB-I @ 1+ PB-I !
  REPEAT
  PB-SIZE @ 1 MAX ALLOCATE ABORT" shell: out of memory"   ( new )
  DUP PB-N @ CELLS + PB-AT !
  0 PB-I !
  BEGIN PB-I @ PB-N @ < WHILE
    PB-AT @ OVER PB-I @ CELLS + !
    PB-I @ POS-SRC DUP CSTRLEN 1+                   ( new src u )
    DUP >R PB-AT @ SWAP MOVE R> PB-AT @ + PB-AT !
    PB-I @ 1+ PB-I !
  REPEAT
  POS-BLOCK @ ?DUP IF FREE DROP THEN
  POS-BLOCK !  PB-N @ POS-PARAM-COUNT ! ;

CREATE DEFAULT-SHELL-NAME  CSTR," sh"
VARIABLE SHELL-NAME   \ set by MAIN: an address stored at load time is
                      \ not relocated when the image is loaded elsewhere

: POS-DIGIT-LOOKUP ( name-addr --- value-addr | 0 )
  \ A decimal name from 1 up names a positional parameter; "0" is the
  \ script name, not ours. Any number of digits: ${10} reaches here as
  \ "10" once the braced path accepts it. An unset one expands to empty
  \ like an unset variable.
  DUP C@ 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  0 SWAP
  BEGIN DUP C@ WHILE
    DUP C@ 48 - DUP 9 U> IF 2DROP DROP 0 EXIT THEN   ( n p d )
    ROT 10 * + SWAP 1+
  REPEAT DROP                                       ( n )
  \ $0: the script's name, or the shell's own (XCU 2.5.1). It gave
  \ nothing at all until Iteration 295.
  DUP 0= IF DROP SHELL-NAME @ EXIT THEN
  DUP 1 < IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  1-
  DUP POS-PARAM-COUNT @ < 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  POS-PARAM-SLOT ;
' POS-DIGIT-LOOKUP IS POS-DIGIT-CALL

: SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-ARGV ( --- )
  \ The parameters become ARGV[1..ARGC-1] - ARGV[0] is the function or
  \ builtin name. Called by RUN-FUNC-BODY as a call begins, while ARGV
  \ still holds the calling line, and by `set`.
  0 POS-FROM-SYS? !  ARGC @ 1- 0 MAX POS-BUILD ;

: SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-SYS-ARGS-AT ( skip --- )
  \ The parameters are relf's own arguments after the first `skip` of
  \ them: one for `relfsh script.sh arg1 arg2`, more for `-s` and for
  \ `-c command name args` (Iteration 375).
  DUP POS-SYS-SKIP !
  -1 POS-FROM-SYS? !  SYS-ARGC SWAP - 0 MAX POS-BUILD ;


: SAVE-POS-PARAMS ( depth --- )
  \ The caller's block moves onto the save stack; the parameters are
  \ empty until SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-ARGV builds the function's own.
  2* CELLS POS-SAVE +
  POS-BLOCK @ OVER !  POS-PARAM-COUNT @ SWAP CELL+ !
  0 POS-BLOCK !  0 POS-PARAM-COUNT ! ;
: RESTORE-POS-PARAMS ( depth --- )
  POS-BLOCK @ ?DUP IF FREE DROP THEN
  2* CELLS POS-SAVE +
  DUP @ POS-BLOCK !  CELL+ @ POS-PARAM-COUNT ! ;
\ ==================================================================
\ Tokenizer: splits LINE-BUF (u chars from ACCEPT) on whitespace, in
\ place. Supports single quotes ('...', fully literal, no escapes
\ recognized inside), double quotes ("...", recognizing \" and \\ as
\ escapes for a literal " or \ - no $/`` handling, since there's no
\ expansion to apply them to), and backslash-escaping a single
\ character outside any quotes. Adjacent quoted/unquoted/escaped
\ spans concatenate into one token, matching real shell behavior
\ (e.g. 'ab'c"d e"f is one token, "abcd ef"). Quote/escape characters
\ themselves are stripped; what's left is compacted in place, which
\ is always safe here since compaction only ever shrinks a token
\ (removes bytes), never grows it. Each token's start address is
\ recorded into ARGV (NUL-terminated per EXECVE's own convention);
\ whether *any* part of a token came from inside quotes or an escape
\ is recorded in the parallel ARGV-QUOTED array, so a token like '<'
\ or "cd" that happens to match an operator or builtin name isn't
\ mistaken for one downstream (see PARSE-REDIRECTIONS,
\ DISPATCH) - it stays a literal argument, matching real shells.
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE TOK-POS
VARIABLE TOK-END
VARIABLE TOK-OUT

4096 CONSTANT TOK-BUF-MAX
TOK-BUF-MAX BUFFER: TOK-BUF
TOK-BUF-MAX BUFFER: EXP-BUF
VARIABLE TOK-OUT-END
VARIABLE OUT-BODY     \ descriptor of the buffer TOK-OUT writes into

\ The words a line expands to are written into TOK-BUF or EXP-BUF, and
\ since Iteration 251 that buffer grows when it fills. Growing MOVES it,
\ and three kinds of pointer into it are live while it is written: the
\ cursor TOK-OUT and its limit, and ARGV's entries for the words written
\ so far - including the one being built. GROW-OUT moves all of them.
\ Anything else that has to remember a place in the output keeps an
\ OFFSET from OUT-BASE (see SKIP-BRACED-WORD and CAPTURE-BRACED-WORD).
\ Until then output past the buffer was dropped without a word: a
\ command substitution was cut at 256 bytes, an expansion at 4 KB.
16777216 CONSTANT EXPAND-HARD-MAX
VARIABLE GRO-OLD
VARIABLE GRO-SIZE
VARIABLE GRO-DELTA
VARIABLE EXP-OVERFLOW?   \ reported once per line

: OUT-BASE ( --- a )  OUT-BODY @ @ ;

: GROW-OUT ( --- f )
  OUT-BODY @ CELL+ @ DUP GRO-SIZE !  EXPAND-HARD-MAX < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  OUT-BASE GRO-OLD !
  GRO-SIZE @ 1+ OUT-BODY @ BUF-ENSURE
  OUT-BASE GRO-OLD @ - GRO-DELTA !
  TOK-OUT @ GRO-DELTA @ + TOK-OUT !
  ARGC @ 1+ 0 DO
    ARGV I CELLS + @
    DUP GRO-OLD @ U< 0= OVER GRO-OLD @ GRO-SIZE @ + U< AND IF
      GRO-DELTA @ + ARGV I CELLS + !
    ELSE DROP THEN
  LOOP
  OUT-BASE OUT-BODY @ CELL+ @ + TOK-OUT-END !
  -1 ;

: EXPANSION-TOO-LARGE ( --- )
  EXP-OVERFLOW? @ 0= IF
    S" shell: expansion too large" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL  2 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN
  -1 EXP-OVERFLOW? ! ;

\ TOK-BUF holds the line's words as they were written; EXP-BUF holds
\ them after expansion. Splitting the two is what lets expansion
\ happen at a different time from tokenizing (Iteration 113).
\ Expanded words are written HERE, not back over the line they came
\ from. Until Iteration 108 the tokenizer compacted in place: TOK-OUT
\ trailed TOK-POS through LINE-BUF, which worked for quote-stripping
\ (output is always shorter) and not for expansion (a value can be
\ longer than the "$name" it replaces). ENSURE-ROOM existed to shift
\ the unread input rightward and keep the write cursor behind the read
\ cursor, and four separate bugs were one call to it being missing -
\ $VAR (26), the numeric expansions (46), "$*"'s separator (99) and
\ "$@"'s field break (103).
\
\ With a separate output buffer the hazard does not exist: there is no
\ shared buffer to overrun. That is item 2 of attic/docs/PARSE-EXPAND-PLAN.md,
\ and the first part of its Stage 1 - the input line now survives
\ tokenizing intact, which is what re-expanding it per command will
\ need.
VARIABLE TOK-WAS-QUOTED?
1 ARGS-BUFFER: ARGV-QUOTED   ( one byte flag per ARGV entry )
1 ARGS-BUFFER: ARGV-LITERAL   \ the word has nothing to expand (tree.4 knows; Iteration 266)
1 ARGS-BUFFER: ARGV-NAME-QUOTED  \ true if THIS token's very
                                          \ first character came from
                                          \ inside a quote - used only
                                          \ by TRY-ASSIGNMENT, to
                                          \ reject a fully-quoted
                                          \ 'NAME=value' (the name
                                          \ itself quoted, not a real
                                          \ assignment) while still
                                          \ accepting NAME="value"
                                          \ (only the value quoted, a
                                          \ perfectly valid one).
                                          \ Distinct from ARGV-QUOTED
                                          \ itself, which records "was
                                          \ ANY part of this token
                                          \ quoted" - using that one
                                          \ here would incorrectly
                                          \ reject NAME="value" too,
                                          \ since it can't distinguish
                                          \ which part was quoted.

: WS? ( c --- f )  DUP 32 = SWAP 9 = OR ;

: EMIT-TOK-CHAR ( c --- )
  \ A full buffer grows (GROW-OUT); only past EXPAND-HARD-MAX is output
  \ dropped, and then it is reported.
  \
  \ TOK-OUT-END rather than a fixed buffer: the same emit path fills
  \ TOK-BUF with raw words and EXP-BUF with expanded ones, and only
  \ whoever set TOK-OUT knows which (Iteration 113).
  TOK-OUT @ TOK-OUT-END @ < 0= IF
    GROW-OUT 0= IF DROP EXPANSION-TOO-LARGE EXIT THEN
  THEN
  TOK-OUT @ C!  TOK-OUT @ 1+ TOK-OUT ! ;

\ ==================================================================
\ $VAR expansion: $NAME, ${NAME}, $? (last exit status), $$ (this
\ shell's own PID). Recognized both outside quotes and inside double
\ quotes - not inside single quotes, which stay fully literal
\ (COPY-SINGLE-QUOTED never looks for $ at all, matching POSIX).
\ Deliberately narrow scope: no ${VAR:-default}-style modifiers, no
\ positional parameters ($1/$@/$#), no command substitution - just
\ environment-variable lookup (reusing GETENV, same as everywhere
\ else in this shell - there's no separate "shell variable" concept)
\ plus the two special forms. An expanded value is never re-split on
\ whitespace (unlike a real shell's unquoted expansion) - the whole
\ expansion becomes part of whichever token it's embedded in, and is
\ never re-interpreted as an operator/builtin name downstream, same
\ as quoted/escaped content (see ARGV-QUOTED).
\ ==================================================================

\ Laid down in the dictionary rather than a BUFFER:, since a buffer's
\ contents are not part of the saved image - the first version answered
\ 0 for every character (Iteration 365).
\ ... and filled by a word rather than an interpreted loop, which this
\ Forth's ?DO cannot run outside a definition.
CREATE NAME-CHAR-TABLE 256 ALLOT
: NAME-TABLE-FILL ( --- )
  256 0 ?DO
    I 95 =
    I 48 < 0= I 58 < AND OR
    I 65 < 0= I 91 < AND OR
    I 97 < 0= I 123 < AND OR
    1 AND I NAME-CHAR-TABLE + C!
  LOOP ;
NAME-TABLE-FILL

: NAME-CHAR? ( c --- f )  \ POSIX portable variable-name characters
  \ A table rather than four range tests: this is called once per
  \ character of every word the shell looks at, and was 5.0% of a
  \ realistic script's dispatches (Iteration 365).
  255 AND NAME-CHAR-TABLE + C@ 0<> ;


: EMIT-DECIMAL ( n --- )
  \ Appends n's decimal digits via EMIT-TOK-CHAR (used for $? and $$).
  \ Through the double-cell conversion, because NEGATE cannot make the
  \ most negative cell positive: `$((1<<31))` in the 4-byte build left
  \ the old divide-and-collect loop with zero digits, and the
  \ `DIGIT-N @ 0 DO ... LOOP` that followed then ran the whole cell
  \ range, writing backwards out of its buffer until it reached an
  \ unmapped page. An ARMv7 board found it; the 8-byte build has the
  \ same fault at $((1<<63)) and nothing had ever asked (Iteration 401).
  S>D TUCK DABS <# #S ROT SIGN #>           ( c-addr u )
  OVER + SWAP ?DO I C@ EMIT-TOK-CHAR LOOP ;

\ ==================================================================
\ IFS field splitting: an *unquoted* $VAR/${...}/$(...)/$((...))
\ expansion result is split into separate ARGV entries wherever a run
\ of IFS whitespace appears within it - unlike a literal character
\ copied straight from the input line, or one coming from inside
\ double quotes (never split, matching POSIX). Scope limit: only
\ space/tab are treated as IFS here - not yet a customizable $IFS
\ shell variable, and not newline either (an embedded newline in an
\ expansion result is left as an ordinary character for now). Whether
\ TOK-WAS-QUOTED? is currently set is exactly the signal already
\ available for "am I inside double quotes right now" (COPY-DOUBLE-
\ QUOTED sets it before calling EXPAND-VAR for a $VAR within "..."),
\ so no new quote-tracking is needed here at all.
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE IFS-RUN-START       \ where the current run of IFS whitespace began
VARIABLE AFTER-RUN-BREAK?    \ ... and a quoted break has already split it
VARIABLE QUOTE-IN-RUN?       \ quoting happened while whitespace was pending
VARIABLE QUOTE-BEFORE-RUN?   \ ... or before it, in this same field
VARIABLE IFS-SPLIT-PENDING?  \ true once an IFS character has been
                               \ seen but not yet resolved - the
                               \ actual split is deferred until the
                               \ next non-IFS character actually needs
                               \ writing, so a run of trailing IFS
                               \ whitespace (at the very end of the
                               \ expansion, or the token) never
                               \ produces a spurious empty trailing
                               \ field, and multiple consecutive IFS
                               \ characters collapse into a single
                               \ split rather than several empty ones.
VARIABLE WORD-IS-ASSIGN?   \ the parser called this word an assignment


: TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? ( --- f )
  \ The parser's tag, and nothing else. Until Iteration 294 a tagged word
  \ still had its output scanned for a NAME= prefix on every call - on
  \ every marked character and every split test - to confirm what the tag
  \ already said: 90 dispatches of every assignment that expands anything.
  WORD-IS-ASSIGN? @ ;

\ True once the token being built looks like "NAME=" - i.e. an
\ assignment word. POSIX does NOT field-split the value of an
\ assignment, so "q=$p" and "x=$(echo a b)" each stay a single word
\ however many blanks the expansion contains. Set by SCAN-TOKEN as it
\ scans, cleared at the start of every token.

VARIABLE IN-DQ-CONTEXT?      \ true while currently inside a real,
                               \ syntactic double-quoted span - set (and
                               \ later cleared) by COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED
                               \ itself around its own body. Distinct
                               \ from TOK-WAS-QUOTED?, which EXPAND-VAR
                               \ sets unconditionally for *every*
                               \ expansion it performs (marking the
                               \ result as "quoted" so it's never
                               \ re-interpreted as an operator or
                               \ keyword later, regardless of whether
                               \ the expansion is syntactically inside
                               \ real double quotes or bare/unquoted) -
                               \ reusing that flag here would make
                               \ EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR think *every*
                               \ expansion is quoted and never split
                               \ anything at all (found as a real bug
                               \ by testing directly: a bare, unquoted
                               \ $x came out completely empty).

4 BUFFER: IFS-NM
VARIABLE IFSC-C
VARIABLE IFSC-P

: IFS-FIRST ( --- c | -1 )
\ What "$*" joins with: IFS's first character, a space when IFS is
\ unset, nothing (-1) when it is empty (XCU 2.5.2).
  73 IFS-NM C!  70 IFS-NM 1+ C!  83 IFS-NM 2 + C!  0 IFS-NM 3 + C!
  IFS-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP 0= IF 32 EXIT THEN
  C@ ?DUP 0= IF -1 THEN ;

VARIABLE IFS-CACHE      \ IFS's value, or 0 when unset
VARIABLE IFS-CACHED?

: IFS-CHAR? ( c --- f )
  \ Is c a field separator? POSIX: the characters in $IFS, defaulting
  \ to space/tab/newline when IFS is unset. This used to be hardcoded
  \ to space and tab, so `IFS=':'` had no effect at all on splitting -
  \ the variable could be set and was simply never read.
  \
  \ An IFS that is set but *empty* correctly disables splitting
  \ entirely, since no character is a member of an empty set.
  IFSC-C !
  \ IFS is looked up once per expansion (Iteration 270; it was once per
  \ character). EXPAND-WORDS clears IFS-CACHED?.
  IFS-CACHED? @ 0= IF
    73 IFS-NM C!  70 IFS-NM 1+ C!  83 IFS-NM 2 + C!  0 IFS-NM 3 + C!
    IFS-NM LOOKUP-VAR IFS-CACHE !  -1 IFS-CACHED? !
  THEN
  IFS-CACHE @ IFSC-P !
  IFSC-P @ 0= IF
    IFSC-C @ 32 = IFSC-C @ 9 = OR IFSC-C @ 10 = OR EXIT
  THEN
  BEGIN IFSC-P @ C@ WHILE
    IFSC-P @ C@ IFSC-C @ = IF -1 EXIT THEN
    IFSC-P @ 1+ IFSC-P !
  REPEAT
  0 ;

: IFS-SPLIT-HERE ( --- )
  \ Finishes the current ARGV entry (NUL-terminating at TOK-OUT,
  \ recording it as unquoted, advancing ARGC) and starts a new one
  \ right after, if there's still room in ARGV.
  \ The NUL goes through EMIT-TOK-CHAR, which may grow the buffer; it
  \ used to be stored directly, one past a full buffer.
  0 EMIT-TOK-CHAR
  \ Inside double quotes this is "$@" separating parameters: the fields
  \ are quoted, and an empty one - `set -- a ""` - is kept. The new
  \ field's flags, which the end of the word reads to decide whether an
  \ empty last field is dropped, were left from whatever ran before
  \ (Iteration 270).
  IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ 0<> ARGV-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
  ARGC @ ROOM-FOR-ARG? IF
    ARGC @ 1+ ARGC !
    TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
    IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ 0<> DUP ARGV-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!  ARGV-NAME-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
  ELSE
    -1 ARGS-OVERFLOW? !
  THEN ;

VARIABLE PATTERN-CAPTURE?   \ a trim's pattern is being captured (EMIT-QUOTED)
VARIABLE QUOTE-DEPTH        \ inside real double quotes

\ Quoted characters in a trim's pattern are literal (XCU 2.6.2): while
\ one is captured, a quoted * ? [ or \ is written with a backslash before
\ it, and the trim matches with GM-ESCAPES? on. QUOTE-DEPTH counts real
\ double quotes, which CAPTURE-BRACED-WORD's forced IN-DQ-CONTEXT? hides.
\ (Iteration 273: `${x#*\*}` and `${z#\[ab\]}` treated them as patterns.)
: EMIT-QUOTED ( c --- )
  PATTERN-CAPTURE? @ IF
    DUP 42 = OVER 63 = OR OVER 91 = OR OVER 92 = OR IF 92 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
  THEN
  EMIT-TOK-CHAR ;

\ ---- shell options (Iteration 270): set -e -u -x -f -n ----
VARIABLE RD-SKIP?      \ this redirection was refused: do not open anything
VARIABLE OPT-NOCLOBBER \ set -C: `>` will not overwrite an existing regular file
VARIABLE OPT-ALLEXPORT \ set -a: every assignment is exported too
VARIABLE OPT-VERBOSE   \ set -v: each line of input is echoed as it is read
VARIABLE OPT-MONITOR   \ set -m: accepted and reported; jobs run as before
VARIABLE OPT-NOTIFY    \ set -b: the same - a finished job is reported at the
                       \ next prompt either way (Iteration 386)
VARIABLE OPT-ERREXIT
VARIABLE OPT-NOUNSET
VARIABLE OPT-XTRACE
VARIABLE OPT-NOGLOB
VARIABLE OPT-NOEXEC
VARIABLE UNSET-FATAL?      \ set -u met an unset parameter: the command fails

\ ---- pathname expansion: where the unquoted pattern characters are ----
\ An unquoted * ? or [ - written in the word, or produced by an unquoted
\ expansion - is remembered by its offset in the output. After the words
\ are expanded, a field holding one is a pattern (Iteration 267).
256 BUFFER: GLOB-MARKS
VARIABLE GLOB-MARKS#
VARIABLE GLOB-META#   \ of those, the ones that make the word a pattern
VARIABLE NO-GLOB?          \ the caller wants no pathname expansion
DEFER GLOB-FIELDS-CALL

: GLOB-MARK ( c --- c )
  \ A `-` and a `]` are marked as well: a quoted `-` inside a bracket is
  \ a literal rather than a range, and a quoted `]` is a member rather
  \ than the terminator - `[0"-"9]` matches three characters,
  \ not ten (Iteration 369). It does not count towards GLOB-META#,
  \ which is what decides whether the word goes to the file system at
  \ all: a word with a dash in it is not a pattern.
  DUP 45 = OVER 93 = OR IF
    TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? 0= IF
      GLOB-MARKS# @ 1+ CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER GLOB-MARKS
      TOK-OUT @ OUT-BASE -  GLOB-MARKS GLOB-MARKS# @ CELLS + !
      1 GLOB-MARKS# +!
    THEN
  THEN
  DUP 42 = OVER 63 = OR OVER 91 = OR IF
    \ Recorded even when pathname expansion is off, because a `case`
    \ pattern is still a pattern and these marks are how an unquoted
    \ metacharacter is told from a quoted one (Iteration 340).
    TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? 0= IF TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? 0= IF
      GLOB-MARKS# @ 1+ CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER GLOB-MARKS
      TOK-OUT @ OUT-BASE -  GLOB-MARKS GLOB-MARKS# @ CELLS + !
      1 GLOB-MARKS# +!  1 GLOB-META# +!
    THEN THEN
  THEN ;

: GLOB-MARK-DASH ( off --- )   \ an unquoted `-`: marked, but not a pattern
  TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? IF DROP EXIT THEN
  GLOB-MARKS# @ 1+ CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER GLOB-MARKS
  GLOB-MARKS GLOB-MARKS# @ CELLS + !
  1 GLOB-MARKS# +! ;

: GLOB-MARK-OFF ( off --- )   \ an unquoted pattern character already written
  TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? IF DROP EXIT THEN
  GLOB-MARKS# @ 1+ CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER GLOB-MARKS
  GLOB-MARKS GLOB-MARKS# @ CELLS + !
  1 GLOB-MARKS# +!  1 GLOB-META# +! ;

DEFER QUOTED-BETWEEN?   ( from to --- f )   \ tree.4: quoting in this span

: EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR ( c --- )
  \ Like EMIT-TOK-CHAR, but for a character coming from an unquoted
  \ expansion result, subject to IFS field splitting.
  IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ TOKEN-IS-ASSIGN-PREFIX? OR IF
    QUOTE-DEPTH @ IF EMIT-QUOTED ELSE EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP IFS-CHAR? IF
    \ XCU 2.6.5: IFS whitespace ends a field that has something in it;
    \ any other IFS character ends exactly one field, even an empty one
    \ - `a::b` with IFS=: is a, "" and b - and whitespace next to it is
    \ part of the same delimiter. A trailing delimiter leaves an empty
    \ last field, which the end of the word drops (Iteration 270; every
    \ IFS character was treated as whitespace).
    DUP 32 = OVER 9 = OR SWAP 10 = OR IF
      IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? @ IF
        \ Quoting inside a run of whitespace breaks the run in two, with
        \ an empty field between: `${x:+b '' c}` is three fields
        \ (Iteration 364).
        \ ... and the quoting has to come AFTER the whitespace started,
        \ which an offset cannot tell since pending whitespace is not
        \ emitted: `${x:+'' }` is one field and `${x:+b ''}` is two.
        \ ... and only when there is a field to close, as at the word's
        \ end: `${x:+ '' }` is one empty field, not two.
        QUOTE-IN-RUN? @
        TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ <> QUOTE-BEFORE-RUN? @ OR AND IF
          IFS-SPLIT-HERE
          0 QUOTE-IN-RUN? !
          TOK-OUT @ OUT-BASE - IFS-RUN-START !
          \ the whitespace after the break is still a delimiter, so the
          \ next content starts a field of its own even though this one
          \ is empty
          -1 AFTER-RUN-BREAK? !
        THEN
      ELSE
        -1 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !
        0 QUOTE-IN-RUN? !
        TOK-OUT @ OUT-BASE - IFS-RUN-START !
      THEN
    ELSE
      IFS-SPLIT-HERE
      0 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !
    THEN
  ELSE
    IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? @ IF
      0 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !
      \ Only actually split if the current entry has received some
      \ content already (TOK-OUT has moved past where it started) -
      \ otherwise this is leading IFS whitespace at the very start of
      \ the token, which should be absorbed rather than producing a
      \ spurious empty leading field.
      \ ... or if quoting contributed to this field even though it left
      \ no characters. An alternate value is emitted character by
      \ character rather than as a region, so `${x:+'' ''}` reaches
      \ this path and not the region splitter (Iteration 363).
      TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ <>
      ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ OUT-BASE -  TOK-OUT @ OUT-BASE -
      QUOTED-BETWEEN? OR  AFTER-RUN-BREAK? @ OR IF
        IFS-SPLIT-HERE
      THEN
      0 AFTER-RUN-BREAK? !
    THEN
    GLOB-MARK EMIT-TOK-CHAR
  THEN ;

: TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT ( c-addr --- )  \ copies a NUL-terminated string
                                       \ into the token being built,
                                       \ subject to IFS field splitting:
                                       \ an unquoted $VAR, ${...}, $(...)
                                       \ or $((...)) result
  BEGIN DUP C@ DUP WHILE EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR CHAR+ REPEAT 2DROP ;

\ ==================================================================
\ $(command) - command substitution
\ Runs the enclosed text as a single external command (no builtins,
\ same v0.7 scope limit as pipe segments not supporting builtins
\ either), captures its stdout via a pipe, strips trailing newlines
\ (matching POSIX), and splices the captured bytes into the token
\ being built - literally, with no further expansion or quote
\ processing applied to the captured output itself.
\
\ This runs *during* the outer line's own tokenization (SCAN-TOKEN or
\ COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED call EXPAND-VAR, which calls this, while LINE-BUF/
\ ARGV/ARGC/TOK-POS/TOK-END/TOK-OUT are all still mid-scan for the
\ *outer* line) - reusing TOKENIZE/ARGV/LINE-BUF for the *inner*
\ command's own parsing would corrupt that in-progress outer state.
\ So this copies the inner text into its own buffer and runs it in the
\ forked child through the REAL tokenizer, as replayed input lines -
\ the same machinery a loop body uses. The child has its own copy of
\ LINE-BUF/ARGV/TOK-*, so there is nothing left to corrupt.
\
\ That replaced CMDSUB-TOKENIZE, a private whitespace-only tokenizer
\ that had been extended three times (Iterations 71, 73, 104) and
\ still could not do pipes, redirection, ';', or several commands.
\ Ramey's regret about bash's own parse_comsub - that it "knows an
\ uncomfortable amount of shell syntax and duplicates rather more of
\ the token-reading code than is optimal" - is recorded in GOALS.md
\ against this exact word, with the instruction to replace rather
\ than extend it. (Iteration 105.)
\
\ RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD needs the replay machinery and RUN-CHILD, defined much later
\ in this file (in the pipe/path-search sections) - broken via the
\ same deferred-word pattern already used for RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL: a
\ stub here, patched to the real word's execution token once it's
\ defined below.
\ ==================================================================

LINE-MAX BUFFER: CMDSUB-LINE-BUF
LINE-MAX BUFFER: CMDSUB-LINES     \ the same text, NUL-separated per
                                   \ line, ready to be replayed
VARIABLE CMDSUB-LINES-LEN
VARIABLE CS-POS
VARIABLE CS-COPY-I
VARIABLE CS-NONBLANK?

256 CONSTANT CMDSUB-OUT-MAX   \ where the output buffer starts; it grows
CMDSUB-OUT-MAX BUFFER: CMDSUB-OUT-BUF
VARIABLE CMDSUB-OUT-LEN

: CMDSUB-SPLIT-LINES ( u --- )
  \ CMDSUB-LINE-BUF holds u raw bytes of command text, which may span
  \ several physical lines. Rewrites it into CMDSUB-LINES with each
  \ newline replaced by a NUL - the NUL-separated shape every replay
  \ source in this file uses - and records whether any non-blank
  \ character was seen at all, so "$( )" runs nothing.
  SHADOW{ CS-POS | CS-COPY-I }
  CS-POS @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER CMDSUB-LINES
  0 CS-NONBLANK? !
  BEGIN CS-COPY-I @ CS-POS @ < WHILE
    CMDSUB-LINE-BUF CS-COPY-I @ + C@
    DUP 10 = IF DROP 0 ELSE
      DUP WS? 0= IF -1 CS-NONBLANK? ! THEN
    THEN
    CMDSUB-LINES CS-COPY-I @ + C!
    CS-COPY-I @ 1+ CS-COPY-I !
  REPEAT
  0 CMDSUB-LINES CS-POS @ + C!
  CS-POS @ 1+ CMDSUB-LINES-LEN ! ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Traps (Iteration 271)
\ ==================================================================
\ TRAPS holds, per condition - 0 is EXIT, then the signal numbers - 0
\ for the default action, -1 for ignored, or a heap copy of the action.
\ A caught signal only raises a flag in the engine; CHECK-TRAPS runs the
\ actions between commands. A subshell resets the caught ones.
65 CONSTANT TRAP-SLOTS
CREATE TRAPS  TRAP-SLOTS CELLS ALLOT
TRAPS TRAP-SLOTS CELLS 0 FILL
VARIABLE IN-TRAP?
VARIABLE TRAP-COPY
VARIABLE TRAP-SAVED-STATUS
DEFER TRAP-RUN-CALL   ( c-addr --- )   \ tree.4: run a trap's action

: TRAP@ ( sig --- x )   CELLS TRAPS + @ ;
: TRAP! ( x sig --- )   CELLS TRAPS + ! ;
: TRAP-ACTION? ( x --- f )   DUP 0= SWAP -1 = OR 0= ;   \ a command, not default or ignored
: TRAP-FREE ( sig --- )
  DUP TRAP@ DUP TRAP-ACTION? IF FREE DROP ELSE DROP THEN
  0 SWAP TRAP! ;

: COPY-TRAP ( str --- )   \ a private copy into TRAP-COPY: the action may change the trap
  DUP CSTRLEN 1+ DUP ALLOCATE DROP DUP TRAP-COPY ! SWAP MOVE ;

: RUN-TRAP ( sig --- )
  TRAP@ DUP TRAP-ACTION? 0= IF DROP EXIT THEN
  IN-TRAP? @ IF DROP EXIT THEN
  -1 IN-TRAP? !
  COPY-TRAP
  LAST-STATUS @ TRAP-SAVED-STATUS !
  TRAP-COPY @ TRAP-RUN-CALL
  \ `$?` is what it was before the trap (XCU 2.14 trap) - unless the
  \ trap ended with `return n`, which sets it and applies to the trap
  \ rather than to whatever the shell was doing (Iteration 355).
  RETURN-PENDING? @ IF
    \ the return carries on out of the enclosing function, keeping the
    \ status it was given; clearing it here stopped the function from
    \ returning at all and the loop below it spun for ever
  ELSE
    TRAP-SAVED-STATUS @ LAST-STATUS !
  THEN
  TRAP-COPY @ FREE DROP
  0 IN-TRAP? ! ;

VARIABLE INT-SEEN?   \ an INT arrived: an interactive prompt starts a new line

DEFER REAP-CALL   ( --- )   \ the job table's silent reap, defined below

: CHECK-TRAPS ( --- )
  IN-TRAP? @ IF EXIT THEN
  \ A background child that has exited stays a zombie until someone
  \ waits for it, and `kill -0` on a zombie succeeds - so a script
  \ spinning on `kill -0 $!` never saw its child finish (Iteration 336).
  \ Only paid when there are jobs: a syscall per command otherwise.
  JOB# @ 0 > IF REAP-CALL THEN
  BEGIN SIGNALS-PENDING ?DUP WHILE
    DUP 2 = IF -1 INT-SEEN? ! THEN             \ ^C during a command
    RUN-TRAP
  REPEAT ;

: SHELL-EXIT ( status --- )   \ the shell ends: its EXIT trap runs first, once
  TRAP-SAVED-STATUS !
  0 TRAP@ TRAP-ACTION? IF
    0 TRAP@ COPY-TRAP  0 TRAP-FREE
    TRAP-SAVED-STATUS @ DUP >R LAST-STATUS !
    -1 IN-TRAP? !
    TRAP-COPY @ TRAP-RUN-CALL
    R> TRAP-SAVED-STATUS !
  THEN
  TRAP-SAVED-STATUS @ SYS-EXIT ;

: CHILD-RESET-JOB-SIGNALS ( --- )
  \ An interactive shell IGNORES TSTP, TTIN and TTOU (Iteration 320), and
  \ SIG_IGN survives exec - so every command it ran inherited a shell
  \ that cannot be stopped, and ^Z did nothing. A child puts them back
  \ to their default (Iteration 322).
  JOB-CONTROL? @ 0= IF EXIT THEN
  20 0 SIGNAL-ACTION DROP  21 0 SIGNAL-ACTION DROP  22 0 SIGNAL-ACTION DROP ;

: CHILD-RESET-TRAPS ( --- )   \ a subshell: caught signals to their default, no EXIT trap
  CHILD-RESET-JOB-SIGNALS
  TRAP-SLOTS 1 DO
    I TRAP@ TRAP-ACTION? IF I 0 SIGNAL-ACTION DROP I TRAP-FREE THEN
  LOOP
  0 TRAP@ TRAP-ACTION? IF 0 TRAP-FREE THEN
  0 IN-TRAP? ! ;

DEFER RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD-CALL

VARIABLE CS-FD
VARIABLE CMDSUB-PIPE-RD
VARIABLE CMDSUB-PIPE-WR
VARIABLE CMDSUB-PID

: READ-ALL-CMDSUB-OUTPUT ( fd --- )
  \ Everything the command writes, growing the buffer as it comes -
  \ until Iteration 251 the buffer was 256 bytes and the rest was left
  \ unread. Past EXPAND-HARD-MAX the rest is read and discarded, so the
  \ command is never left blocked on a full pipe, and it is reported.
  SHADOW{ CS-FD }
  0 CMDSUB-OUT-LEN !
  BEGIN
    CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ EXPAND-HARD-MAX < IF
      CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 4096 + ENSURE-BUFFER CMDSUB-OUT-BUF
      CMDSUB-OUT-BUF CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ +
      BUFFER-SIZE CMDSUB-OUT-BUF CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ -
      CS-FD @ READ-FILE DROP
      DUP CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ + CMDSUB-OUT-LEN !
    ELSE
      DRAIN-BUF LINE-MAX CS-FD @ READ-FILE DROP
      DUP IF EXPANSION-TOO-LARGE THEN
    THEN
  0= UNTIL ;

\ ==================================================================
\ The body arena
\ ==================================================================
\ Defined here rather than down with while/for because the replay
\ machinery below is its first user - the same file-ordering care the
\ deferred-word patches elsewhere in this file exist for.

4096 CONSTANT BODY-ARENA-INIT

VARIABLE BODY-ARENA-BASE    \ heap address, 0 until first use
VARIABLE BODY-ARENA-SIZE    \ current capacity in bytes
VARIABLE BODY-ARENA-TOP     \ bump pointer, an OFFSET not an address
VARIABLE BA-NEED
VARIABLE BA-NEWSIZE

: BODY@ ( offset --- addr )  BODY-ARENA-BASE @ + ;

: BODY-GROW ( needed --- )
  \ Grow (or first create) the arena to at least `needed` bytes,
  \ doubling so repeated growth stays amortized. RESIZE is allowed to
  \ MOVE the block - measured, not assumed: growing a blocked
  \ allocation past glibc's mmap threshold relocates it nearly every
  \ time. That is safe here precisely because nothing outside holds a
  \ raw pointer into the arena; everything holds an offset and goes
  \ through BODY@.
  SHADOW{ BA-NEED }
  BODY-ARENA-SIZE @ DUP 0= IF DROP BODY-ARENA-INIT THEN BA-NEWSIZE !
  BEGIN BA-NEWSIZE @ BA-NEED @ < WHILE
    BA-NEWSIZE @ 2 * BA-NEWSIZE !
  REPEAT
  BODY-ARENA-BASE @ 0= IF
    BA-NEWSIZE @ ALLOCATE ABORT" shell: body arena out of memory"
  ELSE
    BODY-ARENA-BASE @ BA-NEWSIZE @ RESIZE ABORT" shell: body arena out of memory"
  THEN
  BODY-ARENA-BASE !
  BA-NEWSIZE @ BODY-ARENA-SIZE ! ;

: BODY-ALLOC ( n --- offset )
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @ SWAP OVER +            ( old new )
  DUP BODY-ARENA-SIZE @ > IF DUP BODY-GROW THEN
  BODY-ARENA-TOP !                        ( old )
  ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Arithmetic expansion: $((expression)) - a real, precedence-climbing
\ recursive-descent expression grammar (each level below binds
\ tighter than the one above it, matching C/POSIX precedence):
\   AE-OR   := AE-AND ( "||" AE-AND )*
\   AE-AND  := AE-EQ ( "&&" AE-EQ )*
\   AE-EQ   := AE-REL ( ("=="|"!=") AE-REL )*
\   AE-REL  := AE-ADD ( ("<"|">"|"<="|">=") AE-ADD )*
\   AE-ADD  := AE-MUL ( ("+"|"-") AE-MUL )*
\   AE-MUL  := AE-PRIMARY ( ("*"|"/"|"%") AE-PRIMARY )*
\   AE-PRIMARY := NUMBER | NAME | "(" AE-OR ")" | ("-"|"+"|"!") AE-PRIMARY
\ Scope limits, all deliberate: no bitwise operators (&, |, ^, <<, >>,
\ ~), no ternary (?:), no assignment forms (=, +=, etc.) or ++/--
\ within the expression, no octal/hex literals (decimal only). A
\ variable that's unset, or whose value doesn't start with an optional
\ '-' followed by digits, is treated as 0 - matching how POSIX
\ arithmetic treats a non-numeric operand as an error in principle,
\ but this shell's own established style favors a harmless fallback
\ over a hard error throughout (see LOOKUP-VAR/EMIT-DECIMAL and
\ friends elsewhere in this file). Operates on ARITH-BUF, a private
\ copy of the expression text (extracted by EXPAND-ARITH below,
\ tracking paren depth so a nested "(...)" inside the expression
\ itself isn't mistaken for the arithmetic expansion's own closing
\ "))"), with its own AE-POS/AE-END position tracking, entirely
\ separate from the main tokenizer's own TOK-POS/TOK-END - this
\ evaluator has no awareness of quoting, $VAR expansion, or anything
\ else about the outer command line at all, only of the extracted
\ expression text itself.
\ ==================================================================

256 BUFFER: ARITH-BUF
VARIABLE AE-POS
VARIABLE AE-END

: AE-SKIP-WS ( --- )
  BEGIN
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
  REPEAT ;

: AE-DIGIT? ( c --- f )
  DUP 48 < 0= SWAP 58 < AND ;

: AE-HEX-DIGIT ( c --- n | -1 )
  DUP 48 - 10 U< IF 48 - EXIT THEN
  32 OR DUP 97 - 6 U< IF 87 - EXIT THEN
  DROP -1 ;

VARIABLE EXPANSION-FAILED?   \ an expansion error: the command must not run
VARIABLE AE-BASE
: AE-BASE-DIGIT ( c --- n | -1 )   \ 0-9 a-z A-Z, for `base#digits`
  DUP 48 - 10 U< IF 48 - EXIT THEN
  DUP 97 - 26 U< IF 97 - 10 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 65 - 26 U< IF 65 - 10 + EXIT THEN
  DROP -1 ;

: AE-PARSE-NUMBER ( --- n )
  \ C's constants, as POSIX asks (XCU 2.6.4): 0x.. hexadecimal, a leading
  \ 0 octal, otherwise decimal. Only decimal was read until Iteration
  \ 286, so `$(( 010 + 0x10 ))` came to 10 rather than 24.
  10 AE-BASE !
  AE-POS @ C@ 48 = IF
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 32 OR 120 = ELSE 0 THEN IF
      16 AE-BASE !  AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
    ELSE
      8 AE-BASE !   AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    THEN
  THEN
  0
  BEGIN
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ AE-HEX-DIGIT ELSE -1 THEN
    DUP 0< 0= OVER AE-BASE @ < AND
  WHILE
    SWAP AE-BASE @ * +
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
  REPEAT DROP
  \ `09` is not an octal constant: both references reject it
  \ `base#digits` (Iteration 331): the number just read was the base,
  \ and this took it for the whole value - `$((2#101))` gave 2.
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF
    AE-POS @ C@ 35 = IF
      DUP 2 < 0= OVER 37 < AND IF   \ a base between 2 and 36
        AE-BASE !
        AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
        0
        BEGIN
          AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ AE-BASE-DIGIT ELSE -1 THEN
          DUP 0< 0= OVER AE-BASE @ < AND
        WHILE
          SWAP AE-BASE @ * +
          AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
        REPEAT DROP
        EXIT
      THEN
    THEN
  THEN
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF
    AE-POS @ C@ AE-HEX-DIGIT 0< 0= IF
      S" shell: bad arithmetic constant" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
      -1 EXPANSION-FAILED? !
    THEN
  THEN ;
64 BUFFER: AE-VARNAME-BUF
VARIABLE AE-VN-LEN

: AE-READ-VARNAME ( --- )
  0 AE-VN-LEN !
  BEGIN
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ NAME-CHAR? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ AE-VARNAME-BUF AE-VN-LEN @ + C!
    AE-VN-LEN @ 1+ AE-VN-LEN !
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
  REPEAT
  0 AE-VARNAME-BUF AE-VN-LEN @ + C! ;

: AE-LOOKUP-NUMERIC ( --- n )
  \ An unset variable, or one whose value isn't itself a (possibly
  \ "-"-prefixed) run of digits, is treated as 0.
  AE-VARNAME-BUF LOOKUP-VAR DUP 0= IF
    DROP 0
  ELSE
    DUP C@ 45 = IF
      1+ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL NEGATE
    ELSE
      \ A leading `+` is as valid as a leading `-`: `p=+1; $((p))`
      \ is 1, and read as characters it was -49 (Iteration 380).
      DUP C@ 43 = IF 1+ THEN
      DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL
    THEN
  THEN ;

: AE-BOOL-TO-INT ( f --- n )  \ Forth's own -1/0 boolean convention to
                                \ POSIX arithmetic's 1/0
  NEGATE ;
: AE-TRUTHY? ( n --- f )  0= 0= ;

: AE-MUL-OP? ( c --- f )
  DUP 42 = OVER 47 = OR SWAP 37 = OR ;

: AE-ADD-OP? ( c --- f )
  DUP 43 = SWAP 45 = OR ;

DEFER AE-OR-CALL   \ AE-PRIMARY (a parenthesized subexpression is a
                      \ full expression in its own right) and AE-OR
                      \ (the top of the grammar) are mutually
                      \ recursive across several intervening levels -
                      \ rather than defining all seven levels as one
                      \ giant, deeply-nested word, AE-PRIMARY reaches
                      \ AE-OR through this deferred call, patched
                      \ right after AE-OR itself is defined below.

VARIABLE AE-QUIET   \ >0: in the branch of ?: whose value is discarded

32 BUFFER: AE-NUMBUF
VARIABLE AE-SN-NAME

: AE-STORE-NUMBER ( c-addr n --- )   \ name = n, as an assignment would
  \ Formats the number here rather than with N>STR, which is defined
  \ further down with the rest of the assignment machinery.
  AE-QUIET @ IF 2DROP EXIT THEN
  SWAP AE-SN-NAME !
  DUP 0< >R ABS 0 <# #S R> IF 45 HOLD THEN #>   ( a u )
  DUP >R AE-NUMBUF SWAP MOVE
  0 AE-NUMBUF R> + C!
  AE-SN-NAME @ AE-NUMBUF SET-SHVAR ;

: AE-PREFIX-STEP ( up? --- n )   \ ++a / --a: assign, then yield
  AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-POS @ C@ 36 = IF AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! THEN
  AE-READ-VARNAME
  AE-LOOKUP-NUMERIC
  SWAP IF 1+ ELSE 1- THEN
  DUP AE-VARNAME-BUF SWAP AE-STORE-NUMBER ;

: AE-PRIMARY ( --- n )
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 40 = IF                    ( '(' )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    AE-OR-CALL
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ 41 = IF AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! THEN THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  \ `++a` and `--a` assign before yielding the value
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF
    AE-POS @ C@ DUP 43 = SWAP 45 = OR
    AE-POS @ C@ AE-POS @ 1+ C@ = AND IF
      AE-POS @ C@ 43 = AE-PREFIX-STEP EXIT
    THEN
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 45 = IF                    ( unary '-' )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    RECURSE NEGATE
    EXIT
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 43 = IF                    ( unary '+' )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    RECURSE
    EXIT
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 33 = IF                    ( unary '!' logical not )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    RECURSE AE-TRUTHY? 0= AE-BOOL-TO-INT
    EXIT
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ 126 = IF                   ( unary '~' bitwise not )
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
    RECURSE INVERT
    EXIT
  THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-DIGIT? IF
    AE-PARSE-NUMBER
    EXIT
  THEN
  \ "$((  $a + 2 ))" - a '$' before a name inside an arithmetic
  \ expression. The text reaches here unexpanded, because
  \ NORMALIZE-OPERATORS copies a $((...)) region verbatim, so the
  \ sigil is simply skipped: POSIX allows a variable to be written
  \ with or without it here and both mean the same thing.
  AE-POS @ C@ 36 = IF AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! THEN
  AE-READ-VARNAME
  AE-LOOKUP-NUMERIC
  \ `a++` and `a--` (Iteration 331): these parsed as a variable followed
  \ by two unary signs and assigned nothing, so `$((a++))` left a alone.
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF
    AE-POS @ C@ DUP 43 = SWAP 45 = OR
    AE-POS @ C@ AE-POS @ 1+ C@ = AND IF
      AE-POS @ C@ 43 = IF DUP 1+ ELSE DUP 1- THEN
      AE-VARNAME-BUF SWAP AE-STORE-NUMBER
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
    THEN
  THEN ;



: AE-DIV ( n1 n2 --- quot rem )
\ Division as C and POSIX arithmetic do it: the quotient truncated
\ toward zero (SM/REM - the kernel's / floors, so $((-7/2)) was -4).
\ A zero divisor is reported and gives 0; it used to reach the CPU's
\ divide and kill the shell with SIGFPE (Iteration 257).
  DUP 0= IF
    AE-QUIET @ IF 2DROP 0 0 EXIT THEN         \ `1?2:1/0` must not complain
    2DROP S" shell: division by zero" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    -1 EXPANSION-FAILED? !
    0 0 EXIT
  THEN
  SWAP S>D ROT SM/REM SWAP ;

: AE-MUL ( --- n )
  AE-PRIMARY
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ AE-MUL-OP? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@
    DUP 42 = IF
      DROP AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-PRIMARY *
    ELSE DUP 47 = IF
      DROP AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-PRIMARY AE-DIV DROP
    ELSE
      DROP AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-PRIMARY AE-DIV SWAP DROP
    THEN THEN
  REPEAT ;

: AE-ADD ( --- n )
  AE-MUL
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ AE-ADD-OP? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ 43 = IF
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-MUL +
    ELSE
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! AE-MUL -
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

VARIABLE AE-SH-C

: AE-SHIFT-OP? ( --- f )   \ "<<" or ">>" at AE-POS?
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-SH-C !
  AE-SH-C @ 60 = AE-SH-C @ 62 = OR
  AE-POS @ 1+ C@ AE-SH-C @ = AND ;

: AE-ARSHIFT ( n k --- n' )
  \ An arithmetic shift: `-14 >> 3` is -2, not a huge positive number.
  \ Forth's RSHIFT is logical, so the sign bit was shifted in as data
  \ (Iteration 380). A floored division by two to the k, which is what
  \ an arithmetic right shift is.
  1 SWAP LSHIFT DUP 0= IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  SWAP S>D ROT FM/MOD NIP ;

: AE-SHIFT ( --- n )
  \ C/POSIX precedence: shifts bind tighter than the relational
  \ operators and looser than "+"/"-". AE-REL below now calls this
  \ instead of AE-ADD, and must not mistake "<<" for "<".
  AE-ADD
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-SHIFT-OP?
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ 60 = IF
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !  AE-ADD LSHIFT
    ELSE
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !  AE-ADD AE-ARSHIFT
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

: AE-REL ( --- n )
  \ "<="/">=" checked by matching the leading '<'/'>' first, then
  \ peeking one further character for a following '=' - matching how
  \ AE-EQ itself distinguishes "==<"/"!=" from a bare '='/'!' (which
  \ don't appear in this grammar at all, so a bare one is simply not
  \ matched by anything here and left for the caller to report as an
  \ unmatched trailing character, the same sparse-error-handling style
  \ used throughout this shell).
  AE-SHIFT
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF
      AE-POS @ C@ DUP 60 = SWAP 62 = OR
      AE-SHIFT-OP? 0= AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ 60 = IF                      ( starts with '<' )
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
      AE-POS @ AE-END @ < AE-POS @ C@ 61 = AND IF
        AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
        AE-SHIFT SWAP < 0= AE-BOOL-TO-INT     ( "<=" )
      ELSE
        AE-SHIFT < AE-BOOL-TO-INT             ( "<" )
      THEN
    ELSE                                       ( starts with '>' )
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
      AE-POS @ AE-END @ < AE-POS @ C@ 61 = AND IF
        AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
        AE-SHIFT < 0= AE-BOOL-TO-INT           ( ">=" )
      ELSE
        AE-SHIFT SWAP < AE-BOOL-TO-INT         ( ">" )
      THEN
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

: AE-EQ ( --- n )
  AE-REL
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF
      AE-POS @ C@ DUP 61 = SWAP 33 = OR
      AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 61 = ELSE 0 THEN AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ C@ 61 = IF
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
      AE-REL = AE-BOOL-TO-INT
    ELSE
      AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
      AE-REL = 0= AE-BOOL-TO-INT
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

VARIABLE AE-BIT-C

: AE-SINGLE-OP? ( c --- f )
  \ Is character c present at AE-POS and NOT doubled? Distinguishes
  \ the bitwise "&"/"|" from the logical "&&"/"||", which bind looser
  \ and are handled further out.
  AE-BIT-C !
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-BIT-C @ = 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ 1+ C@ AE-BIT-C @ <> ELSE -1 THEN ;

: AE-BITAND ( --- n )
  AE-EQ
  BEGIN AE-SKIP-WS 38 AE-SINGLE-OP? WHILE
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  AE-EQ AND
  REPEAT ;

: AE-BITXOR ( --- n )
  AE-BITAND
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ 94 = ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  AE-BITAND XOR
  REPEAT ;

: AE-BITOR ( --- n )
  AE-BITXOR
  BEGIN AE-SKIP-WS 124 AE-SINGLE-OP? WHILE
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  AE-BITXOR OR
  REPEAT ;

: AE-AND ( --- n )
  AE-BITOR
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < AND IF
      AE-POS @ C@ 38 = AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 38 = AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
    \ `&&` evaluates its right operand only when the left is true, so
    \ `x=0; $((x && 1/0))` is 0 rather than a complaint. The right side
    \ is still PARSED, to move the cursor past it, with AE-QUIET set -
    \ the same flag the untaken branch of `?:` uses (Iteration 380).
    AE-TRUTHY? IF
      AE-BITOR AE-TRUTHY? AE-BOOL-TO-INT
    ELSE
      1 AE-QUIET +!  AE-BITOR DROP  -1 AE-QUIET +!
      0
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

: AE-OR ( --- n )
  AE-AND
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < AND IF
      AE-POS @ C@ 124 = AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 124 = AND
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !
    \ ... and `||` evaluates its right operand only when the left is
    \ false.
    AE-TRUTHY? IF
      1 AE-QUIET +!  AE-AND DROP  -1 AE-QUIET +!
      1
    ELSE
      AE-AND AE-TRUTHY? AE-BOOL-TO-INT
    THEN
  REPEAT ;

VARIABLE AE-EVAL-LEN

24 BUFFER: AE-NUMBUF
64 BUFFER: AE-ASGN-NAME
VARIABLE AE-ASGN-OP
VARIABLE AE-SAVE-POS
VARIABLE AE-ASGN-C
VARIABLE AE-SLOT


: N>STR ( n --- c-addr )
  \ The same double-cell conversion as EMIT-DECIMAL, for the same
  \ reason: NEGATE cannot make the most negative cell positive, and
  \ the digit loop it fed then wrote backwards out of its buffer
  \ (Iteration 401).
  S>D TUCK DABS <# #S ROT SIGN #>           ( c-addr u )
  23 MIN  DUP >R
  AE-NUMBUF SWAP MOVE
  0 AE-NUMBUF R@ + C!
  R> DROP  AE-NUMBUF ;

: AE-ASGN-OP? ( --- f )
  \ At AE-POS: "=" (not "=="), or one of "+= -= *= /= %=".
  \ Consumes the operator and records it in AE-ASGN-OP (0 for plain
  \ "="), or leaves AE-POS untouched and answers false.
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-ASGN-C !
  AE-ASGN-C @ 61 = IF
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 61 = IF 0 EXIT THEN THEN
    0 AE-ASGN-OP !  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  -1 EXIT
  THEN
  \ `<<=` and `>>=`: three characters, and the first two must match, so
  \ `x <= 3` stays a comparison (Iteration 380).
  AE-ASGN-C @ 60 = AE-ASGN-C @ 62 = OR IF
    AE-POS @ 2 + AE-END @ < IF
      AE-POS @ 1+ C@ AE-ASGN-C @ =  AE-POS @ 2 + C@ 61 =  AND IF
        AE-ASGN-C @ AE-ASGN-OP !  AE-POS @ 3 + AE-POS !  -1 EXIT
      THEN
    THEN
    0 EXIT
  THEN
  \ `&=`, `^=` and `|=`: two characters, and `&&` or `||` is not one.
  AE-ASGN-C @ 38 = AE-ASGN-C @ 94 = OR AE-ASGN-C @ 124 = OR IF
    AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < IF
      AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 61 = IF
        AE-ASGN-C @ AE-ASGN-OP !  AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !  -1 EXIT
      THEN
    THEN
    0 EXIT
  THEN
  AE-ASGN-C @ AE-MUL-OP? AE-ASGN-C @ AE-ADD-OP? OR 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-END @ < 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ 1+ C@ 61 = 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-ASGN-C @ AE-ASGN-OP !  AE-POS @ 2 + AE-POS !  -1 ;

: AE-TRY-ASSIGN? ( --- f )
  \ Lookahead for "NAME op= ...". Restores AE-POS on any mismatch, so
  \ an ordinary expression starting with a name is unaffected.
  AE-POS @ AE-SAVE-POS !
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < 0= IF AE-SAVE-POS @ AE-POS ! 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ NAME-CHAR? 0= IF AE-SAVE-POS @ AE-POS ! 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ C@ AE-DIGIT?   IF AE-SAVE-POS @ AE-POS ! 0 EXIT THEN
  AE-READ-VARNAME
  AE-VARNAME-BUF AE-ASGN-NAME 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-ASGN-OP? 0= IF AE-SAVE-POS @ AE-POS ! 0 EXIT THEN
  -1 ;

: AE-ASGN-OLD ( --- n )   \ current numeric value of the target
  AE-ASGN-NAME AE-VARNAME-BUF 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  AE-LOOKUP-NUMERIC ;

DEFER AE-EXPR-CALL   ( --- n )

: AE-COND ( --- n )   \ XCU 2.6.4's ternary, which was missing entirely
  AE-OR
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ 63 = ELSE 0 THEN 0= IF EXIT THEN
  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !
  AE-TRUTHY? >R                              \ was it taken: on the return stack
  R@ 0= IF 1 AE-QUIET +! THEN
  AE-EXPR-CALL                               ( then )
  R@ 0= IF -1 AE-QUIET +! THEN
  AE-SKIP-WS
  AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF
    AE-POS @ C@ 58 = IF AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS ! THEN
  THEN
  R@ IF 1 AE-QUIET +! THEN
  RECURSE                                    ( then else )
  R@ IF -1 AE-QUIET +! THEN
  R> IF DROP ELSE NIP THEN ;

: AE-EXPR ( --- n )
  \ Lowest precedence level: assignment, right-associative. Anything
  \ that is not an assignment falls straight through to AE-OR, so this
  \ costs one lookahead per expression.
  BODY-ARENA-TOP @
  SHADOW{ BODY-ARENA-TOP | AE-SLOT }
  AE-TRY-ASSIGN? 0= IF AE-COND EXIT THEN
  \ The target name and the operator both have to survive the
  \ recursive evaluation of the right-hand side, which may itself be
  \ an assignment ("a=b=1") and overwrite AE-ASGN-NAME/AE-ASGN-OP.
  \ The operator rides on the data stack; the name gets a slot in the
  \ body arena, released by the local holding the bump pointer.
  64 BODY-ALLOC AE-SLOT !
  AE-ASGN-NAME AE-SLOT @ BODY@ 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  AE-ASGN-OP @
  RECURSE                                   ( op rhs )
  AE-SLOT @ BODY@ AE-ASGN-NAME 64 SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  SWAP ?DUP IF
    DUP 43 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD + ELSE
    DUP 45 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD SWAP - ELSE
    DUP 42 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD * ELSE
    DUP 47 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD SWAP / ELSE
    DUP 60 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD SWAP LSHIFT ELSE
    DUP 62 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD SWAP AE-ARSHIFT ELSE
    DUP 38 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD AND ELSE
    DUP 94 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD XOR ELSE
    DUP 124 = IF DROP AE-ASGN-OLD OR ELSE
                DROP AE-ASGN-OLD SWAP MOD
    THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN THEN
  THEN
  AE-QUIET @ IF EXIT THEN                    \ the untaken branch assigns nothing
  DUP N>STR
  AE-ASGN-NAME SWAP SET-SHVAR ;

\ Patched here, not next to AE-OR: the deferred call must resolve to
\ AE-EXPR, which is defined above only now.
: AE-COMMA ( --- n )   \ expr [ , expr ]... - the last one's value (bash's;
  \ dash rejects it, POSIX does not require it, and giving the FIRST
  \ value quietly, as this did, is the worst of the three).
  AE-EXPR
  BEGIN
    AE-SKIP-WS
    AE-POS @ AE-END @ < IF AE-POS @ C@ 44 = ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    DROP  AE-POS @ 1+ AE-POS !  AE-EXPR
  REPEAT ;

' AE-COMMA IS AE-OR-CALL    \ a parenthesized subexpression may assign
' AE-EXPR IS AE-EXPR-CALL   \ the middle of ?: is a whole expression

: AE-EVAL ( c-addr u --- n )
  \ Copies (c-addr u) into ARITH-BUF (a private copy, so the
  \ evaluator's own AE-POS/AE-END never alias the main tokenizer's
  \ LINE-BUF/TOK-POS/TOK-END) and evaluates it as a full expression.
  DUP AE-EVAL-LEN !
  ARITH-BUF SWAP MOVE
  ARITH-BUF AE-POS !
  ARITH-BUF AE-EVAL-LEN @ + AE-END !
  AE-COMMA ;

VARIABLE CMDSUB-STATUS   \ the FIRST command substitution's exit status
VARIABLE CMDSUB-RAN?     \ ... and whether one ran in this command

VARIABLE CMDSUB-NODE   \ the parsed subtree the child is to run, or 0
DEFER RUN-CMDSUB-JOB-CALL

: WAIT->STATUS ( raw --- status )
  \ wait(2)'s status word. A child killed by a signal exits 128+n, as
  \ both references report it; only the normal-exit byte was read until
  \ Iteration 299, so `kill $p; wait $p` said 0 where they say 143.
  DUP 127 AND ?DUP IF                        \ terminated by a signal
    NIP 128 +
  ELSE
    8 RSHIFT 255 AND
  THEN ;

: RUN-CMDSUB-TEXT ( c-addr u --- )   \ text the child parses and runs
  DUP 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER CMDSUB-LINE-BUF
  DUP CS-COPY-I !
  CMDSUB-LINE-BUF SWAP MOVE
  0 CMDSUB-LINE-BUF CS-COPY-I @ + C!
  CS-COPY-I @ CMDSUB-SPLIT-LINES
  0 CMDSUB-NODE !
  RUN-CMDSUB-JOB-CALL ;

: RUN-CMDSUB-JOB ( --- )   \ fork, let the child produce it, read it here
  \ Split out of EXPAND-CMDSUB (276) and split again in 283: the child's
  \ work - a parsed subtree, or text to parse - is the caller's business.
  CS-NONBLANK? @ CMDSUB-NODE @ 0<> OR IF      \ text with something in it, or a subtree
    PIPE DROP CMDSUB-PIPE-WR ! CMDSUB-PIPE-RD !
    FORK DUP CMDSUB-PID !
    0 = IF
      CMDSUB-PIPE-WR @ 1 DUP2 DROP
      CMDSUB-PIPE-WR @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      CMDSUB-PIPE-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      CHILD-RESET-TRAPS
      RUN-CMDSUB-CHILD-CALL
      127 SYS-EXIT
    THEN
    CMDSUB-PIPE-WR @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
    CMDSUB-PIPE-RD @ READ-ALL-CMDSUB-OUTPUT
    CMDSUB-PIPE-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
    \ its status is the status of a command that is only assignments
    \ (XCU 2.9.1; Iteration 269 - it was dropped)
    \ An assignment-only command takes the status of the FIRST
    \ substitution in it, which is what both references report:
    \ `v=`exit 2` `false`` is 2 in bash and dash alike. This kept the
    \ last one and answered 1 (Iteration 345).
    CMDSUB-PID @ WAITPID DROP WAIT->STATUS
    CMDSUB-RAN? @ IF DROP ELSE CMDSUB-STATUS ! THEN
    -1 CMDSUB-RAN? !
    BEGIN                                    ( strip trailing newlines )
      CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 0 > IF
        CMDSUB-OUT-BUF CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 1- + C@ 10 =
      ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE
      CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 1- CMDSUB-OUT-LEN !
    REPEAT
    \ CMDSUB-OUT-LEN can genuinely be 0 here (the substituted command
    \ produced no output at all, e.g. $(true) or $(printf)) - guarded
    \ explicitly rather than relying on "0 0 DO ... LOOP" to mean
    \ zero iterations. It doesn't in this kernel: DO/LOOP here treats
    \ start=limit as wrapping through the *entire* unsigned range
    \ rather than skipping the loop, so an unguarded zero-length splice
    \ here read (and wrote, via EMIT-TOK-CHAR) billions of bytes past
    \ CMDSUB-OUT-BUF until it hit unmapped memory and segfaulted -
    \ found via mrsh's async.sh (see PROGRESS.md's crash-hardening
    \ entry) and reproduced down to a single command: `echo $(true)`.
    CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 0 > IF
      \ EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR, not EMIT-TOK-CHAR: an *unquoted* command
      \ substitution is subject to IFS field splitting exactly as an
      \ unquoted $VAR is, so "for c in $(echo a s d f)" runs four
      \ times. EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR checks IN-DQ-CONTEXT? itself, so
      \ "$(...)" inside double quotes still yields one word.
      CMDSUB-OUT-BUF CMDSUB-OUT-LEN @ 0 DO
        DUP I + C@ EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR
      LOOP DROP
    THEN
  THEN ;

' RUN-CMDSUB-JOB IS RUN-CMDSUB-JOB-CALL

VARIABLE EAP-I
VARIABLE EAP-AT
VARIABLE EMPTY-AT-WORD?   \ this word was an unsatisfied "$@"

: EMIT-ALL-POS-PARAMS ( at? --- )
  \ Emits every positional parameter. at? distinguishes $@ from $*,
  \ which differ only INSIDE double quotes: "$@" gives one field per
  \ parameter, "$*" one field with them joined. Unquoted they behave
  \ alike, because the joining space is then an ordinary IFS character
  \ and normal splitting does the rest.
  \
  \ "$@" is the one expansion POSIX allows to produce several fields
  \ from a quoted word, so it cannot go through EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR -
  \ which suppresses splitting inside quotes, correctly, for
  \ everything else - and calls IFS-SPLIT-HERE directly.
  SHADOW{ EAP-AT | EAP-I }
  \ POSIX: "$@" with no positional parameters produces NO field, where
  \ any other quoted empty word produces one. Recorded here and acted
  \ on when the token ends, since whether the word is empty overall is
  \ not known yet - "x$@y" is still a single field "xy".
  EAP-AT @ POS-PARAM-COUNT @ 0= AND
  TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ = AND IF
    -1 EMPTY-AT-WORD? !
  THEN
  BEGIN EAP-I @ POS-PARAM-COUNT @ < WHILE
    \ The separator needs room reserved just as the parameters do.
    \ Without this each one overwrote a byte of unread input, so
    \ "echo \"[$*]\"" with two parameters lost its closing bracket -
    \ the same in-place-growth hazard as the numeric expansions in
    \ Iteration 46, in the one place that emits a character of its own
    \ rather than copying a value.
    EAP-I @ 0 > IF
      \ Unquoted, each positional parameter is a field of its own,
      \ whatever IFS holds: `IFS=:; for x in $*` gives one field per
      \ parameter. This emitted a space and left the splitting to IFS,
      \ which worked only while IFS contained a space (Iteration 358).
      \
      \ The field break needs room reserved too. IFS-SPLIT-HERE writes
      \ a NUL and advances TOK-OUT, so it grows the output by one byte
      \ like the separator below. It is balanced when
      \ EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR calls it - the IFS character that triggered
      \ the split was consumed from the input and not emitted - but
      \ here nothing was consumed: the break is synthesised. Without
      \ this, `"1  $@  2"` with three parameters lost its "  2"
      \ (Iteration 103).
      \ ... but an assignment's value is not split, so `v=$*` joins
      \ like the quoted form rather than making fields.
      \ Inside an assignment nothing is split, so both forms join with
      \ IFS's first character - `v="$@"` is one value, not two words.
      WORD-IS-ASSIGN? @ 0=
      IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ 0= EAP-AT @ IN-DQ-CONTEXT? @ AND OR AND IF
        IFS-SPLIT-HERE
      ELSE
        \ `"$*"` joins with IFS's first character, and with nothing at
        \ all when IFS is set and empty (Iteration 257).
        IFS-FIRST DUP 0< IF DROP ELSE EMIT-EXPANDED-CHAR THEN
      THEN
    THEN
    EAP-I @ POS-PARAM-SLOT TYPE0-TO-TOK-SPLIT
    EAP-I @ 1+ EAP-I !
  REPEAT ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Parameter-expansion modifiers: ${#VAR} (length), ${VAR:-word}/
\ ${VAR-word} (default value), ${VAR:=word}/${VAR=word} (assign
\ default), ${VAR:+word}/${VAR+word} (alternate value). The ':'
\ variants trigger on VAR being either unset OR empty; the plain
\ variants trigger on unset only. Scope limits, both documented here
\ rather than silently: the "word" portion is treated as a literal
\ string - no further $-expansion is applied to it, unlike real POSIX
\ - and no nested braces are tracked while reading it, so an inner
\ "}" ends it early. ${VAR%word}/${VAR%%word}/${VAR#word}/
\ ${VAR##word} (prefix/suffix removal) are a separate, not yet
\ implemented piece - genuinely new pattern-matching logic, not just
\ string comparison, unlike the modifiers here.
\ ==================================================================

\ SCAN-TOKEN's one-character dispatch - quotes, backslash, '$', a
\ backquote, an assignment tilde. Patched to the real word far below,
\ where COPY-DOUBLE-QUOTED and friends exist; needed here because the
\ "word" in ${VAR:-word} is ordinary word text and gets expanded by
\ exactly the same machinery as any other, rather than by a second
\ copy of it.

: EMIT-OPTION-FLAGS ( --- )   \ $-
  OPT-NOTIFY @ IF 98 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN      \ -b (Iteration 386)
  OPT-ERREXIT @ IF 101 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
  OPT-NOGLOB @ IF 102 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
  OPT-NOEXEC @ IF 110 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
  OPT-NOUNSET @ IF 117 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
  OPT-ALLEXPORT @ IF 97 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
  OPT-NOCLOBBER @ IF 67 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
  OPT-VERBOSE @ IF 118 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN
  OPT-XTRACE @ IF 120 EMIT-TOK-CHAR THEN ;

: NOUNSET-CHECK ( c-addr u --- )   \ an unset parameter was expanded
  OPT-NOUNSET @ 0= IF 2DROP EXIT THEN
  S" shell: " ERR-TYPE ERR-TYPE S" : parameter not set" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  -1 UNSET-FATAL? !  -1 EXPANSION-FAILED? ! ;

64  BUFFER: PW-HOME
64  BUFFER: PW-WANT
VARIABLE PW-LEN
: PASSWD-HOME ( name-addr name-len --- addr | 0 )
  \ A named user's home directory. Returns 0 for an unknown user,
  \ which leaves "~nosuchuser" literal - what POSIX requires.
  \
  \ Through GETPWHOME, which is getpwnam(3), and therefore through
  \ NSS. This used to read /etc/passwd directly, which is one NSS
  \ source among several: on a host using LDAP, SSSD, NIS or
  \ systemd-homed a real user need not appear in that file at all,
  \ and "~alice" stayed literal. The failure was quiet and
  \ environment-dependent - it could never show up on a developer
  \ machine and would show up on exactly the hosts where centrally
  \ managed accounts are the point, which is why it was recorded in
  \ GOALS.md rather than trusted to be remembered (Iteration 120).
  \
  \ The name is copied into a NUL-terminated buffer first: it arrives
  \ as an address and a length pointing into the line being scanned,
  \ and getpwnam wants a C string. The result points into getpwnam's
  \ own static storage, valid until the next call, so it is copied
  \ out here rather than returned raw.
  PW-LEN !
  PW-WANT PW-LEN @ MOVE
  0 PW-WANT PW-LEN @ + C!
  PW-WANT GETPWHOME
  DUP 0= IF EXIT THEN
  \ The copy takes the address and its length, and leaves nothing else:
  \ there used to be one more DROP here, which took a cell from the
  \ CALLER's stack on every ~user. It surfaced in Iteration 253, when a
  \ guard page above the empty data stack turned the resulting
  \ underflow into a fault. PW-HOME grows to fit, too: a home over 63
  \ characters used to leave the previous one in place.
  DUP CSTRLEN DUP 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER PW-HOME
  PW-HOME BUFFER-SIZE PW-HOME SAFE-COPY-NUL DROP
  PW-HOME ;


1 ARGS-BUFFER: ARGV-ASSIGN        \ the parser's verdict, per word
1 ARGS-BUFFER: ARGV-REDIR-TGT     \ this word is a redirection's target
1 ARGS-BUFFER: EW-ASSIGN
1 ARGS-BUFFER: EW-RTGT
1 CELLS ARGS-BUFFER: ARGV-NODES   \ each word's tree node, when it has one
1 CELLS ARGS-BUFFER: EW-NODES     \ ... copied like EW-ARGV (Iteration 275)
DEFER ENC-EXPAND-CALL   ( node --- )   \ tree.4: expand a word's encoding
: EMIT-RUN ( c-addr u --- )   \ a whole run of literal bytes
  DUP 0= IF 2DROP EXIT THEN
  \ A split left pending by an expansion is performed here, because the
  \ text that ends the run of whitespace may arrive in bulk rather than
  \ character by character: `H${x:+ }H` is two fields, and was one
  \ because the trailing `H` came through this word (Iteration 372).
  IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? @ IF
    0 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !
    TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ <> QUOTE-BEFORE-RUN? @ OR
    QUOTE-IN-RUN? @ OR AFTER-RUN-BREAK? @ OR IF
      IFS-SPLIT-HERE
    THEN
    0 AFTER-RUN-BREAK? !
  THEN
  BEGIN TOK-OUT @ OVER + TOK-OUT-END @ > WHILE
    GROW-OUT 0= IF 2DROP EXPANSION-TOO-LARGE EXIT THEN
  REPEAT
  TUCK TOK-OUT @ SWAP MOVE
  TOK-OUT @ + TOK-OUT ! ;

1 CELLS ARGS-BUFFER: EW-ARGV
1 ARGS-BUFFER: EW-ARGQ
1 ARGS-BUFFER: EW-LIT
1 ARGS-BUFFER: EW-QUOT      \ the word's own quoting, for COPY-LITERAL
VARIABLE EW-ARGC
VARIABLE EW-I

: LITERAL-FITS? ( --- f )   \ the word at TOK-POS fits the output as it is
  TOK-OUT @ TOK-END @ TOK-POS @ - 1+ + TOK-OUT-END @ <
  ARGC @ ROOM-FOR-ARG? AND ;
VARIABLE CL-N   \ the literal word's length, for the mark scan
VARIABLE CL-BRACKET   \ where an unclosed `[` sits in it, or -1

: COPY-LITERAL ( --- )
  \ A word the parser saw no quote, expansion, tilde or pattern character
  \ in comes out as it went in: copied, not scanned a character at a time
  \ (Iteration 266 - scanning was 40% of a loop's dispatches).
  TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
  EW-ARGQ EW-I @ + C@ ARGV-NAME-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
  EW-QUOT EW-I @ + C@ ARGV-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
  \ A literal word has no quoting in it, so every metacharacter in it is
  \ live: mark them, since a `case` pattern is matched from the marks
  \ and this path never recorded any (Iteration 341).
  TOK-END @ TOK-POS @ - 1+                  ( n, the NUL too )
  DUP CL-N !
  TOK-POS @ TOK-OUT @ 2 PICK MOVE
  \ `*` and `?` always make the word a pattern; a `[` only does when a
  \ `]` follows it, so `[ $x ]` - every test command in every script -
  \ does not drag the whole word through pathname expansion. That cost
  \ 3.4% of a loop benchmark's dispatches from Iteration 341 until this
  \ one (Iteration 367).
  -1 CL-BRACKET !
  CL-N @ 1- 0 ?DO
    TOK-POS @ I + C@
    DUP 42 = OVER 63 = OR IF
      TOK-OUT @ I + OUT-BASE - GLOB-MARK-OFF
    ELSE
      DUP 45 = OVER 93 = OR IF
        TOK-OUT @ I + OUT-BASE - GLOB-MARK-DASH
      THEN
      DUP 91 = IF CL-BRACKET @ 0< IF I CL-BRACKET ! THEN THEN
      DUP 93 = CL-BRACKET @ 0< 0= AND IF
        TOK-OUT @ CL-BRACKET @ + OUT-BASE - GLOB-MARK-OFF
        -1 CL-BRACKET !
      THEN
    THEN DROP
  LOOP
  TOK-OUT +!  ARGC @ 1+ ARGC !
  TOK-END @ TOK-POS ! ;

VARIABLE ARGV-NONLITERAL   \ how many of this command's words need expanding

: ALL-LITERAL? ( --- f )   \ no word needs expanding, splitting or globbing
  \ Counted as the words were added (tree.4's ARGV-ADD) rather than
  \ scanned here: the scan cost a command with any expansion in it about
  \ ten dispatches a word to learn nothing.
  ARGV-NONLITERAL @ 0= ;

\ The four spellings of "nothing but $@". These stay byte-built: they
\ are COUNTED strings, where CSTR," lays down a NUL-terminated one, and
\ two of them hold a double quote, which PARSE would read as the end of
\ the text (Iteration 353).
CREATE OA-BARE   2 C, 36 C, 64 C,                                  \ $@
CREATE OA-QUOTED 4 C, 34 C, 36 C, 64 C, 34 C,                      \ "$@"
CREATE OA-BRACE  4 C, 36 C, 123 C, 64 C, 125 C,                    \ ${@}
CREATE OA-QBRACE 6 C, 34 C, 36 C, 123 C, 64 C, 125 C, 34 C,        \ "${@}"

: OA-MATCH? ( a u counted --- f )   COUNT STR= ;

: WORD-IS-ONLY-AT? ( --- f )
  \ An unsatisfied "$@" produces no field - but only when the word holds
  \ nothing else. `"$@"""` still produces one empty field, because the
  \ quoted empty string beside it is a word in its own right (Iteration
  \ 333). The word's own source text answers it.
  EW-ARGV EW-I @ CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN      ( a u )
  2DUP OA-BARE   OA-MATCH? IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP OA-QUOTED OA-MATCH? IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP OA-BRACE  OA-MATCH? IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
       OA-QBRACE OA-MATCH? ;

DEFER NOSPLIT-ON    ( --- )   \ tree.4's: expand this word without splitting
DEFER NOSPLIT-OFF   ( --- )
VARIABLE REDIR-TARGET-WORD?   \ expanding a redirection's target

\ ---- assignment prefixes, applied as they are expanded (Iteration 357)
\ Each one is applied the moment its own expansion is finished, so the
\ next prefix sees it; the originals are put back before the command's
\ own words are expanded, and TEMP-ASSIGN or TRY-ASSIGNMENT applies the
\ finished values afterwards as it always did.

8 CONSTANT MAX-PREFIX-SAVE
MAX-PREFIX-SAVE CELLS BUFFER: EWA-NAMES
MAX-PREFIX-SAVE CELLS BUFFER: EWA-OLDS
VARIABLE EWA-N
VARIABLE EWA-RUN?     \ still inside the leading run of assignments

DEFER EQPOS-CALL       ( c-addr --- eqpos | -1 )   \ defined further down
DEFER SPLIT-ASSIGN-CALL ( c-addr eqpos --- )
DEFER STRDUP-CALL      ( c-addr --- a )
DEFER NAME-BUF-CALL    ( --- c-addr )
DEFER VALUE-BUF-CALL   ( --- c-addr )

: EWA-APPLY ( c-addr --- )   \ NAME=value, expanded
  DUP EQPOS-CALL DUP -1 = IF 2DROP EXIT THEN
  SPLIT-ASSIGN-CALL
  EWA-N @ MAX-PREFIX-SAVE < IF
    NAME-BUF-CALL STRDUP-CALL EWA-NAMES EWA-N @ CELLS + !
    NAME-BUF-CALL GET-SHVAR ?DUP IF STRDUP-CALL ELSE 0 THEN
    EWA-OLDS EWA-N @ CELLS + !
    1 EWA-N +!
    NAME-BUF-CALL VALUE-BUF-CALL SET-SHVAR
  THEN ;

: EWA-UNDO ( --- )   \ put the originals back
  BEGIN EWA-N @ 0 > WHILE
    -1 EWA-N +!
    EWA-NAMES EWA-N @ CELLS + @
    EWA-OLDS EWA-N @ CELLS + @ ?DUP IF
      2DUP SET-SHVAR FREE DROP           \ ( name old ) is the order it wants
    ELSE
      DUP REMOVE-SHVAR
    THEN
    FREE DROP
  REPEAT
  0 EWA-RUN? ! ;

: EXPAND-WORDS ( --- )
  \ ARGV holds the line's raw words. Expands each one into EXP-BUF and
  \ rebuilds ARGV from the results.
  \
  \ One raw word can produce several fields - IFS splitting of an
  \ unquoted expansion - or none at all, which is why the raw list is
  \ snapshotted first rather than rewritten in place.
  \
  \ SCAN-TOKEN stops at unquoted whitespace. That cannot occur inside
  \ a raw word - the spans were cut at exactly those points - but it
  \ certainly can inside a word that has already been expanded once,
  \ and some paths do reach this twice: a group used as a pipeline
  \ stage has its body carved out of an ARGV the enclosing line
  \ expanded, then runs it through RUN-TOKENIZED, whose leaf expands
  \ it again. So each word is scanned until its span is used up,
  \ splitting at any whitespace left in the middle. A second pass then
  \ re-splits rather than truncating - `"a b"` stays two words instead
  \ of becoming `a`.
  0 EXPANSION-FAILED? !
  0 EWA-N !  -1 EWA-RUN? !
  0 GLOB-MARKS# !  0 GLOB-META# !
  0 IFS-CACHED? !
  \ Nothing to expand: every word is literal text the lexer already put
  \ in the tree, and ARGV points at it with its flags set. The pass
  \ below would copy all of it into the output buffer to arrive at the
  \ same thing (Iteration 293).
  ARGC @ 0 > IF ALL-LITERAL? IF EXIT THEN THEN
  ARGC @ EW-ARGC !
  \ The word's own entries are saved before the second pass overwrites
  \ ARGV with the fields it produces. Six MOVEs, not six copies per word:
  \ cell-at-a-time was 840 dispatches of every loop iteration in
  \ tests/bench-vm, 9% of it (PERFORMANCE.md, Iteration 293).
  ARGV             EW-ARGV  EW-ARGC @ CELLS MOVE
  ARGV-NODES       EW-NODES EW-ARGC @ CELLS MOVE
  ARGV-ASSIGN      EW-ASSIGN EW-ARGC @ MOVE
  ARGV-REDIR-TGT   EW-RTGT  EW-ARGC @ MOVE
  ARGV-NAME-QUOTED EW-ARGQ  EW-ARGC @ MOVE
  ARGV-QUOTED      EW-QUOT  EW-ARGC @ MOVE
  ARGV-LITERAL     EW-LIT   EW-ARGC @ MOVE
  \ Write into whichever of the two buffers the current words are NOT
  \ in. Normally that is EXP-BUF, since the words come from
  \ TOKENIZE-RAW. But a group used as a pipeline stage has its body
  \ carved out of an ARGV the enclosing line already expanded, and
  \ that body is expanded again when it runs; reading and writing one
  \ buffer at once corrupts it, where alternating merely re-splits -
  \ which for an already-expanded word is what would have happened
  \ anyway.
  ARGC @ 0 > IF ARGV @ ELSE 0 THEN
  DUP EXP-BUF 1- > SWAP EXP-BUF BUFFER-SIZE EXP-BUF + < AND IF
    BUFFER-BODY TOK-BUF ELSE BUFFER-BODY EXP-BUF
  THEN
  DUP OUT-BODY !  0 EXP-OVERFLOW? !
  DUP @ TOK-OUT !
  DUP @ SWAP CELL+ @ + TOK-OUT-END !
  0 ARGC !
  0 EW-I !
  BEGIN EW-I @ EW-ARGC @ < WHILE
    EW-ASSIGN EW-I @ + C@ WORD-IS-ASSIGN? !
    \ XCU 2.7: the word after a redirection operator is expanded but NOT
    \ field-split, so `v='a b'; > $v` writes to one file named `a b`.
    \ This shell split it like any other word until Iteration 335.
    EW-RTGT EW-I @ + C@ REDIR-TARGET-WORD? !
    EW-ARGV EW-I @ CELLS + @ TOK-POS !
    TOK-POS @ DUP CSTRLEN + TOK-END !
    EW-LIT EW-I @ + C@ IF LITERAL-FITS? ELSE 0 THEN IF
      COPY-LITERAL
    ELSE
    BEGIN
    0 TOK-WAS-QUOTED? !
    0 AFTER-RUN-BREAK? !
    0 QUOTE-IN-RUN? !
    0 QUOTE-BEFORE-RUN? !
    0 EMPTY-AT-WORD? !
    0 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !
    0 IN-DQ-CONTEXT? !
    ARGC @ ROOM-FOR-ARG? IF
      TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
      EW-ARGQ EW-I @ + C@ ARGV-NAME-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
      \ every word is expanded from its encoding (Stage C)
      REDIR-TARGET-WORD? @ IF NOSPLIT-ON THEN
      EW-NODES EW-I @ CELLS + @ ENC-EXPAND-CALL
      NOSPLIT-OFF
      \ Whitespace still pending at the word's end is a delimiter when
      \ quoting followed it: `${x:+b ''}` ends with an empty field
      \ (Iteration 364).
      \ ... and only when there is a field to close: with nothing before
      \ the whitespace, `${x:+ ''}` is the one empty field the quotes
      \ make, not two.
      \ Offsets cannot tell quoting before the whitespace from quoting
      \ after it, since pending whitespace is not emitted, so each is
      \ noted as it happens.
      IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? @ QUOTE-IN-RUN? @ AND
      TOK-OUT @ ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ <> QUOTE-BEFORE-RUN? @ OR
      AND IF
        IFS-SPLIT-HERE  0 IFS-SPLIT-PENDING? !  0 QUOTE-IN-RUN? !
      THEN
      TOK-END @ TOK-POS !
      0 EMIT-TOK-CHAR
      TOK-WAS-QUOTED? @ ARGV-QUOTED ARGC @ + C!
      \ POSIX: an unquoted expansion that produces nothing yields NO
      \ field at all - "echo A ${nope:-} B" passes two arguments, not
      \ three. A word that was quoted still counts, even when empty:
      \ "" is a field.
      \
      \ ARGV-NAME-QUOTED, not TOK-WAS-QUOTED?: the latter is also set
      \ by an expansion (it is EXPAND-VAR's signal that the result is
      \ not to be re-read as an operator), so it cannot distinguish
      \ "" from ${nope:-}. ARGV-NAME-QUOTED records an actual leading
      \ quote character, which is the question being asked here.
      \ An empty field is dropped only when nothing in the word was
      \ quoted. The parser's flag covers `""` at the start of a word;
      \ TOK-WAS-QUOTED? covers quoting that appeared during expansion,
      \ as in `${x:+''}` (Iteration 355). A redirection target is kept
      \ whatever it holds (Iteration 344).
      \
      \ Written as nested tests rather than one expression: AND does
      \ not short-circuit, so WORD-IS-ONLY-AT? - four string compares -
      \ ran for every word the shell expanded (Iteration 367).
      ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + @ C@ 0= IF
        REDIR-TARGET-WORD? @ IF
          0                                   \ a target is never dropped
        ELSE
          ARGV-NAME-QUOTED ARGC @ + C@ 0= TOK-WAS-QUOTED? @ 0= AND IF
            -1                                \ nothing quoted it: drop
          ELSE
            EMPTY-AT-WORD? @ IF WORD-IS-ONLY-AT? ELSE 0 THEN
          THEN
        THEN
      ELSE 0 THEN
      IF
        TOK-OUT @ 1- TOK-OUT !      ( un-emit the NUL )
      ELSE
        ARGC @ 1+ ARGC !
      THEN
    ELSE
      REDIR-TARGET-WORD? @ IF NOSPLIT-ON THEN
      EW-NODES EW-I @ CELLS + @ ENC-EXPAND-CALL
      NOSPLIT-OFF
      TOK-END @ TOK-POS !
      0 EMIT-TOK-CHAR
    THEN
    BEGIN
      TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < IF TOK-POS @ C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE
      TOK-POS @ 1+ TOK-POS !
    REPEAT
    TOK-POS @ TOK-END @ < 0=
    UNTIL
    THEN
    \ An assignment prefix is applied as soon as it is expanded, so the
    \ next one sees it: `X=1.2 X=${X#1} B=${X%%.*}` sets B (XCU 2.9.1,
    \ Iteration 357). The command's own words must still see the values
    \ from before the command, so everything applied here is put back
    \ when the run of prefixes ends.
    EW-ASSIGN EW-I @ + C@ EWA-RUN? @ AND IF
      ARGC @ 0 > IF ARGV ARGC @ 1- CELLS + @ EWA-APPLY THEN
    ELSE
      EWA-RUN? @ IF EWA-UNDO THEN
    THEN
    EW-I @ 1+ EW-I !
  REPEAT
  EWA-RUN? @ IF EWA-UNDO THEN
  0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
  \ The marks are recorded even when pathname expansion is off, since a
  \ `case` pattern needs them; the decision to expand against the file
  \ system is taken here instead (Iteration 340).
  GLOB-META# @ NO-GLOB? @ 0= AND OPT-NOGLOB @ 0= AND IF GLOB-FIELDS-CALL THEN ;

: ARGV-QUOTED@ ( i --- f )  ARGV-QUOTED + C@ ;
: ARGV-NAME-QUOTED@ ( i --- f )  ARGV-NAME-QUOTED + C@ ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Redirection: <, >, >>
\ Scans the tokenized ARGV for these operators; each one found records
\ the filename token that follows it into a dedicated variable, and
\ both tokens are excluded from the argv the command itself will see.
\ Built as a fresh copy into a second array (ARGV2/ARGC2) rather than
\ shifting ARGV in place - an earlier attempt at this (see PROGRESS.md,
\ Iteration 5) got tangled trying to do in-place index shifting with
\ unverified stack tricks; a plain copy-excluding-some-entries pass is
\ far harder to get wrong, at the cost of one extra small buffer.
\ At most one of each operator is honored per command line (a second
\ ">" just overwrites the first's recorded filename); combining
\ multiple output redirections isn't meaningful anyway without pipes.
\ ==================================================================

1 CELLS ARGS-BUFFER: ARGV2
VARIABLE ARGC2
\ A general list of redirections rather than one slot per operator.
\ The old shape had a fixed REDIR-IN/OUT/APPEND-FILE, which cannot
\ express "2>file" (a redirection on a file descriptor other than the
\ default) or "2>&1" (duplicating one descriptor onto another) at all.
\ Each entry is a target fd, an operation, and either a filename or a
\ source fd. They are applied in order, which matters: "> f 2>&1"
\ sends both streams to the file, while "2>&1 > f" does not.

\ READ-NEXT-INPUT-LINE is defined much further down (it needs the
\ script-file and replay machinery), but here-documents have to read
\ input at redirection-parsing time. Same deferred-word pattern used
\ for RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL and friends.

8192 CONSTANT HEREDOC-MAX   \ where the body buffer starts; it grows
HEREDOC-MAX BUFFER: HEREDOC-BUF
VARIABLE HEREDOC-LEN
8 CONSTANT MAX-HEREDOCS   \ per command; more are ignored
MAX-HEREDOCS CELLS BUFFER: HD-OFFS
MAX-HEREDOCS CELLS BUFFER: HD-LENS
VARIABLE HD-N        \ how many this command has prepared
VARIABLE HD-POOL#    \ where the next one goes in HEREDOC-BUF
VARIABLE HD-USE      \ which one the next redirection takes
VARIABLE HD-ADDR     \ where the one being written starts
VARIABLE HD-RD
VARIABLE HD-WR

1 CELLS ARGS-BUFFER: REDIR-FD
1 CELLS ARGS-BUFFER: REDIR-OP
1 CELLS ARGS-BUFFER: REDIR-TGT
VARIABLE REDIR-N
VARIABLE PR-I     \ shared scan cursor, declared as a local by each scanner
VARIABLE RA-FD
VARIABLE RA-OP
VARIABLE AR-I

: DIGIT-TOKEN? ( i --- f )   \ a one-character token that is a digit
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN 1 = SWAP C@ AE-DIGIT? AND ;

: REDIR-OP-AT ( i --- op | 0 )   \ 1 "<"  2 ">"  3 ">>"  4 ">&"  5 "<&"
  DUP ARGV-QUOTED@ IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN
  2DUP S" <"  STR= IF 2DROP 1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" >"  STR= IF 2DROP 2 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" >>" STR= IF 2DROP 3 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" >&" STR= IF 2DROP 4 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" <&" STR= IF 2DROP 5 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" <<" STR= IF 2DROP 6 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" <<-" STR= IF 2DROP 7 EXIT THEN
  2DUP S" <>"  STR= IF 2DROP 8 EXIT THEN      \ reading and writing
       S" >|"  STR= IF 9 EXIT THEN            \ truncate whatever noclobber says
  0 ;

: REDIR-ADD ( fd op --- )
  \ Records one redirection, consuming its target token. Reads PR-I,
  \ which is a local of PARSE-REDIRECTIONS - safe because a local IS
  \ the variable, so it holds that invocation's value throughout.
  SHADOW{ RA-FD RA-OP }
  PR-I @ ARGC @ < 0= IF EXIT THEN
  RA-FD @      REDIR-FD  REDIR-N @ CELLS + !
  RA-OP @      REDIR-OP  REDIR-N @ CELLS + !
  PR-I @ ARGV@ REDIR-TGT REDIR-N @ CELLS + !
  REDIR-N @ 1+ REDIR-N !
  PR-I @ 1+ PR-I ! ;

: PARSE-REDIRECTIONS ( --- )
  \ Pulls redirections out of ARGV into the list above; what remains
  \ is what the command itself sees. A leading digit attached to an
  \ operator ("2>file") arrives as its own token, so the digit and the
  \ operator are recognised as a pair here rather than in the
  \ tokenizer.
  SHADOW{ | PR-I }
  0 REDIR-N !
  0 ARGC2 !
  BEGIN PR-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    PR-I @ DIGIT-TOKEN? PR-I @ 1+ ARGC @ < AND
    IF PR-I @ 1+ REDIR-OP-AT ELSE 0 THEN
    ?DUP IF
      PR-I @ ARGV@ C@ 48 - SWAP        ( fd op )
      PR-I @ 2 + PR-I !
      REDIR-ADD
    ELSE
      PR-I @ REDIR-OP-AT ?DUP IF
        \ which descriptor it defaults to: `>`, `>>`, `>&` and `>|` write
        DUP 2 = OVER 3 = OR OVER 4 = OR OVER 9 = OR IF 1 ELSE 0 THEN SWAP
        PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
        REDIR-ADD
      ELSE
        PR-I @ ARGV@ ARGV2 ARGC2 @ CELLS + !
        ARGC2 @ 1+ ARGC2 !
        PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
      THEN
    THEN
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV2 ARGC2 @ CELLS + !
  0 PR-I !
  BEGIN PR-I @ ARGC2 @ < WHILE
    ARGV2 PR-I @ CELLS + @  ARGV PR-I @ CELLS + !
    PR-I @ 1+ PR-I !
  REPEAT
  0 ARGV ARGC2 @ CELLS + !
  ARGC2 @ ARGC ! ;

: OPEN-FOR ( c-addr fam --- fid ok? )
  SWAP DUP CSTRLEN ROT OPEN-FILE 0= ;

VARIABLE REDIR-FAILED?   \ set by APPLY-REDIRECTIONS when an open fails
VARIABLE HD-WRITER       \ a here-document writer to reap, or 0

: OPEN-OR-CREATE ( c-addr fam --- fid ok? )
  \ `<>` names a file that need not exist yet; every other form either
  \ creates it itself (W/O, A/O) or has to find it (R/O).
  2DUP OPEN-FOR                              ( c-addr fam fid ok? )
  IF >R 2DROP R> -1 EXIT THEN                ( c-addr fam fid )
  DROP                                       ( c-addr fam )
  R/W = IF
    DUP CSTRLEN R/W CREATE-FILE 0=
  ELSE
    DROP 0 0
  THEN ;

: APPLY-REDIRECTIONS ( --- )
  \ Runs in the forked child, before RUN-CHILD/EXECVE. A redirection
  \ that fails to open is skipped rather than aborting the command -
  \ no error reporting yet, matching this file's sparse error handling
  \ elsewhere.
  0 AR-I !  0 REDIR-FAILED? !
  BEGIN AR-I @ REDIR-N @ < WHILE
    REDIR-OP AR-I @ CELLS + @
    DUP 6 = OVER 7 = OR IF
      \ Feed the body through a pipe rather than a temporary file. A
      \ small body is written here, before anything reads, which only
      \ works while it fits the pipe; POSIX promises 4 KB. A larger one
      \ is written by a child of its own, as dash does, so nothing waits
      \ on a reader that does not exist yet. Until Iteration 251 bodies
      \ were cut at 8 KB, so the direct write was always enough; when
      \ they grew, a 100 KB body hung the shell for ever.
      DROP
      PIPE DROP HD-WR ! HD-RD !
      \ each here-document in turn, in the order they were prepared
      HD-USE @ HD-N @ < IF
        HD-OFFS HD-USE @ CELLS + @ HEREDOC-BUF + HD-ADDR !
        HD-LENS HD-USE @ CELLS + @ HEREDOC-LEN !
        1 HD-USE +!
      ELSE
        HEREDOC-BUF HD-ADDR !
      THEN
      HEREDOC-LEN @ 4096 > IF
        FORK DUP 0= IF
          DROP
          HD-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
          HD-ADDR @ HEREDOC-LEN @ HD-WR @ WRITE-FILE DROP
          0 SYS-EXIT
        THEN
        HD-WRITER !   \ reaped by END-REDIRECT when a builtin read it
      ELSE
        HD-ADDR @ HEREDOC-LEN @ HD-WR @ WRITE-FILE DROP
      THEN
      HD-WR @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      \ The pipe's read end can land ON the descriptor being redirected -
      \ `cat 3<<E` gets fd 3 for the read end, since 3 is the lowest
      \ free one - and then the copy is a no-op and the close below
      \ destroys it. Same shape as `exec 3>file` in Iteration 297
      \ (Iteration 355).
      HD-RD @ REDIR-FD AR-I @ CELLS + @ = IF
        0 REDIR-FAILED? !
      ELSE
        HD-RD @ REDIR-FD AR-I @ CELLS + @ DUP2 DROP
        HD-RD @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      THEN
    ELSE
    DUP 4 = OVER 5 = OR IF
      \ "2>&1": duplicate the named source descriptor onto the target.
      DROP
      \ `>&-` and `<&-` close it (XCU 2.7.6). The target was read as a
      \ number, and PARSE-DECIMAL of "-" is 0, so `exec 3>&-` made fd 3 a
      \ copy of STDIN and left it open for good (Iteration 296).
      REDIR-TGT AR-I @ CELLS + @ DUP C@ 45 = IF
        DROP  REDIR-FD AR-I @ CELLS + @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
      ELSE
        \ Duplicating from a descriptor that is not open fails, and the
        \ command must not run (XCU 2.7.6); the result used to be dropped,
        \ so `echo x >&3` with 3 closed quietly wrote to stdout instead.
        \ Duplicating from a descriptor that is not open fails, and the
        \ command must not run (XCU 2.7.6). 296 tried this and had to
        \ revert it: `exec 3>file` did not really keep fd 3, so the very
        \ next command looked wrong. With that fixed the check stands.
        DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL
        REDIR-FD  AR-I @ CELLS + @ DUP2 IF
          S" shell: " ERR-TYPE
          REDIR-TGT AR-I @ CELLS + @ ERR-CSTR
          S" : bad file descriptor" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
          -1 REDIR-FAILED? !
        THEN
      THEN
    ELSE
      \ set -C: `>` refuses a file that is there, but only a REGULAR one -
      \ `> /dev/null` must still work, which is why this needs FILE-KIND
      \ rather than O_EXCL alone (XCU 2.7.2, Iteration 307).
      0 RD-SKIP? !
      DUP 2 = OPT-NOCLOBBER @ AND IF
        REDIR-TGT AR-I @ CELLS + @ FILE-KIND 1 = IF
          S" shell: cannot create " ERR-TYPE
          REDIR-TGT AR-I @ CELLS + @ ERR-CSTR S" : file exists" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
          -1 REDIR-FAILED? !  -1 RD-SKIP? !
        THEN
      THEN
      \ `<>` (op 8) opens for both, creating the file if it is not there
      \ and truncating nothing (XCU 2.7.1); it used to be handed to the
      \ shell as a plain `<`, so it failed on a file that did not exist.
      \ X/O (the engine's exclusive create, added in Iteration 305) is
      \ what `set -C` will use once a stat primitive can tell a regular
      \ file from a device: O_EXCL alone also refuses `> /dev/null`,
      \ which both references allow.
      DUP 8 = IF DROP R/W ELSE DUP 1 = IF DROP R/O ELSE 3 = IF A/O ELSE W/O THEN THEN THEN
      \ refused by noclobber: nothing to open, and the message is already out
      RD-SKIP? @ IF DROP -1 ELSE
        REDIR-TGT AR-I @ CELLS + @ SWAP OPEN-OR-CREATE
      THEN
      IF
        \ The open may land ON the target: 3 is the lowest free
        \ descriptor when the target is 3. Copying it onto itself does
        \ nothing and the close below then shut it, which is why
        \ `exec 3>file` never persisted while `exec >file` did
        \ (Iteration 297).
        RD-SKIP? @ 0= IF
          REDIR-FD AR-I @ CELLS + @              ( fd target )
          2DUP = IF
            2DROP
          ELSE
            2DUP DUP2 DROP  DROP CLOSE-FILE DROP
          THEN
        THEN
      ELSE
        \ Reported, and the command is not run (Iteration 255). It used
        \ to be skipped silently, so `read x < missing` read the shell's
        \ own input and `cmd > /no/such/dir/f` wrote to the terminal.
        DROP
        S" shell: cannot open " ERR-TYPE
        REDIR-TGT AR-I @ CELLS + @ ERR-CSTR ERR-NL
        -1 REDIR-FAILED? !
      THEN
    THEN
    THEN
    AR-I @ 1+ AR-I !
  REPEAT ;

\ ==================================================================
\ PATH search + exec
\ ==================================================================

: HAS-SLASH? ( c-addr u --- f )
  \ u can be 0 in principle (an empty ARGV[0], however unlikely in
  \ practice) - guarded explicitly, same reasoning as EXPAND-CMDSUB's
  \ splice loop: this kernel's DO/LOOP doesn't treat start=limit as
  \ zero iterations.
  DUP 0 > IF
    0 DO DUP I + C@ 47 = IF UNLOOP DROP -1 EXIT THEN LOOP
    DROP 0
  ELSE
    2DROP 0
  THEN ;

VARIABLE PATH-CUR
VARIABLE PATH-END2

\ Try one PATH directory (c-addr u, no trailing slash) against ARGV[0].
\ Builds "dir/cmd" into PATHBUF and attempts EXECVE. Only returns if
\ that attempt failed.
\ The first exec failure that was not "no such file": a command that was
\ found but could not run is status 126 and says why; one that was not
\ found is 127 (XCU 2.9.1.1; Iteration 265). -2 ENOENT, -20 ENOTDIR.
VARIABLE EXEC-ERR
: NOTE-EXEC-ERR ( ior --- )
  DUP -2 = OVER -20 = OR EXEC-ERR @ OR IF DROP ELSE EXEC-ERR ! THEN ;


\ ---- where external commands are (Iteration 269) ----
\ dash finds a command along PATH once and remembers it; this shell
\ tried execve in each directory, every time - about 70 microseconds on a
\ nine-entry PATH (DASH-COMPARISON.md). The parent now looks the name up
\ before forking, with access(2), and keeps name -> path; the entries are
\ dropped whenever PATH is not what they were found with. The child
\ execs the remembered path, and falls back to the search if that fails.
256 BUFFER: CC-TEXT        \ entries: name NUL path NUL, one after another
VARIABLE CC#
256 BUFFER: CC-PATHVAL     \ the PATH the entries were found with
VARIABLE CC-PATHLEN
VARIABLE CCF-NAME
VARIABLE CCF-LEN
VARIABLE CMD-RESOLVED      \ the path found for this command, or 0

: CC-CURRENT-PATH ( --- c-addr u )
  S" PATH" ENVNAMBUF SWAP MOVE  0 ENVNAMBUF 4 + C!
  ENVNAMBUF GETENV DUP IF DUP CSTRLEN ELSE 0 THEN ;

: CC-CHECK ( --- )   \ forget the entries if PATH changed
  CC-CURRENT-PATH 2DUP CC-PATHVAL CC-PATHLEN @ COMPARE IF
    DUP 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER CC-PATHVAL
    DUP CC-PATHLEN !  CC-PATHVAL SWAP MOVE  0 CC# !
  ELSE 2DROP THEN ;

: CC-FIND ( name --- path | 0 )
  DUP CCF-NAME !  CSTRLEN CCF-LEN !
  0 BEGIN DUP CC# @ < WHILE                  ( off )
    DUP CC-TEXT + DUP CSTRLEN                ( off e elen )
    2DUP CCF-NAME @ CCF-LEN @ COMPARE 0= IF
      + 1+ NIP EXIT                          ( path )
    THEN
    + 1+ DUP CSTRLEN + 1+ CC-TEXT - NIP      ( off' )
  REPEAT DROP 0 ;

: CC-ADD ( name path --- )   \ both NUL-terminated
  OVER CSTRLEN OVER CSTRLEN + 2 + CC# @ + ENSURE-BUFFER CC-TEXT
  SWAP DUP CSTRLEN 1+ >R  CC-TEXT CC# @ + R@ MOVE  R> CC# +!
  DUP CSTRLEN 1+ >R  CC-TEXT CC# @ + R@ MOVE  R> CC# +! ;

VARIABLE LC-ANY?   \ look for any file, not only an executable one
: EXECUTABLE-FILE? ( c-addr --- f )   \ executable (or, with LC-ANY?, existing), not a directory
  DUP LC-ANY? @ IF 0 ELSE 1 THEN ACCESS IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  OPEN-DIR ?DUP IF CLOSE-DIR 0 ELSE -1 THEN ;

VARIABLE LC-AT
VARIABLE LC-END
: LOCATE-COMMAND ( name --- path | 0 )
  CC-CHECK
  DUP CC-FIND ?DUP IF NIP EXIT THEN
  CC-PATHVAL LC-AT !  CC-PATHVAL CC-PATHLEN @ + LC-END !
  BEGIN LC-AT @ LC-END @ < WHILE
    LC-AT @                                   ( name dir )
    BEGIN LC-AT @ LC-END @ < IF LC-AT @ C@ 58 <> ELSE 0 THEN WHILE 1 LC-AT +! REPEAT
    LC-AT @ OVER -                            ( name dir len )
    DUP IF
      \ Grown to fit first: B-CHAR has no bound, and a command name of 250
      \ characters ran PATHBUF's 256 bytes into CWDBUF and on (Iteration 421,
      \ found by tools/crashfuzz.py).
      2 PICK CSTRLEN OVER + 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER PATHBUF
      PATHBUF B-RESET B-STR 47 B-CHAR DUP B-CSTR B-FINISH
      PATHBUF EXECUTABLE-FILE? IF
        DUP PATHBUF CC-ADD  CC-FIND EXIT
      THEN
    ELSE 2DROP THEN
    LC-AT @ LC-END @ < IF 1 LC-AT +! THEN
  REPEAT
  DROP 0 ;

VARIABLE UNDO-BASE   64 UNDO-BASE !   \ next spare descriptor

: CLOSE-SAVED-FDS ( --- )
  \ The copies a redirection keeps so it can put descriptors back are
  \ the shell's business, not the command's: `find /proc/self/fd` in a
  \ redirected command listed fd 64 as well as its own (Iteration 372).
  \ Real shells mark them close-on-exec; this shell has no fcntl, so
  \ the child closes the range before it execs.
  \ Everything above 9 goes: the saved copies from 64 up, and the
  \ script's own descriptor, which this shell keeps at 63 and which a
  \ redirected `ls /proc/self/fd` could otherwise see. Descriptors 0 to
  \ 9 are the script's to use and are left alone.
  UNDO-BASE @ 10 ?DO I CLOSE-FILE DROP LOOP ;

: RUN-CHILD ( --- )  \ resolve ARGV[0] and exec it; exits 126 or 127 if it cannot
  CLOSE-SAVED-FDS
  0 EXEC-ERR !
  CMD-RESOLVED @ ?DUP IF ARGV SWAP EXECVE NOTE-EXEC-ERR THEN
  ARGV @ DUP CSTRLEN HAS-SLASH? IF
    ARGV ARGV @ EXECVE NOTE-EXEC-ERR
  ELSE
    \ No remembered path, or it failed: look again, here. An executable
    \ first; failing that, any file, so that one found but not runnable
    \ is 126 with its reason (bash; a directory is skipped, as bash and
    \ dash both do).
    0 CC# !  0 LC-ANY? !
    ARGV @ LOCATE-COMMAND ?DUP IF ARGV SWAP EXECVE NOTE-EXEC-ERR THEN
    EXEC-ERR @ 0= IF
      0 CC# !  -1 LC-ANY? !
      ARGV @ LOCATE-COMMAND ?DUP IF ARGV SWAP EXECVE NOTE-EXEC-ERR THEN
    THEN
  THEN
  \ Reached only when the exec failed, which every caller follows with
  \ `127 SYS-EXIT`. Until Iteration 154 nothing was printed at all:
  \ `relfsh -c nosuchcommand` exited 127 with BOTH streams empty,
  \ where every reference shell reports on stderr. That silence is
  \ why Iteration 152's status-127 bug looked like correct output -
  \ the stray `{` segment was being run as a command name and failing
  \ without a word.
  \
  \ On fd 2 via ERR-*, not `."`: this runs in the forked child, whose
  \ stdout may be a pipe belonging to the next stage of a pipeline,
  \ and a diagnostic must not become that stage's input.
  S" shell: " ERR-TYPE
  ARGV @ ERR-CSTR
  EXEC-ERR @ ?DUP 0= IF
    S" : command not found" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 127 SYS-EXIT
  THEN
  DUP -7 = IF S" : argument list too long" ERR-TYPE ELSE
  DUP -13 = IF S" : permission denied" ERR-TYPE ELSE
    S" : cannot execute" ERR-TYPE
  THEN THEN DROP
  ERR-NL 126 SYS-EXIT ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Builtins - these must run in the shell's own process (not a forked
\ child), since their whole point is to change *this* process's own
\ state (cwd, environment) in a way a child's changes couldn't
\ propagate back from.
\ ==================================================================

CREATE HOMEBUF-NM  CSTR," HOME"
CREATE OLDPWD-NM  CSTR," OLDPWD"
CREATE PWD-NM     CSTR," PWD"
CREATE CDPATH-NM  CSTR," CDPATH"


: CD-FAILED ( name --- )
  S" shell: cd: " ERR-TYPE ERR-CSTR S" : no such directory" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  1 LAST-STATUS ! ;

\ ---- cd, with POSIX's logical path (Iteration 319) ----
\ Until now `cd` was `chdir` plus `getcwd`, so PWD held the resolved
\ path: `cd /tmp/link` left PWD at /tmp/real where both references keep
\ /tmp/link. POSIX builds the path from PWD and the operand, canonicalises
\ it by TEXT - "." dropped, ".." removing the component before it, without
\ resolving symlinks - and only then changes directory. `-P` asks for the
\ old behaviour; `-L` is the default.

PATH-MAX BUFFER: CD-PATH        \ the logical path being built
PATH-MAX BUFFER: CD-TMP         \ CD-CANON writes into CD-PATH, so its input
                                \ cannot be CD-PATH itself
VARIABLE CD-LEN
VARIABLE CD-PHYS?               \ -P was given
VARIABLE CD-I
VARIABLE CD-SEG                 \ where the current component starts
VARIABLE CD-PRINT?              \ the destination came from CDPATH
VARIABLE CD-FROM-DOT?           \ ... from an empty entry, which means "."

: CD-PUSH ( c --- )
  CD-LEN @ PATH-MAX 2 - < IF
    CD-PATH CD-LEN @ + C!  1 CD-LEN +!
  ELSE DROP THEN ;

: CD-PUSH-STR ( c-addr --- )
  BEGIN DUP C@ ?DUP WHILE CD-PUSH 1+ REPEAT DROP ;

: CD-DROP-COMPONENT ( --- )   \ back over the last component, and its slash
  BEGIN CD-LEN @ 0 > IF CD-PATH CD-LEN @ 1- + C@ 47 <> ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE -1 CD-LEN +! REPEAT
  CD-LEN @ 1 > IF -1 CD-LEN +! THEN ;


: CD-END-COMPONENT ( --- )   \ fold "." and ".." as POSIX says, lexically
  CD-LEN @ CD-SEG @ = IF EXIT THEN                \ empty: a repeated slash
  CD-LEN @ CD-SEG @ - 1 = CD-PATH CD-SEG @ + C@ 46 = AND IF
    CD-SEG @ CD-LEN !  EXIT                       \ "."
  THEN
  CD-LEN @ CD-SEG @ - 2 = IF
    CD-PATH CD-SEG @ + C@ 46 = CD-PATH CD-SEG @ + 1+ C@ 46 = AND IF
      CD-SEG @ CD-LEN !                           \ ".."
      CD-LEN @ 1 > IF -1 CD-LEN +! THEN           \ its slash
      CD-DROP-COMPONENT
      EXIT
    THEN
  THEN ;

: CD-CANON ( c-addr --- )   \ build CD-PATH from PWD and the operand
  0 CD-LEN !
  DUP C@ 47 = IF
    47 CD-PUSH
  ELSE
    PWD-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP IF CD-PUSH-STR ELSE
      CWDBUF PATH-MAX GETCWD DROP 0 CWDBUF ROT + C! CWDBUF CD-PUSH-STR
    THEN
    CD-LEN @ 0= IF 47 CD-PUSH THEN
    CD-PATH CD-LEN @ 1- + C@ 47 <> IF 47 CD-PUSH THEN
  THEN
  CD-LEN @ CD-SEG !
  BEGIN DUP C@ ?DUP WHILE
    DUP 47 = IF
      DROP CD-END-COMPONENT
      CD-LEN @ 0 > IF CD-PATH CD-LEN @ 1- + C@ 47 <> IF 47 CD-PUSH THEN THEN
      CD-LEN @ CD-SEG !
    ELSE
      CD-PUSH
    THEN
    1+
  REPEAT DROP
  CD-END-COMPONENT
  CD-LEN @ 0= IF 47 CD-PUSH THEN
  CD-LEN @ 1 > IF
    CD-PATH CD-LEN @ 1- + C@ 47 = IF -1 CD-LEN +! THEN   \ no trailing slash
  THEN
  0 CD-PATH CD-LEN @ + C! ;

: CD-SET-PWD ( --- )   \ PWD and OLDPWD after a successful move
  PWD-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP IF OLDPWD-NM SWAP SET-SHVAR THEN
  CD-PHYS? @ IF
    CWDBUF PATH-MAX GETCWD DROP  0 CWDBUF ROT + C!
    PWD-NM CWDBUF SET-SHVAR
  ELSE
    PWD-NM CD-PATH SET-SHVAR
  THEN ;

: CD-TO ( c-addr --- f )   \ try it; -1 if the shell moved
  CD-PHYS? @ IF
    DUP CHDIR 0= IF DROP CD-SET-PWD -1 ELSE DROP 0 THEN
  ELSE
    DUP CD-CANON DROP
    CD-PATH CHDIR 0= IF CD-SET-PWD -1 ELSE 0 THEN
  THEN ;

: CD-DOT-PREFIX? ( c-addr --- f )   \ starts with `./` or `../`
  DUP C@ 46 = 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 1+ C@ 47 = IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 1+ C@ 46 = IF 2 + C@ 47 = EXIT THEN
  DROP 0 ;

: CD-TRY-CDPATH ( c-addr --- f )   \ each CDPATH entry in turn (XCU cd)
  DUP C@ 47 = IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN                 \ absolute: not a search
  \ ... and neither is an operand that begins with `./` or `../`: XCU
  \ `cd` step 5 says CDPATH is not used for those, and this shell was
  \ searching it, so `cd ./dev` could land somewhere else entirely
  \ (Iteration 381).
  DUP CD-DOT-PREFIX? IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  CDPATH-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  SWAP >R                                          \ the operand goes on R
  BEGIN DUP C@ WHILE
    0 CD-LEN !
    BEGIN                                        \ up to the next ':'
      DUP C@ DUP 0<> SWAP 58 <> AND
    WHILE
      DUP C@ CD-PUSH 1+
    REPEAT
    \ An empty entry means the current directory, and a destination
    \ found there is NOT announced - XCU `cd` prints only when the
    \ operand was found somewhere else (Iteration 381).
    CD-LEN @ 0= IF 46 CD-PUSH  -1 CD-FROM-DOT? !
                ELSE 0 CD-FROM-DOT? ! THEN
    CD-PATH CD-LEN @ 1- + C@ 47 <> IF 47 CD-PUSH THEN
    R@ CD-PUSH-STR  0 CD-PATH CD-LEN @ + C!
    CD-PATH CHDIR 0= IF
      R> DROP  DROP                            \ the operand and the entry list
      CD-FROM-DOT? @ 0= CD-PRINT? !
      CD-PHYS? @ 0= IF
        CD-PATH CD-TMP CD-LEN @ 1+ MOVE  CD-TMP CD-CANON
      THEN
      CD-SET-PWD  -1 EXIT
    THEN
    DUP C@ 58 = IF 1+ THEN
  REPEAT
  DROP R> DROP 0 ;

: DO-CD ( --- )
  0 CD-PHYS? !  0 CD-PRINT? !
  1 CD-I !
  BEGIN
    CD-I @ ARGC @ < IF CD-I @ ARGV@ C@ 45 = CD-I @ ARGV@ 1+ C@ 0<> AND ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    CD-I @ ARGV@ 1+ C@ 80 = IF -1 CD-PHYS? ! THEN   \ -P
    CD-I @ 1+ CD-I !
  REPEAT
  CD-I @ ARGC @ < IF
    CD-I @ ARGV@ S" -" ROT STR0= IF                 \ `cd -`
      OLDPWD-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP 0= IF
        S" shell: cd: OLDPWD not set" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL  1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
      THEN
      DUP CD-TO IF
        DROP 0 LAST-STATUS !
        PWD-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP IF DUP CSTRLEN TYPE CR THEN
      ELSE CD-FAILED THEN
      EXIT
    THEN
    CD-I @ ARGV@ DUP CD-TRY-CDPATH IF
      DROP 0 LAST-STATUS !
      \ Announced only when the operand was found somewhere other than
      \ the current directory: an empty CDPATH entry means `.`, and
      \ `cd dev` through one is as quiet as a plain `cd dev`
      \ (Iteration 381).
      CD-PRINT? @ IF
        PWD-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP IF DUP CSTRLEN TYPE CR THEN
      THEN
      EXIT
    THEN
    DUP CD-TO IF DROP 0 LAST-STATUS ! ELSE CD-FAILED THEN
  ELSE
    HOMEBUF-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP 0= IF
      S" shell: cd: HOME not set" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL  1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
    THEN
    DUP CD-TO IF DROP 0 LAST-STATUS ! ELSE CD-FAILED THEN
  THEN ;

: DO-PWD ( --- )
  \ `pwd` prints the LOGICAL path - $PWD - unless -P asks for the
  \ resolved one (XCU pwd). It always resolved until Iteration 319.
  0 CD-PHYS? !
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    1 ARGV@ 1+ C@ 80 = IF -1 CD-PHYS? ! THEN
  THEN
  CD-PHYS? @ 0= IF
    PWD-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP IF
      DUP C@ 47 = IF DUP CSTRLEN TYPE CR 0 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
      DROP
    THEN
  THEN
  CWDBUF PATH-MAX GETCWD DROP CWDBUF SWAP TYPE CR
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

VARIABLE EX-I

: .EXPORT-LINE ( c-addr --- )   \ NAME=VALUE printed as `export NAME='VALUE'`
  S" export " TYPE
  BEGIN DUP C@ DUP 0<> SWAP 61 <> AND WHILE DUP C@ EMIT 1+ REPEAT
  DUP C@ 61 = IF
    1+ [CHAR] = EMIT [CHAR] ' EMIT
    BEGIN DUP C@ ?DUP WHILE
      \ a quote inside the value, closed and reopened as dash does it
      DUP 39 = IF DROP S" '" TYPE 34 EMIT 39 EMIT 34 EMIT S" '" TYPE ELSE EMIT THEN  1+
    REPEAT
    [CHAR] ' EMIT
  THEN
  DROP CR ;

: EXPORT-LIST ( --- )   \ everything in the environment, as the shell reads it
  0 EX-I !
  BEGIN EX-I @ ENV-AT ?DUP WHILE
    .EXPORT-LINE
    EX-I @ 1+ EX-I !
  REPEAT
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: EXPORT-ONE ( c-addr --- )   \ one operand: NAME=value, or NAME
  DUP CSTRLEN 0 ?DO
    DUP I + C@ 61 = IF               \ NAME=value: split at the '=' in place
      DUP I + 0 SWAP C!
      DUP I CHARS + CHAR+            ( name value )
      2DUP SET-SHVAR
      SWAP SETENV DROP
      UNLOOP EXIT
    THEN
  LOOP
  \ A bare NAME exports the value it has. One exported while it has none
  \ is not remembered yet: this shell's "exported" is "in the process
  \ environment", and there is nothing to put there.
  DUP GET-SHVAR ?DUP IF SWAP SETENV DROP ELSE DROP THEN ;

: DO-EXPORT ( --- )
  \ EVERY operand. This handled ARGV[1] and ignored the rest, so
  \ `export a=A b c=C` set a and left b unexported and c unassigned -
  \ found by yash's export-p.tst (Iteration 422).
  ARGC @ 1 = IF EXPORT-LIST EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 2 = IF S" -p" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF EXPORT-LIST EXIT THEN THEN
  ARGC @ 1 ?DO I ARGV@ EXPORT-ONE LOOP
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

VARIABLE UN-I
VARIABLE UN-FUNCS?
DEFER REMOVE-FUNC-CALL   ( name-addr --- )   \ the function table is below
DEFER FT-SHIFT-CALL      ( i --- i )   \ tree.4 moves its own row

: DO-UNSET ( --- )
  \ unset NAME - removes an exported environment variable and, if
  \ present, a shell-local one (REMOVE-SHVAR) too. Silently does
  \ nothing if given no argument or a name that was never set
  \ (matching real shells: unsetting an already-unset name isn't an
  \ error).
  \ `-f` removes a function, which it never did until Iteration 326, and
  \ a readonly variable cannot be unset at all (XCU unset).
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ARGC @ 1 = IF EXIT THEN
  1 UN-I !  0 UN-FUNCS? !
  S" -f" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF -1 UN-FUNCS? !  2 UN-I ! THEN
  S" -v" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF 2 UN-I ! THEN
  BEGIN UN-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    UN-FUNCS? @ IF
      UN-I @ ARGV@ REMOVE-FUNC-CALL
    ELSE
      UN-I @ ARGV@ FIND-SHVAR DUP 0< 0= IF
        DUP SHVAR-RO + C@ IF
          DROP
          S" shell: unset: " ERR-TYPE UN-I @ ARGV@ ERR-CSTR
          S" : is read only" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL  1 SPECIAL-ERROR
        ELSE
          DROP UN-I @ ARGV@ DUP REMOVE-SHVAR UNSETENV DROP
        THEN
      ELSE
        DROP UN-I @ ARGV@ DUP REMOVE-SHVAR UNSETENV DROP
      THEN
    THEN
    UN-I @ 1+ UN-I !
  REPEAT ;

16 CONSTANT MAX-FUNCS           \ the table's FIRST size; it doubles when full
64 CONSTANT FUNC-NAME-MAX
VARIABLE FUNC-CAP                \ how many rows the arrays hold now
MAX-FUNCS FUNC-CAP !
DEFER FUNC-GROW-CALL   ( cap --- )   \ tree.4's three arrays, grown alongside

MAX-FUNCS FUNC-NAME-MAX * BUFFER: FUNC-NAMES
VARIABLE FUNC-COUNT

: FUNC-NAME-SLOT ( i --- addr )  FUNC-NAME-MAX * FUNC-NAMES + ;

VARIABLE FF-NAME
VARIABLE FF-I

: FIND-FUNC ( name-addr --- i | -1 )  \ linear scan, matching
                                        \ FIND-SHVAR's own style.
                                        \ Declared here, well before
                                        \ SET-FUNC/RUN-FUNC-BODY define
                                        \ the rest of a function's own
                                        \ storage (a function's body
                                        \ can be arbitrarily large, so
                                        \ that part of the table lives
                                        \ down near RUN-STORED-LINE
                                        \ instead), since DISPATCH
                                        \ needs FIND-FUNC to check
                                        \ whether a command name is
                                        \ actually a defined function
                                        \ before falling through to a
                                        \ builtin or external command.
  SHADOW{ FF-NAME | FF-I }
  BEGIN FF-I @ FUNC-COUNT @ < WHILE
    FF-I @ FUNC-NAME-SLOT FF-NAME @ CSTR= IF FF-I @ EXIT THEN
    FF-I @ 1+ FF-I !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

DEFER RUN-FUNC-BODY-CALL   \ patched to RUN-FUNC-BODY's own xt right
                              \ after it's defined, much further down -
                              \ the same deferred-word pattern already
                              \ used for RUN-TOKENIZED-CALL, needed
                              \ here for the same reason: DISPATCH is
                              \ defined long before a function's own
                              \ body-replay logic is.

\ ==================================================================
\ test / [ - string, numeric, and (limited) file-existence tests.
\ Scope limits, all deliberate: no -f/-d/-r/-w/-x/-s (would need a
\ real stat/access primitive this kernel doesn't expose, only
\ OPEN-FILE - -e is approximated by attempting to open the path
\ read-only, which can't distinguish a file from a directory); no
\ -a/-o (deprecated in POSIX anyway); no "(" ")" grouping; a 3-arg
\ form is always "STRING1 OP STRING2" and a 4-arg form is always
\ "! STRING1 OP STRING2" - "! -z STRING"-style 3-arg negated unary
\ tests, and 5+ argument forms generally, aren't handled.
\ ==================================================================

VARIABLE TEST-START
VARIABLE TEST-END

: TEST-NARGS ( --- n )  TEST-END @ TEST-START @ - ;
: TEST-ARGN ( i --- c-addr )  TEST-START @ + ARGV@ ;

VARIABLE TEST-NUM-BAD?          \ an operand to -eq and friends was not a number
VARIABLE TPI-NEG
VARIABLE TPI-N
VARIABLE TPI-DIGITS

: TEST-BLANKS ( c-addr --- c-addr' )   \ past spaces and tabs
  BEGIN DUP C@ DUP 32 = SWAP 9 = OR WHILE 1+ REPEAT ;

: TEST-PARSE-INT ( c-addr --- n )
  \ An integer operand is blanks, an optional sign, at least one digit,
  \ and blanks - and nothing else. Anything else is an error, and the
  \ comparison's status is 2, as dash has it.
  \
  \ This read the empty string, and anything that was not a number, as
  \ 0: `[ "" -eq 0 ]` was TRUE, so the Advanced Bash-Scripting Guide's
  \ am-i-root example - `[ "$UID" -eq 0 ]`, UID being bash's and unset
  \ here - told an ordinary user they were root. And it refused what
  \ dash accepts: a `+` sign, and blanks around the digits
  \ (Iteration 413).
  DUP >R
  TEST-BLANKS
  0 TPI-NEG !
  DUP C@ 45 = IF -1 TPI-NEG ! 1+ ELSE DUP C@ 43 = IF 1+ THEN THEN
  0 TPI-N !  0 TPI-DIGITS !
  BEGIN DUP C@ 48 58 WITHIN WHILE
    DUP C@ 48 -  TPI-N @ 10 * +  TPI-N !  1 TPI-DIGITS +!  1+
  REPEAT
  TEST-BLANKS
  C@ 0<>  TPI-DIGITS @ 0=  OR IF
    S" test: " ERR-TYPE  R> ERR-CSTR  S" : bad number" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    -1 TEST-NUM-BAD? !  0 EXIT
  THEN
  R> DROP
  TPI-N @  TPI-NEG @ IF NEGATE THEN ;

: TEST-FILE-EXISTS? ( c-addr --- f )
  \ Asked by stat rather than by opening the file: `test -e` on a FIFO
  \ with no writer blocked for ever, because opening one waits for the
  \ other end. No suite here had ever made a FIFO (Iteration 378).
  \ stat, not lstat: `-e` follows the link, so a broken one is not
  \ there.
  -1 FILE-MODE 0<> ;

: TEST-IS-BANG? ( c-addr --- f )
  S" !" ROT STR0= ;

VARIABLE TEST-OP
VARIABLE TEST-A
VARIABLE TEST-B

: TEST-BINARY? ( a-addr op-addr b-addr --- f )
  SHADOW{ TEST-A TEST-OP TEST-B }
  S" ="   TEST-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-A @ TEST-B @ CSTR= EXIT THEN
  S" !="  TEST-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-A @ TEST-B @ CSTR= 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -eq" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT = EXIT
  THEN
  S" -ne" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT = 0= EXIT
  THEN
  S" -lt" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT < EXIT
  THEN
  S" -le" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT < 0= EXIT
  THEN
  S" -gt" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT < EXIT
  THEN
  S" -ge" TEST-OP @ STR0= IF
    TEST-A @ TEST-PARSE-INT TEST-B @ TEST-PARSE-INT < 0= EXIT
  THEN
  0 ;

VARIABLE TEST-U-OP
VARIABLE TEST-U-OPERAND

: TEST-FILE-SIZE ( c-addr --- u )   \ 0 if it cannot be opened
  \ Only a regular file or a directory is opened: a FIFO would block
  \ and a device has no size to report, and dash answers 0 for both
  \ (Iteration 378).
  DUP -1 FILE-MODE 61440 AND DUP 32768 = SWAP 16384 = OR 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN R/O OPEN-FILE IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  >R R@ FILE-SIZE 2DROP R> CLOSE-FILE DROP ;

: FILE-KIND-IS ( c-addr --- kind-bits )   \ S_IFMT of the file itself
  -1 FILE-MODE 61440 AND ;

: LINK-KIND-IS ( c-addr --- kind-bits )   \ ... without following a link
  0 FILE-MODE 61440 AND ;

: TEST-UNARY? ( op-addr operand-addr --- f )
  \ -f/-d are approximated as "does this path exist at all" (same as
  \ -e) - this kernel has no real stat/access primitive to actually
  \ distinguish a regular file from a directory, only OPEN-FILE.
  SHADOW{ TEST-U-OP TEST-U-OPERAND }
  S" -z" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ CSTRLEN 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -n" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ CSTRLEN 0= 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -e" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ TEST-FILE-EXISTS? EXIT THEN
  S" -f" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ FILE-KIND 1 = EXIT THEN
  \ -f and -d answered "does it exist" until Iteration 317; FILE-KIND
  \ (added in 307 for `set -C`) can tell them apart, and ACCESS answers
  \ -r -w -x, which were missing altogether.
  S" -d" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ FILE-KIND 2 = EXIT THEN
  S" -r" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ 4 ACCESS 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -w" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ 2 ACCESS 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -x" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ 1 ACCESS 0= EXIT THEN
  S" -s" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ TEST-FILE-SIZE 0 > EXIT THEN
  \ The rest read the mode bits. `FILE-MODE` is an engine primitive
  \ added in Iteration 378 because `FILE-KIND` answers only none,
  \ regular, directory or other, which cannot tell a block device from
  \ a socket or see the set-user bit. The constants are the ones
  \ <sys/stat.h> fixes: this shell targets Linux, and a port would
  \ have to check them.
  S" -b" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ FILE-KIND-IS 24576 = EXIT THEN
  S" -c" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ FILE-KIND-IS 8192  = EXIT THEN
  S" -p" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ FILE-KIND-IS 4096  = EXIT THEN
  S" -S" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ FILE-KIND-IS 49152 = EXIT THEN
  S" -h" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ LINK-KIND-IS 40960 = EXIT THEN
  S" -L" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ LINK-KIND-IS 40960 = EXIT THEN
  S" -u" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ -1 FILE-MODE 2048 AND 0<> EXIT THEN
  S" -g" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ -1 FILE-MODE 1024 AND 0<> EXIT THEN
  S" -k" TEST-U-OP @ STR0= IF TEST-U-OPERAND @ -1 FILE-MODE 512  AND 0<> EXIT THEN
  0 ;

VARIABLE TP-I          \ the cursor, an index from TEST-START
VARIABLE TP-BAD?       \ a malformed expression

: TP-ARG ( --- c-addr | 0 )   TP-I @ TEST-NARGS < IF TP-I @ TEST-ARGN ELSE 0 THEN ;
: TP-ARG-AT ( n --- c-addr | 0 )
  TP-I @ + DUP TEST-NARGS < IF TEST-ARGN ELSE DROP 0 THEN ;
VARIABLE TP-T
VARIABLE TP-TU

: TP-IS? ( c-addr text-addr u --- f )   \ is the argument this text
  \ Four values, so they go in variables: juggling them got the order
  \ wrong and crashed (the lesson of Iteration 308, again).
  TP-TU !  TP-T !
  ?DUP 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN TP-T @ TP-TU @ STR= ;

: TP-BINARY-OP? ( c-addr --- f )   \ one of the two-operand operators
  ?DUP 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP S" =" TP-IS? IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP S" !=" TP-IS? IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP S" -eq" TP-IS? IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP S" -ne" TP-IS? IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP S" -lt" TP-IS? IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP S" -le" TP-IS? IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP S" -gt" TP-IS? IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
      S" -ge" TP-IS? ;

DEFER TP-EXPR-CALL   ( --- f )

: TP-PRIMARY ( --- f )
  TP-ARG ?DUP 0= IF -1 TP-BAD? ! 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP S" (" TP-IS? IF
    DROP 1 TP-I +!
    TP-EXPR-CALL
    TP-ARG S" )" TP-IS? IF 1 TP-I +! ELSE -1 TP-BAD? ! THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  DUP TEST-IS-BANG? IF
    DROP 1 TP-I +!  RECURSE 0= EXIT           \ a Forth word names itself so
  THEN
  \ a binary operator in the middle makes this three arguments
  1 TP-ARG-AT TP-BINARY-OP? IF
    1 TP-ARG-AT  2 TP-ARG-AT                 ( a op b )
    DUP 0= IF 2DROP DROP -1 TP-BAD? ! 0 EXIT THEN
    TEST-BINARY? 3 TP-I +! EXIT
  THEN
  \ a unary operator takes one argument, and anything else is a string
  DUP C@ 45 = 1 TP-ARG-AT 0<> AND IF
    1 TP-ARG-AT TEST-UNARY? 2 TP-I +! EXIT   ( op operand )
  THEN
  CSTRLEN 0= 0= 1 TP-I +! ;

: TP-AND ( --- f )
  TP-PRIMARY
  BEGIN TP-ARG S" -a" TP-IS? WHILE
    1 TP-I +!  TP-PRIMARY AND
  REPEAT ;

: TP-OR ( --- f )
  TP-AND
  BEGIN TP-ARG S" -o" TP-IS? WHILE
    1 TP-I +!  TP-AND OR
  REPEAT ;

' TP-OR IS TP-EXPR-CALL

: EVAL-TEST-ARGS ( --- f )
  \ POSIX's test grammar: primaries joined by -a and -o, negated with !,
  \ grouped with ( ). Until Iteration 317 this counted arguments instead,
  \ handling at most four, so `[ 1 -eq 1 -a 2 -eq 2 ]` was false.
  TEST-NARGS 0 = IF 0 EXIT THEN
  0 TP-I !  0 TP-BAD? !
  \ ... and POSIX decides one to four arguments by their NUMBER, before
  \ any grammar: 317 replaced counting with the grammar so that long -a
  \ and -o chains work, and lost the counted rules on the way. With
  \ three, a binary operator in the middle wins over a leading `!` or
  \ `(`, so `[ ! = ! ]` compares two strings; `!` alone is a non-empty
  \ string; `! X` negates X's one-argument test; `! A op B` negates the
  \ comparison. The grammar took each leading `!` as an operator and
  \ found the rest malformed - `[ '!' = '!' ]` was an error where bash
  \ and dash say true - which the construct matrix's new value dimension
  \ found at once (tools/gen-value-cases.py, Iteration 422).
  TEST-NARGS 1 = IF 0 TP-ARG-AT CSTRLEN 0<> EXIT THEN
  TEST-NARGS 2 = IF 0 TP-ARG-AT TEST-IS-BANG? IF
    1 TP-ARG-AT CSTRLEN 0= EXIT
  THEN THEN
  TEST-NARGS 3 = IF 1 TP-ARG-AT TP-BINARY-OP? IF
    0 TP-ARG-AT 1 TP-ARG-AT 2 TP-ARG-AT TEST-BINARY? EXIT
  THEN THEN
  TEST-NARGS 4 = IF 0 TP-ARG-AT TEST-IS-BANG? IF 2 TP-ARG-AT TP-BINARY-OP? IF
    1 TP-ARG-AT 2 TP-ARG-AT 3 TP-ARG-AT TEST-BINARY? 0= EXIT
  THEN THEN THEN
  TP-OR
  TP-I @ TEST-NARGS < IF DROP 0 -1 TP-BAD? ! THEN
  TP-BAD? @ IF DROP 0 THEN ;

: DO-TEST ( --- )  \ ARGV[0] is "test" - evaluates ARGV[1..ARGC).
  1 TEST-START !
  ARGC @ TEST-END !
  0 TEST-NUM-BAD? !
  \ 2, not 1, for an expression that could not be evaluated - a
  \ missing operand, or an operand that is not a number. It was 1,
  \ which made `[ 1 -eq ]` indistinguishable from a comparison that
  \ was merely false (Iteration 413).
  EVAL-TEST-ARGS  TP-BAD? @ TEST-NUM-BAD? @ OR IF DROP 2 ELSE IF 0 ELSE 1 THEN THEN
  LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-BRACKET-TEST ( --- )  \ ARGV[0] is "[" - like DO-TEST, but the
                             \ last argument must be a matching "]",
                             \ excluded from the range evaluated.
  ARGC @ 2 < IF
    ." [: missing matching ']'" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !  -1 VALUE-FAILED? !      \ the status the caller reports
    EXIT
  THEN
  ARGC @ 1- ARGV@ S" ]" ROT STR0= 0= IF
    ." [: missing matching ']'" CR
    2 LAST-STATUS !  -1 VALUE-FAILED? !      \ the status the caller reports
    EXIT
  THEN
  1 TEST-START !
  ARGC @ 1- TEST-END !
  0 TEST-NUM-BAD? !
  \ 2, not 1, for an expression that could not be evaluated - a
  \ missing operand, or an operand that is not a number. It was 1,
  \ which made `[ 1 -eq ]` indistinguishable from a comparison that
  \ was merely false (Iteration 413).
  EVAL-TEST-ARGS  TP-BAD? @ TEST-NUM-BAD? @ OR IF DROP 2 ELSE IF 0 ELSE 1 THEN THEN
  LAST-STATUS ! ;

\ ==================================================================
\ Assignment machinery, moved ahead of DISPATCH so `readonly NAME=value`
\ can reuse it instead of reimplementing the split (Iteration 51).
\ ==================================================================


: FIND-EQ-OR-END ( c-addr --- offset )   \ the first '=', or the length
  \ CSTRLEN and SCAN are both engine primitives, so this is a handful of
  \ dispatches whatever the word's length; the loop it replaces cost
  \ three a character and was 3.6% of a realistic script
  \ (Iteration 365).
  DUP CSTRLEN  2DUP 61 SCAN  NIP  -  NIP ;

: VALID-NAME? ( c-addr u --- f )  \ a non-empty, POSIX-valid name?
  \ The table NAME-CHAR? reads is indexed straight from here, so the
  \ loop is two dispatches a character rather than five (Iteration 366).
  DUP 0= IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  OVER C@ DUP 48 < 0= SWAP 58 < AND IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  OVER + SWAP ?DO
    I C@ NAME-CHAR-TABLE + C@ 0= IF UNLOOP 0 EXIT THEN
  LOOP -1 ;

: ASSIGNMENT-EQPOS ( c-addr --- eqpos | -1 )  \ c-addr: a NUL-
                                                \ terminated string
                                                \ (ARGV[0]). The offset
                                                \ of its first '=' if
                                                \ everything before it
                                                \ forms a valid,
                                                \ non-empty POSIX name
                                                \ - else -1 (not an
                                                \ assignment word at
                                                \ all).
  DUP FIND-EQ-OR-END
  2DUP + C@ 61 <> IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  2DUP VALID-NAME? 0= IF 2DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  SWAP DROP ;

SHVAR-NAME-MAX BUFFER: ASSIGN-NAME-BUF
SHVAR-VALUE-MAX BUFFER: ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF

VARIABLE DA-ADDR
VARIABLE DA-EQ

: SPLIT-ASSIGN-AT ( c-addr eqpos --- )
  \ Split a validated "NAME=value" word into ASSIGN-NAME-BUF and
  \ ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF, without deciding what to do with them. Shared by
  \ DO-ASSIGN-AT (shell variables) and DO-ALIAS.
  SHADOW{ DA-ADDR DA-EQ }
  \ The whole name, however long: SET-SHVAR refuses one that does not
  \ fit the table. This copy used to fail silently for a name over 63
  \ characters and leave the PREVIOUS assignment's name in the buffer,
  \ so `x=5; <70-character name>=1` set x (Iteration 252).
  DA-EQ @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER ASSIGN-NAME-BUF
  DA-ADDR @ DA-EQ @ ASSIGN-NAME-BUF BUFFER-SIZE ASSIGN-NAME-BUF SAFE-COPY-NUL DROP
  DA-ADDR @ DA-EQ @ + 1+
  DA-ADDR @ CSTRLEN DA-EQ @ - 1-
  DUP 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF
  ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF BUFFER-SIZE ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF VALUE-COPY ;

: DO-ASSIGN-AT ( c-addr eqpos --- )
  \ Split a validated "NAME=value" word at its '=' and record it.
  \ Shared by assignment lines and `readonly NAME=value`.
  SPLIT-ASSIGN-AT
  ASSIGN-NAME-BUF ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF SET-SHVAR
  \ set -a: an assignment exports as well (XCU 2.14 `set`, Iteration 305)
  OPT-ALLEXPORT @ IF ASSIGN-VALUE-BUF ASSIGN-NAME-BUF SETENV DROP THEN ;

\ ==================================================================
\ The builtin table
\ ==================================================================
\ DISPATCH used to be sixty lines of
\ `S" name" ARGV @ STR0= IF DO-name -1 EXIT THEN`, one per builtin,
\ and `command -v` needed a second hand-maintained copy of the same
\ name list. Both are replaced by one table: registering a builtin is
\ adding a row, and every consumer reads the same rows.
\
\ A linked list built in the dictionary rather than a fixed array, so
\ there is no maximum number of builtins to pick and get wrong. Each
\ entry is [ link | xt | len | name bytes ], where link and xt are
\ OFFSETS from START - they are written into an image, and an
\ absolute address there is stale the moment it reloads (see
\ FORTH-STYLE.md).
\
\ Because registration is a runtime call rather than compiled-in
\ syntax, Forth code loaded later can add builtins the same way -
\ which is the mechanism the `forth` builtin discussed in Iteration
\ 38 needs.

VARIABLE BUILTIN-LIST   0 BUILTIN-LIST !
VARIABLE BI-XT
VARIABLE BI-ADDR
VARIABLE BI-LEN
VARIABLE BI-HERE

VARIABLE BUILTINS-CHANGED?   \ the index is rebuilt at the next lookup

: BUILTIN ( xt c-addr u --- )
  SHADOW{ BI-XT BI-ADDR BI-LEN | BI-HERE }
  \ ALIGN first: HERE after a colon definition is not aligned, and the
  \ entry is cells read with @ (PROGRESS.md 212).
  ALIGN HERE BI-HERE !
  BUILTIN-LIST @ ,
  BI-XT @ START @ - ,
  BI-LEN @ ,
  BI-ADDR @ HERE BI-LEN @ MOVE
  BI-LEN @ ALLOT
  ALIGN
  BI-HERE @ START @ - BUILTIN-LIST !
  -1 BUILTINS-CHANGED? ! ;    \ a builtin registered later joins the index

DEFER FIND-BUILTIN-CALL   ( c-addr --- xt | 0 )
: BUILTIN-NAME? ( c-addr --- f )  FIND-BUILTIN-CALL 0= 0= ;

\ ---- the builtin index (Iteration 292) ----
\ FIND-BUILTIN walked the whole list comparing names: 532 dispatches of
\ every loop iteration in tests/bench-vm, 6% of it, where dash bsearches
\ a sorted table in about six comparisons. The table is fixed once the
\ file has loaded, so it is hashed once, the same way the variables are
\ (Iteration 272) - open addressing, at least twice the entries.
128 CELLS BUFFER: BI-INDEX
VARIABLE BI-INDEX-SIZE
VARIABLE BI-COUNT
VARIABLE BI-E
VARIABLE BI-H

: BI-SLOT ( k --- addr )   CELLS BI-INDEX + ;
: BI-MASK ( --- m )        BI-INDEX-SIZE @ 1- ;
: BI-ENTRY-NAME ( entry --- c-addr )   3 CELLS + ;

: BI-NAME-HASH ( c-addr u --- h )   \ FNV-1a over exactly u bytes
  \ NAME-HASH reads to a NUL, and a builtin's name is stored WITHOUT
  \ one: for a name that exactly fills a cell - `continue` is eight
  \ characters - it ran on into the next entry, so the table's hash and
  \ the lookup's disagreed and that one builtin went missing.
  \ The accumulator is a variable, not the return stack: the loop index
  \ lives there, so an R> inside a ?DO would take that instead.
  2166136261 BI-H !
  OVER + SWAP ?DO I C@ BI-H @ XOR 16777619 * BI-H ! LOOP
  BI-H @ DUP 16 RSHIFT XOR  73244475 *  DUP 16 RSHIFT XOR ;

: BI-INSERT ( entry --- )
  DUP DUP BI-ENTRY-NAME SWAP 2 CELLS + @ BI-NAME-HASH BI-MASK AND
  BEGIN DUP BI-SLOT @ WHILE 1+ BI-MASK AND REPEAT
  BI-SLOT ! ;

: BUILD-BUILTIN-INDEX ( --- )
  0 BI-COUNT !
  BUILTIN-LIST @ BI-E !
  BEGIN BI-E @ WHILE 1 BI-COUNT +!  BI-E @ START @ + @ BI-E ! REPEAT
  16 BEGIN DUP BI-COUNT @ 2* < WHILE 2* REPEAT
  DUP BI-INDEX-SIZE !  CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER BI-INDEX
  BI-INDEX BI-INDEX-SIZE @ CELLS 0 FILL
  BUILTIN-LIST @ BI-E !
  BEGIN BI-E @ WHILE
    BI-E @ START @ + DUP BI-INSERT  @ BI-E !
  REPEAT ;

: FIND-BUILTIN-HASHED ( c-addr --- xt | 0 )
  BUILTINS-CHANGED? @ IF BUILD-BUILTIN-INDEX 0 BUILTINS-CHANGED? ! THEN
  DUP DUP CSTRLEN BI-NAME-HASH BI-MASK AND   ( name k )
  BEGIN
    DUP BI-SLOT @ ?DUP WHILE                 ( name k entry )
    DUP BI-ENTRY-NAME OVER 2 CELLS + @       ( name k entry addr len )
    4 PICK STR0= IF
      NIP NIP CELL+ @ START @ + EXIT         \ its xt
    THEN
    DROP 1+ BI-MASK AND
  REPEAT
  2DROP 0 ;
' FIND-BUILTIN-HASHED IS FIND-BUILTIN-CALL

\ The alias TABLE lives here, ahead of DO-COMMAND, which has to
\ report an alias. The expansion machinery (TRY-ALIAS/DO-ALIAS)
\ stays further down, where NORMALIZE-OPERATORS and TOKENIZE are
\ available.

32  CONSTANT MAX-ALIASES          \ the FIRST size; it doubles when full (419)
64  CONSTANT ALIAS-NAME-MAX
256 CONSTANT ALIAS-VALUE-MAX
MAX-ALIASES ALIAS-NAME-MAX  * BUFFER: ALIAS-NAMES
MAX-ALIASES ALIAS-VALUE-MAX * BUFFER: ALIAS-VALUES
VARIABLE ALIAS-COUNT
VARIABLE AL-I
VARIABLE AL-NAME
VARIABLE AL-VALUE

VARIABLE ALIAS-CAP
MAX-ALIASES ALIAS-CAP !

: ALIAS-GROW ( --- )   \ the alias table doubles rather than refusing
  ALIAS-CAP @ 2* ALIAS-CAP !
  ALIAS-CAP @ ALIAS-NAME-MAX  * ENSURE-BUFFER ALIAS-NAMES
  ALIAS-CAP @ ALIAS-VALUE-MAX * ENSURE-BUFFER ALIAS-VALUES ;

: ALIAS-NAME-SLOT  ( i --- addr )  ALIAS-NAME-MAX  * ALIAS-NAMES  + ;
: ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT ( i --- addr )  ALIAS-VALUE-MAX * ALIAS-VALUES + ;

: FIND-ALIAS ( name-addr --- i | -1 )
  SHADOW{ AL-NAME | AL-I }
  BEGIN AL-I @ ALIAS-COUNT @ < WHILE
    AL-I @ ALIAS-NAME-SLOT AL-NAME @ CSTR= IF AL-I @ EXIT THEN
    AL-I @ 1+ AL-I !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

: SET-ALIAS ( name-addr value-addr --- )
  SHADOW{ AL-NAME AL-VALUE | AL-I }
  AL-NAME @ CSTRLEN ALIAS-NAME-MAX < 0= IF
    S" alias: name too long" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL  2 LAST-STATUS !
    EXIT
  THEN
  AL-NAME @ FIND-ALIAS AL-I !
  AL-I @ -1 = IF
    ALIAS-COUNT @ ALIAS-CAP @ < 0= IF ALIAS-GROW THEN
    ALIAS-COUNT @ AL-I !
    ALIAS-COUNT @ 1+ ALIAS-COUNT !
  THEN
  AL-NAME  @ AL-I @ ALIAS-NAME-SLOT  ALIAS-NAME-MAX  SAFE-CSTR-COPY DROP
  \ Reported when too long, never left stale (Iteration 252).
  AL-VALUE @ DUP CSTRLEN AL-I @ ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT ALIAS-VALUE-MAX VALUE-COPY ;

VARIABLE DO-I

: MARK-READONLY ( name-addr --- )
  FIND-SHVAR DUP -1 = IF DROP EXIT THEN
  SHVAR-RO + -1 SWAP C! ;

: PRINT-READONLY ( --- )
  \ "readonly -p", and "readonly" with no operands: list the read-only
  \ variables in a form that could be re-read as input, per POSIX.
  0 DO-I !
  BEGIN DO-I @ SHVAR-COUNT @ < WHILE
    DO-I @ SHVAR-RO + C@ IF
      ." readonly "
      DO-I @ SHVAR-NAME-SLOT DUP CSTRLEN TYPE
      [CHAR] = EMIT
      DO-I @ SHVAR-VALUE-SLOT DUP CSTRLEN TYPE
      CR
    THEN
    DO-I @ 1+ DO-I !
  REPEAT ;

: DO-READONLY ( --- )
  \ readonly NAME | readonly NAME=value | readonly -p. With a value it
  \ assigns first, then marks; the mark must come second or the
  \ assignment would be refused by its own flag.
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ARGC @ 1 = IF PRINT-READONLY EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 2 = IF
    S" -p" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF PRINT-READONLY EXIT THEN
  THEN
  ARGC @ 1 < IF EXIT THEN
  1 DO-I !
  BEGIN DO-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    DO-I @ ARGV@ ASSIGNMENT-EQPOS
    DUP -1 = IF
      DROP
      DO-I @ ARGV@ MARK-READONLY
    ELSE
      DO-I @ ARGV@ SWAP DO-ASSIGN-AT
      ASSIGN-NAME-BUF MARK-READONLY
    THEN
    DO-I @ 1+ DO-I !
  REPEAT ;


: (SPECIAL-ERROR) ( status --- )
  LAST-STATUS !
  INTERACTIVE? @ 0= IF LAST-STATUS @ SHELL-EXIT THEN ;
' (SPECIAL-ERROR) IS SPECIAL-ERROR

: DO-SHIFT ( --- )
  \ shift [n]: drop the first n positional parameters. Out-of-range
  \ n is an error and leaves the parameters untouched, per POSIX.
  1
  ARGC @ 1 > IF DROP ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL THEN
  DUP POS-PARAM-COUNT @ > IF
    DROP S" shell: shift: count out of range" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    2 SPECIAL-ERROR EXIT
  THEN
  \ "shift 0" is valid and a no-op. Without this guard the count of 0
  \ reaches DO as "0 0 DO", which in this kernel runs the entire
  \ unsigned range - the same hazard as TYPE-N-TO-TOK in Iteration 88,
  \ found by auditing every DO here rather than by anything hitting it.
  DUP 0 > IF
  DUP 0 DO
    0 DO-I !
    BEGIN DO-I @ 1+ POS-PARAM-COUNT @ < WHILE
      DO-I @ 1+ CELLS POS-BLOCK @ + @  DO-I @ CELLS POS-BLOCK @ + !
      DO-I @ 1+ DO-I !
    REPEAT
    POS-PARAM-COUNT @ 1- POS-PARAM-COUNT !
  LOOP
  THEN
  DROP
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

VARIABLE CMD-VERBOSE?   \ `command -V`: a sentence, not just the word
VARIABLE CMD-KIND       \ 0 builtin, 1 keyword, 2 function, 3 a path

: CMD-V-FOUND ( c-addr u --- )   \ report a hit and exit 0
  \ `-v` prints the word or path; `-V` says what it is, as dash words it
  CMD-VERBOSE? @ IF
    2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN TYPE S"  is " TYPE
    CMD-KIND @ 3 = IF TYPE ELSE
      2DROP
      CMD-KIND @ 1 = IF S" a shell keyword" TYPE ELSE
      CMD-KIND @ 2 = IF S" a shell function" TYPE ELSE
                        S" a shell builtin" TYPE THEN THEN
    THEN
  ELSE
    TYPE
  THEN
  CR 0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: PATH-LOOKUP? ( --- f )
  \ Is ARGV[1] findable in PATH? Prints the full path if so. Same
  \ PATH walk as SEARCH-PATH, but testing for existence instead of
  \ exec'ing - deliberately a separate word rather than a flag on
  \ SEARCH-PATH, whose whole contract is "only returns on failure".
  S" PATH" ENVNAMBUF SWAP MOVE 0 ENVNAMBUF 4 + C!
  ENVNAMBUF GETENV
  DUP 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN OVER + PATH-END2 !
  PATH-CUR !
  BEGIN PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < WHILE
    PATH-CUR @
    BEGIN
      PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < IF PATH-CUR @ C@ 58 <> ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE PATH-CUR @ 1+ PATH-CUR ! REPEAT
    PATH-CUR @ OVER -
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ CSTRLEN OVER + 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER PATHBUF   \ grown to fit (421)
    PATHBUF B-RESET B-STR 47 B-CHAR ARGV 1 CELLS + @ B-CSTR B-FINISH
    PATHBUF TEST-FILE-EXISTS? IF
      3 CMD-KIND !  PATHBUF DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND   \ a path, so print it
      -1 EXIT
    THEN
    PATH-CUR @ PATH-END2 @ < IF PATH-CUR @ 1+ PATH-CUR ! THEN
  REPEAT
  0 ;

VARIABLE RW-NAME

: RESERVED-WORD? ( c-addr --- f )
  \ The POSIX shell reserved words. Kept as one list here rather than
  \ scattered through RUN-TOKENIZED's own LINE-IS? checks, which test
  \ them individually for a different purpose (dispatch, not
  \ classification).
  RW-NAME !
  S" if"    RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" then"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" else"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" elif"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" fi"    RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" do"    RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" done"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" case"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" esac"  RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" while" RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" until" RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" for"   RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" in"    RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" {"     RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" }"     RW-NAME @ STR0= IF -1 EXIT THEN
  S" !"     RW-NAME @ STR0= ;

DEFER DROP-ARGV0-CALL   ( --- )   \ defined below, used by `command`
DEFER DISPATCH-CALL     ( --- f )
DEFER RUN-EXTERNAL-CALL ( --- )
CREATE PPID-NM  CSTR," PPID"   \ $PPID (Iteration 318)
CREATE PATH-NM  CSTR," PATH"
CREATE DEFAULT-PATH                          \ what `command -p` looks in
  47 C, 98 C, 105 C, 110 C, 58 C,
  47 C, 117 C, 115 C, 114 C, 47 C, 98 C, 105 C, 110 C, 0 C,
1024 BUFFER: CMD-SAVED-PATH
VARIABLE NO-FUNCTIONS?   \ `command` looks past the function table

: COMMAND-RUN ( --- )
  \ `command NAME args` runs it as if it were not a function. It did
  \ nothing at all until Iteration 306: `command echo hi` printed
  \ nothing and returned 0.
  DROP-ARGV0-CALL
  ARGC @ 0= IF 0 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  -1 NO-FUNCTIONS? !
  DISPATCH-CALL 0= IF RUN-EXTERNAL-CALL THEN
  0 NO-FUNCTIONS? ! ;

: DROP-ARGV1 ( --- )   \ the second word, out of the list
  ARGC @ 2 ?DO
    I ARGV@  ARGV I 1- CELLS + !
    I ARGV-QUOTED + C@  ARGV-QUOTED I 1- + C!
  LOOP
  ARGC @ 1- ARGC !  0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + ! ;

: DO-COMMAND ( --- )
  \ command -v NAME, and command [-p] NAME args.
  ARGC @ 2 < IF 0 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  \ More than one option: `command -p -V name` and `command -v -p name`
  \ are both valid, and this read one option and took the next for the
  \ name (Iteration 350). `-p` is noted and taken out of the list so the
  \ rest reads as it always has.
  ARGC @ 3 > IF
    S" -p" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF
      2 ARGV@ C@ 45 = IF DROP-ARGV1 THEN
    ELSE
      S" -p" 2 ARGV@ STR0= IF
        ARGC @ 3 ?DO I ARGV@ ARGV I 1- CELLS + ! LOOP
        ARGC @ 1- ARGC !  0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
      THEN
    THEN
  THEN
  S" -p" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF
    DROP-ARGV0-CALL
    \ MOVE takes ( src dst u ): written the other way round in Iteration
    \ 306 it copied the EMPTY buffer over PATH, so every command after a
    \ `command -p` went missing.
    PATH-NM LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP IF
      DUP CSTRLEN 1+ CMD-SAVED-PATH SWAP MOVE -1
    ELSE 0 THEN >R
    PATH-NM DEFAULT-PATH SET-SHVAR
    COMMAND-RUN
    R> IF PATH-NM CMD-SAVED-PATH SET-SHVAR THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  S" -V" 1 ARGV@ STR0= -1 = IF -1 ELSE 0 THEN CMD-VERBOSE? !  0 CMD-KIND !
  S" -v" 1 ARGV@ STR0= CMD-VERBOSE? @ OR 0= IF COMMAND-RUN EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 3 < IF 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  S" -v" 1 ARGV@ STR0= CMD-VERBOSE? @ OR 0= IF 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  \ POSIX: command -v reports reserved words and aliases too, not just
  \ functions, builtins and PATH entries. Order matters - an alias
  \ shadows a command of the same name, and a reserved word is not a
  \ command at all.
  \ a path operand answers for itself, if it can be run (XCU `command`)
  2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN HAS-SLASH? IF
    2 ARGV@ 1 ACCESS 0= IF
      3 CMD-KIND !  2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND EXIT
    ELSE 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  THEN
  2 ARGV@ RESERVED-WORD? IF
    1 CMD-KIND !  2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND EXIT
  THEN
  2 ARGV@ FIND-ALIAS DUP -1 <> IF
    ." alias " 2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN TYPE
    [CHAR] = EMIT [CHAR] ' EMIT
    ALIAS-VALUE-SLOT DUP CSTRLEN TYPE
    [CHAR] ' EMIT CR
    0 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  DROP
  2 ARGV@ FIND-FUNC -1 <> IF
    2 CMD-KIND !  2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND EXIT
  THEN
  2 ARGV@ BUILTIN-NAME? IF
    2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN CMD-V-FOUND EXIT
  THEN
  ARGV 2 CELLS + @ ARGV 1 CELLS + !   \ PATH-LOOKUP? reads ARGV[1]
  PATH-LOOKUP? 0= IF
    \ Not found is status 127, and `-V` says so in dash's words; this
    \ answered 1 and said nothing (Iteration 356).
    CMD-VERBOSE? @ IF
      ARGV 1 CELLS + @ ERR-CSTR  S" : not found" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    THEN
    127 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

\ The builtins that used to be written inline in DISPATCH's chain.

: DO-COLON ( --- )  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-EXIT ( --- )
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL SHELL-EXIT
  ELSE
    \ `exit` with no operand inside a trap takes the status the shell
    \ had when the trap was entered, not the status of the last command
    \ the trap itself ran (XCU 2.14 trap). This used the latter, so
    \ `trap 'echo x; exit' EXIT` always exited 0 (Iteration 336).
    IN-TRAP? @ IF TRAP-SAVED-STATUS @ ELSE LAST-STATUS @ THEN SHELL-EXIT
  THEN ;

VARIABLE DOT-DEPTH   \ files being run by `.` (tree.4)

: DO-RETURN ( --- )
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL LAST-STATUS !
  ELSE
    \ `return` with no operand inside a trap takes the status the shell
    \ had when the trap was entered, as `exit` does since Iteration 336,
    \ not the status of the last command the trap ran (Iteration 360).
    IN-TRAP? @ IF TRAP-SAVED-STATUS @ LAST-STATUS ! THEN
  THEN
  \ from a function, or from a file run by `.` (Iteration 270)
  FUNC-DEPTH @ DOT-DEPTH @ + 0 > IF
    -1 RETURN-PENDING? !
  ELSE
    S" shell: return: not in a function" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL  1 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

: DIGITS-ONLY? ( c-addr u --- f )   \ at least one digit, and nothing else
  DUP 0= IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  OVER + SWAP DO I C@ 48 58 WITHIN 0= IF UNLOOP 0 EXIT THEN LOOP -1 ;

: LOOP-COUNT-ARG ( name-cstr --- n | 0 )   \ 0: not a count, and reported
  \ `break [n]` and `continue [n]` leave n loops (XCU); the operand was
  \ ignored entirely until Iteration 329, so `break 2` broke one loop.
  \ The count must be a positive integer. This clamped anything below 1
  \ up to 1, so `break 0`, `break -1` and `break abc` all quietly meant
  \ `break 1`; dash reports them, and as these are special builtins a
  \ non-interactive shell then exits (Iteration 421, from yash's
  \ break-p.tst and continue-p.tst).
  ARGC @ 1 > 0= IF DROP 1 LOOP-DEPTH @ MIN EXIT THEN
  1 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN 2DUP DIGITS-ONLY? IF PARSE-DECIMAL ELSE 2DROP 0 THEN
  DUP 1 < IF
    DROP S" shell: " ERR-TYPE ERR-CSTR S" : " ERR-TYPE
    1 ARGV@ ERR-CSTR S" : bad number" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    2 SPECIAL-ERROR  0 EXIT
  THEN
  NIP LOOP-DEPTH @ MIN ;

CREATE BREAK-NM     CSTR," break"
CREATE CONTINUE-NM  CSTR," continue"

: DO-BREAK ( --- )
  LOOP-DEPTH @ 0 > IF
    BREAK-NM LOOP-COUNT-ARG ?DUP 0= IF EXIT THEN LOOP-LEVEL !
    -1 LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? !  -1 LOOP-BREAK? !  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ELSE
    ." break: not in a loop" CR  1 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

: DO-CONTINUE ( --- )
  LOOP-DEPTH @ 0 > IF
    CONTINUE-NM LOOP-COUNT-ARG ?DUP 0= IF EXIT THEN LOOP-LEVEL !
    -1 LOOP-CONTROL-PENDING? !  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ELSE
    ." continue: not in a loop" CR  1 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

: OPT-LETTER ( c --- addr | 0 )
  DUP 101 = IF DROP OPT-ERREXIT EXIT THEN
  DUP 117 = IF DROP OPT-NOUNSET EXIT THEN
  DUP 120 = IF DROP OPT-XTRACE EXIT THEN
  DUP 102 = IF DROP OPT-NOGLOB EXIT THEN
  DUP 109 = IF DROP OPT-MONITOR EXIT THEN
  DUP 98 = IF DROP OPT-NOTIFY EXIT THEN      \ -b (Iteration 386)
  DUP 67 = IF DROP OPT-NOCLOBBER EXIT THEN   \ -C (Iteration 307)
  DUP 97 = IF DROP OPT-ALLEXPORT EXIT THEN   \ -a
  DUP 118 = IF DROP OPT-VERBOSE EXIT THEN    \ -v
  110 = IF OPT-NOEXEC EXIT THEN
  0 ;
: OPT-NAME ( c-addr --- addr | 0 )
  >R
  S" errexit" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-ERREXIT EXIT THEN
  S" nounset" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-NOUNSET EXIT THEN
  S" xtrace" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-XTRACE EXIT THEN
  S" noglob" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-NOGLOB EXIT THEN
  S" allexport" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-ALLEXPORT EXIT THEN
  S" verbose" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-VERBOSE EXIT THEN
  S" noexec" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-NOEXEC EXIT THEN
  S" monitor" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-MONITOR EXIT THEN
  S" notify" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-NOTIFY EXIT THEN
  S" noclobber" R@ STR0= IF R> DROP OPT-NOCLOBBER EXIT THEN
  R> DROP 0 ;
: .OPT ( c-addr u addr on? --- )   \ one line of set -o or set +o
  IF
    >R 2DUP TYPE 16 OVER - 1 MAX SPACES 2DROP
    R> @ IF S" on" ELSE S" off" THEN TYPE CR
  ELSE
    @ IF S" set -o " ELSE S" set +o " THEN TYPE TYPE CR
  THEN ;
: LIST-OPTIONS ( minus? --- )   \ set -o, or set +o
  DUP IF S" Current option settings" TYPE CR THEN >R
  S" allexport" OPT-ALLEXPORT R@ .OPT
  S" errexit" OPT-ERREXIT R@ .OPT
  S" monitor" OPT-MONITOR R@ .OPT
  S" notify" OPT-NOTIFY R@ .OPT
  S" noclobber" OPT-NOCLOBBER R@ .OPT
  S" noexec" OPT-NOEXEC R@ .OPT
  S" noglob" OPT-NOGLOB R@ .OPT
  S" nounset" OPT-NOUNSET R@ .OPT
  S" verbose" OPT-VERBOSE R@ .OPT
  S" xtrace" OPT-XTRACE R@ .OPT
  R> DROP ;

: SET-BAD-OPTION ( c-addr --- )
  S" shell: set: " ERR-TYPE ERR-CSTR S" : invalid option" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  2 SPECIAL-ERROR ;

VARIABLE SET-I
: SET-PARAMS-FROM ( i --- )   \ ARGV[i..] become the positional parameters
  ARGC @ OVER - 0 ?DO                        ( i )
    DUP I + ARGV@  ARGV I 1+ CELLS + !
  LOOP
  ARGC @ SWAP - 1+ ARGC !
  0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + !
  SET-POS-PARAMS-FROM-ARGV ;

DEFER LIST-VARIABLES-CALL   \ set with no arguments (defined further on)

: DO-SET ( --- )
  ARGC @ 1 = IF LIST-VARIABLES-CALL 0 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  \ Options (Iteration 270): -e -u -x -f -n and their + forms, -o and +o
  \ by name, set -o / set +o to list them. What follows the options, or
  \ follows `--`, becomes the positional parameters; with only options,
  \ they are left alone. It used to take every argument as a parameter,
  \ so `set -e` set $1 to -e.
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  1 SET-I !
  BEGIN SET-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    SET-I @ ARGV@
    DUP S" --" ROT STR0= IF DROP SET-I @ 1+ SET-PARAMS-FROM EXIT THEN
    DUP S" -" ROT STR0= IF DROP 0 OPT-XTRACE ! SET-I @ 1+ SET-PARAMS-FROM EXIT THEN
    DUP C@ DUP 45 = SWAP 43 = OR OVER 1+ C@ 0<> AND 0= IF
      DROP SET-I @ SET-PARAMS-FROM EXIT
    THEN
    DUP C@ 45 =                              ( arg on? )
    OVER 1+ C@ 111 = 2 PICK 2 + C@ 0= AND IF   \ -o / +o
      NIP
      SET-I @ 1+ ARGC @ < IF
        1 SET-I +!
        SET-I @ ARGV@ OPT-NAME ?DUP IF ! ELSE
          DROP SET-I @ ARGV@ SET-BAD-OPTION EXIT
        THEN
      ELSE
        LIST-OPTIONS
      THEN
    ELSE
      SWAP 1+
      BEGIN DUP C@ WHILE
        DUP C@ OPT-LETTER ?DUP IF 2 PICK SWAP ! ELSE
          2DROP SET-I @ ARGV@ SET-BAD-OPTION EXIT
        THEN
        1+
      REPEAT 2DROP
    THEN
    1 SET-I +!
  REPEAT ;

LINE-MAX BUFFER: FORTH-BUF
VARIABLE FTH-LEN
VARIABLE FA-ADDR
VARIABLE FA-LEN
VARIABLE FTH-I

: FORTH-APPEND ( c-addr u --- )
  SHADOW{ FA-ADDR FA-LEN }
  FTH-LEN @ FA-LEN @ + 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER FORTH-BUF
  FTH-LEN @ FA-LEN @ + LINE-HARD-MAX < IF
    FA-ADDR @ FORTH-BUF FTH-LEN @ + FA-LEN @ MOVE
    FTH-LEN @ FA-LEN @ + FTH-LEN !
    32 FORTH-BUF FTH-LEN @ + C!
    FTH-LEN @ 1+ FTH-LEN !
  THEN ;

: DO-FORTH ( --- )
  \ forth WORDS... - hand the rest of the line to the Forth
  \ interpreter. This is the extension mechanism discussed back in
  \ Iteration 38: the shell is written in Forth and the interpreter is
  \ already present, so an escape hatch costs almost nothing - and
  \ because BUILTIN is an ordinary runtime call, Forth loaded this way
  \ can register further builtins itself.
  \
  \ The arguments arrive already tokenized and expanded, so $VAR works
  \ and quoting is the shell's, not Forth's; they are rejoined with
  \ single spaces, which is fine for Forth's own whitespace-delimited
  \ syntax. Caveats, deliberately not papered over: a Forth error
  \ ABORTs the whole shell, and a word that unbalances the stack
  \ corrupts this shell rather than a child process, because there is
  \ no isolation. That is the nature of the facility.
  SHADOW{ | FTH-I }
  0 FTH-LEN !
  1 FTH-I !
  BEGIN FTH-I @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    FTH-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN FORTH-APPEND
    FTH-I @ 1+ FTH-I !
  REPEAT
  FORTH-BUF FTH-LEN @ EVALUATE
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

LINE-MAX        BUFFER: READ-BUF     \ grows: see LINE-BUF
SHVAR-VALUE-MAX BUFFER: READ-FIELD
VARIABLE RD-LEN
VARIABLE RD-FLEN
VARIABLE RD-RAW?
VARIABLE RD-EOF?
VARIABLE RD-POS
VARIABLE RD-VI
: RD-EMIT ( c --- )   \ a character of the field being built
  RD-FLEN @ 2 + ENSURE-BUFFER READ-FIELD
  READ-FIELD RD-FLEN @ + C!  1 RD-FLEN +! ;

: RD-ESCAPED? ( --- f )   \ is the character at RD-POS quoted by a backslash
  RD-RAW? @ IF 0 EXIT THEN
  READ-BUF RD-POS @ + C@ 92 = ;

: RD-GET-LINE ( --- got? )   \ a logical line: \-newline joins the next
  0 RD-LEN !  0 RD-EOF? !
  BEGIN
    RD-LEN @ BUFFER-BODY READ-BUF 0 READ-LINE-INTO   ( u flag )
    LONG-LINE? @ IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
    SWAP RD-LEN @ + RD-LEN !                          ( flag )
    RL-NEWLINE? @ 0= IF -1 RD-EOF? ! THEN
    0= IF RD-LEN @ 0 > EXIT THEN                      \ nothing came
    RD-RAW? @ 0= RD-LEN @ 0 > AND IF
      READ-BUF RD-LEN @ 1- + C@ 92 =
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    RD-LEN @ 1- RD-LEN !                              \ the backslash goes
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

: RD-IFS-WS? ( c --- f )   \ an IFS character that is also whitespace
  DUP IFS-CHAR? SWAP DUP 32 = OVER 9 = OR SWAP 10 = OR AND ;

: RD-SKIP-IFS-WS ( --- )
  BEGIN
    RD-POS @ RD-LEN @ < IF READ-BUF RD-POS @ + C@ RD-IFS-WS? ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE 1 RD-POS +! REPEAT ;

: RD-ASSIGN ( name --- )   \ READ-FIELD, as built, to that variable
  0 READ-FIELD RD-FLEN @ + C!
  READ-FIELD SET-SHVAR ;

VARIABLE RD-LAST-ESC   \ where the last escaped character landed, or -1

: RD-FIELD ( last? --- )   \ one field from RD-POS, escapes removed
  0 RD-FLEN !  -1 RD-LAST-ESC !
  BEGIN
    RD-POS @ RD-LEN @ < IF
      DUP IF -1 ELSE READ-BUF RD-POS @ + C@ IFS-CHAR? 0= THEN
    ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    RD-ESCAPED? IF
      1 RD-POS +!
      RD-POS @ RD-LEN @ < IF
        RD-FLEN @ RD-LAST-ESC !
        READ-BUF RD-POS @ + C@ RD-EMIT  1 RD-POS +!
      THEN
    ELSE
      READ-BUF RD-POS @ + C@ RD-EMIT  1 RD-POS +!
    THEN
  REPEAT
  DROP ;

: RD-TRIM-WS ( n --- n' )   \ n, less READ-FIELD's trailing IFS whitespace
  BEGIN DUP 0 > IF DUP 1- READ-FIELD + C@ RD-IFS-WS? ELSE 0 THEN WHILE 1- REPEAT ;

: RD-HAS-IFS? ( n --- f )   \ an IFS character in READ-FIELD's first n bytes
  0 ?DO READ-FIELD I + C@ IFS-CHAR? IF -1 UNLOOP EXIT THEN LOOP 0 ;

: RD-TRIM-FIELD ( --- )   \ the last variable's remainder, as POSIX trims it
  \ Trailing IFS whitespace goes; and when what is left is ONE field
  \ closed by a single non-whitespace delimiter, the delimiter goes too:
  \ `b:` gives b and `b :` gives b, while `b::` and `b:c:` hold more
  \ than one field and keep everything. This did only the first step,
  \ so `echo :: | IFS=": " read x y` left y as ":" where dash and bash
  \ leave it empty - busybox's many_ifs found it, 1045 of its 6856
  \ cases (Iteration 422). A delimiter that was escaped is text.
  RD-FLEN @ RD-TRIM-WS RD-FLEN !
  RD-FLEN @ 0= IF EXIT THEN
  RD-FLEN @ 1-                                    ( m: the last character )
  DUP READ-FIELD + C@ DUP IFS-CHAR? SWAP RD-IFS-WS? 0= AND
  OVER RD-LAST-ESC @ <> AND 0= IF DROP EXIT THEN   \ not a bare closing delimiter
  RD-TRIM-WS
  DUP RD-HAS-IFS? IF DROP EXIT THEN                \ more than one field: all stay
  RD-FLEN ! ;

: RD-DELIMITER ( --- )   \ past one delimiter and the whitespace around it
  RD-SKIP-IFS-WS
  RD-POS @ RD-LEN @ < IF
    READ-BUF RD-POS @ + C@ IFS-CHAR? IF 1 RD-POS +!  RD-SKIP-IFS-WS THEN
  THEN ;

CREATE EMPTY-STRING CSTR," "   \ for `read` at end of input

: DO-READ ( --- )
  \ XCU's read: fields split by IFS (not just blanks, as this did), a
  \ backslash quoting the next character and joining lines unless -r, and
  \ status 1 when the line ended at end of file (Iteration 288).
  0 VALUE-FAILED? !  0 IFS-CACHED? !
  0 RD-RAW? !  1 RD-VI !
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    S" -r" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF -1 RD-RAW? !  2 RD-VI ! THEN
  THEN
  RD-GET-LINE 0= IF
    \ End of input with nothing read: POSIX still assigns - to the empty
    \ string - and leaving the variables alone made the common idiom
    \ `while read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]` loop for ever (Iteration 327).
    RD-VI @ ARGC @ < IF
      RD-VI @ BEGIN DUP ARGC @ < WHILE
        DUP ARGV@ EMPTY-STRING SET-SHVAR
        1+
      REPEAT DROP
    THEN
    1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  0 RD-POS !
  ARGC @ RD-VI @ > 0= IF
    RD-EOF? @ IF 1 ELSE 0 THEN LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  RD-SKIP-IFS-WS
  BEGIN RD-VI @ ARGC @ < WHILE
    RD-VI @ 1+ ARGC @ < 0=                   ( last? )
    DUP RD-FIELD
    IF RD-TRIM-FIELD ELSE RD-DELIMITER THEN
    RD-VI @ ARGV@ RD-ASSIGN
    1 RD-VI +!
  REPEAT
  VALUE-FAILED? @ IF 2 ELSE RD-EOF? @ IF 1 ELSE 0 THEN THEN LAST-STATUS ! ;

64 BUFFER: GO-NMBUF
8  BUFFER: GO-VALBUF
VARIABLE GO-CPOS      \ character position within the current argument
VARIABLE GO-LASTIND   \ OPTIND as this word last left it
VARIABLE GO-IND
VARIABLE GO-OPTS
VARIABLE GO-NAME
VARIABLE GO-ARG
VARIABLE GO-C
VARIABLE GO-J
VARIABLE GO-POS
VARIABLE GO-NMLEN

: >NM ( c-addr u --- c-addr' )
  \ NUL-terminated copy of an S" literal, for the shell-variable words
  \ which all take C strings.
  DUP GO-NMLEN !
  GO-NMBUF SWAP MOVE
  0 GO-NMBUF GO-NMLEN @ + C!
  GO-NMBUF ;

: GO-OPTIND@ ( --- n )
  \ At least 1. GO-SLOT counts from 1, so `OPTIND=0` asked for
  \ positional parameter -1, before the start of the table, and getopts
  \ segfaulted; dash takes 0 - or anything below 1, or not a number - as
  \ a fresh start (Iteration 421, found by the busybox triage:
  \ ash-getopts/getopt_optarg, and pinned with gdb to DO-GETOPTS).
  \ And a value that is not a number is an error, not an index: `abc`
  \ read as 5451. 0 means 0 if returned: DO-GETOPTS stops, status 2.
  S" OPTIND" >NM LOOKUP-VAR
  DUP 0= IF DROP 1 EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN 0= IF DROP 1 EXIT THEN
  DUP DUP CSTRLEN DIGITS-ONLY? 0= IF
    S" shell: getopts: OPTIND: " ERR-TYPE ERR-CSTR S" : bad number" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    0 EXIT
  THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL 1 MAX ;

: GO-OPTIND! ( n --- )   N>STR S" OPTIND" >NM SWAP SET-SHVAR ;
: GO-OPTARG! ( c-addr --- )  S" OPTARG" >NM SWAP SET-SHVAR ;

: GO-SETNAME ( c --- )   \ set the user's variable to one character
  GO-VALBUF C!  0 GO-VALBUF 1+ C!
  GO-NAME @ GO-VALBUF SET-SHVAR ;

: GO-FIND-OPT ( c --- pos | -1 )
  GO-C !
  0 GO-J !
  BEGIN GO-J @ GO-OPTS @ CSTRLEN < WHILE
    GO-OPTS @ GO-J @ + C@ GO-C @ = IF GO-J @ EXIT THEN
    GO-J @ 1+ GO-J !
  REPEAT
  -1 ;

: GO-DONE ( c --- )   \ no more options: set the variable and return 1
  GO-SETNAME
  GO-IND @ GO-OPTIND!
  GO-IND @ GO-LASTIND !
  1 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: GO-CLEAR-OPTARG ( --- )
  \ An option that takes no argument leaves OPTARG unset, so a value
  \ from a previous iteration must not linger - "opt=a arg=val" where
  \ the val belonged to the -c before it.
  0 GO-VALBUF C!
  S" OPTARG" >NM GO-VALBUF SET-SHVAR ;

: GO-STEP ( --- )   \ finished with this character; advance if at end
  GO-CPOS @ 1+ GO-CPOS !
  GO-ARG @ GO-CPOS @ + C@ 0= IF
    GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
    1 GO-CPOS !
  THEN ;

: GO-FINISH ( --- )
  GO-IND @ GO-OPTIND!
  GO-IND @ GO-LASTIND !
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

VARIABLE GO-OPERANDS?   \ arguments were given to getopts itself

: GO-COUNT ( --- n )   \ how many words getopts is walking
  GO-OPERANDS? @ IF ARGC @ 3 - ELSE POS-PARAM-COUNT @ THEN ;

: GO-SLOT ( i --- c-addr )   \ the i'th of them, counting from 1
  GO-OPERANDS? @ IF 2 + ARGV@ ELSE 1- POS-PARAM-SLOT THEN ;

CREATE GO-CHARBUF 2 ALLOT

: GO-CHAR-BUF ( c --- c-addr )   \ that one character, NUL-terminated
  GO-CHARBUF C!  0 GO-CHARBUF 1+ C!  GO-CHARBUF ;

: GO-OPTARG-CHAR! ( c --- )   \ OPTARG = that one character
  GO-CHAR-BUF GO-OPTARG! ;

: DO-GETOPTS ( --- )
  \ getopts OPTSTRING NAME - parse the positional parameters one
  \ option at a time, per POSIX. OPTIND is a shell variable so a
  \ script can reset it; GO-CPOS tracks the position *within* a
  \ clustered argument like "-abc" and is reset whenever OPTIND
  \ changes underneath us, which is how "OPTIND=1" restarts parsing.
  ARGC @ 3 < IF 2 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  1 ARGV@ GO-OPTS !
  2 ARGV@ GO-NAME !
  \ `getopts optstring name [arg...]`: the operands stand in for the
  \ positional parameters, which this ignored until Iteration 328.
  ARGC @ 3 > IF -1 ELSE 0 THEN GO-OPERANDS? !
  GO-OPTIND@ ?DUP 0= IF 2 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN GO-IND !
  GO-IND @ GO-LASTIND @ <> IF 1 GO-CPOS ! THEN
  GO-CPOS @ 0= IF 1 GO-CPOS ! THEN
  BEGIN
    GO-IND @ GO-COUNT > IF 63 GO-DONE EXIT THEN
    GO-IND @ GO-SLOT GO-ARG !
    GO-ARG @ C@ 45 <> IF 63 GO-DONE EXIT THEN
    GO-ARG @ CSTRLEN 1 = IF 63 GO-DONE EXIT THEN
    GO-ARG @ CSTRLEN 2 = GO-ARG @ 1+ C@ 45 = AND IF
      GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND ! 63 GO-DONE EXIT
    THEN
    GO-ARG @ GO-CPOS @ + C@ GO-C !
    GO-C @ 0= IF
      GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
      1 GO-CPOS !
    ELSE
      GO-C @ GO-FIND-OPT GO-POS !
      GO-POS @ -1 = IF
        GO-OPTS @ C@ 58 = IF
          GO-C @ GO-OPTARG-CHAR!                 \ silent mode names it too
        ELSE
          GO-CLEAR-OPTARG
          \ Outside silent mode getopts reports the unknown option, in
          \ dash's words; this said nothing at all (Iteration 346).
          S" Illegal option -" ERR-TYPE  GO-C @ GO-CHAR-BUF ERR-CSTR  ERR-NL
        THEN
        63 GO-SETNAME GO-STEP GO-FINISH EXIT
      THEN
      GO-OPTS @ GO-POS @ 1+ + C@ 58 = IF
        \ this option takes an argument: the rest of this word if
        \ there is any, otherwise the whole of the next one
        GO-CPOS @ 1+ GO-CPOS !
        GO-ARG @ GO-CPOS @ + C@ 0= IF
          GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
          GO-IND @ GO-COUNT > IF
            \ A missing argument in silent mode names the option in OPTARG
            \ (XCU getopts); this left OPTARG empty until Iteration 328.
            GO-OPTS @ C@ 58 = IF
              GO-C @ GO-OPTARG-CHAR!  58 GO-SETNAME
            ELSE
              S" No arg for -" ERR-TYPE
              GO-C @ GO-CHAR-BUF ERR-CSTR
              S"  option" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
              GO-CLEAR-OPTARG  63 GO-SETNAME
            THEN
          ELSE
            GO-IND @ GO-SLOT GO-OPTARG!
            GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
            GO-C @ GO-SETNAME
          THEN
        ELSE
          GO-ARG @ GO-CPOS @ + GO-OPTARG!
          GO-IND @ 1+ GO-IND !
          GO-C @ GO-SETNAME
        THEN
        1 GO-CPOS !
        GO-FINISH EXIT
      THEN
      GO-CLEAR-OPTARG GO-C @ GO-SETNAME GO-STEP GO-FINISH EXIT
    THEN
  0 UNTIL ;

DEFER KILL-JOB-PID   ( c-addr --- pid | 0 )   \ the job table is below
DEFER JOB-STATUS-OF-CALL  ( pid --- status | -1 )
DEFER JOB-FORGET-PID-CALL ( pid --- )
VARIABLE DW-PID
DEFER JOB-MARK-DONE-CALL   ( pid raw --- )   \ the job table is below

: DO-WAIT ( --- )
  \ wait [pid]: with an operand, wait for that child; with none, wait
  \ for all of them. A shell with no children at all is not an error.
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  \ Whatever `wait` reaps is recorded in the job table, so the notice
  \ still appears at the next prompt (Iteration 324). Variables, because
  \ the pid has to outlive the status.
  ARGC @ 1 > IF
    \ `wait %1` names a job, as `kill` does (Iteration 345). An unknown
    \ job is an error, and a job this shell has already reaped keeps its
    \ status in the table - `wait` reports that rather than 0.
    ARGV 1 CELLS + @ DUP C@ 37 = IF
      KILL-JOB-PID ?DUP 0= IF
        S" shell: wait: no such job" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
        127 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
      THEN
      DW-PID !
    ELSE
      DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL DW-PID !
    THEN
    DW-PID @ JOB-STATUS-OF-CALL DUP 0< 0= IF
      LAST-STATUS !  DW-PID @ JOB-FORGET-PID-CALL EXIT
    THEN DROP
    DW-PID @ WAITPID                           ( raw ior )
    0= IF
      DUP WAIT->STATUS LAST-STATUS !
      DW-PID @ SWAP JOB-MARK-DONE-CALL
    ELSE DROP THEN
    EXIT
  THEN
  BEGIN
    -1 WAIT-JOB                                ( pid raw )
    OVER 0< 0= WHILE
    DUP 127 AND 127 = IF 2DROP EXIT THEN       \ stopped: it stays a job
    DUP WAIT->STATUS LAST-STATUS !
    JOB-MARK-DONE-CALL                         ( consumes pid and raw )
  REPEAT
  2DROP
  \ `wait` with no operands waits for every child and yields 0 (XCU
  \ wait); this reported the last child's status (Iteration 345).
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ;   \ the job stays until the prompt reports it (Iteration 320)


\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ ulimit - POSIX specifies exactly one resource, the file size limit,
\ in 512-byte blocks. GETFSIZE/SETFSIZE (engine primitives, Iteration
\ 125) already work in blocks for that reason, so there is no
\ conversion here. bash reports 1024-byte blocks instead, which is why
\ mrsh's ulimit.sh passes under sh and fails under bash - see
\ PROGRESS.md's Iteration 124 entry.
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------




\ ---- ulimit (Iteration 308) ----
\ It handled only -f, through SETFSIZE. GETRLIMIT and SETRLIMIT (the new
\ escaped primitives) give the rest. The units are POSIX's: -f and -c
\ count 512-byte blocks, -d -l -m -s -v kilobytes, -t seconds, and -n -u
\ are plain counts.

VARIABLE UL-RES        \ which resource
VARIABLE UL-SCALE      \ the operand's unit, in bytes (1 for a count)
VARIABLE UL-HARD?      \ -H
VARIABLE UL-SOFT?      \ -S
VARIABLE UL-I
VARIABLE UL-NEW
VARIABLE UL-SOFT-CUR
VARIABLE UL-HARD-CUR

: UL-SELECT ( c --- f )   \ the resource letter, into UL-RES and UL-SCALE
  1 UL-SCALE !
  DUP 116 = IF DROP 0 UL-RES ! -1 EXIT THEN            \ -t  cpu seconds
  DUP 102 = IF DROP 1 UL-RES ! 512 UL-SCALE ! -1 EXIT THEN   \ -f  file size
  DUP 100 = IF DROP 2 UL-RES ! 1024 UL-SCALE ! -1 EXIT THEN  \ -d  data
  DUP 115 = IF DROP 3 UL-RES ! 1024 UL-SCALE ! -1 EXIT THEN  \ -s  stack
  DUP 99  = IF DROP 4 UL-RES ! 512 UL-SCALE ! -1 EXIT THEN   \ -c  core
  DUP 109 = IF DROP 5 UL-RES ! 1024 UL-SCALE ! -1 EXIT THEN  \ -m  resident
  DUP 117 = IF DROP 6 UL-RES ! -1 EXIT THEN             \ -u  processes
  DUP 110 = IF DROP 7 UL-RES ! -1 EXIT THEN             \ -n  open files
  DUP 108 = IF DROP 8 UL-RES ! 1024 UL-SCALE ! -1 EXIT THEN  \ -l  locked
  118 = IF 9 UL-RES ! 1024 UL-SCALE ! -1 EXIT THEN      \ -v  address space
  0 ;

: .UL-VALUE ( v --- )   \ -1 is no limit
  DUP -1 = IF DROP S" unlimited" TYPE EXIT THEN
  UL-SCALE @ / 0 <# #S #> TYPE ;

: UL-REPORT ( --- )
  UL-RES @ GETRLIMIT                         ( soft hard ior )
  IF 2DROP S" ulimit: cannot read limit" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  UL-HARD? @ IF NIP ELSE DROP THEN           ( value )
  .UL-VALUE CR ;

: UL-APPLY ( value --- )
  \ POSIX: -H sets the hard limit, -S the soft one, neither sets both.
  \ Written with variables after a stack-juggled version crashed.
  UL-NEW !
  UL-RES @ GETRLIMIT                         ( soft hard ior )
  IF 2DROP S" ulimit: cannot read limit" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  UL-HARD-CUR !  UL-SOFT-CUR !
  UL-HARD? @ UL-SOFT? @ OR 0= IF
    UL-NEW @ UL-NEW @                        ( soft hard )
  ELSE
    UL-HARD? @ IF
      UL-SOFT-CUR @ UL-NEW @
    ELSE
      UL-NEW @ UL-HARD-CUR @
    THEN
  THEN
  UL-RES @ SETRLIMIT IF
    S" ulimit: cannot set limit" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 1 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

: UL-PARSE-VALUE ( c-addr --- v )
  \ STR= takes all four: keep a copy of the address for the number path
  DUP  DUP CSTRLEN S" unlimited" STR= IF DROP -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL UL-SCALE @ * ;

: DO-ULIMIT ( --- )
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  102 UL-SELECT DROP                         \ -f is the default resource
  0 UL-HARD? !  0 UL-SOFT? !
  1 UL-I !
  BEGIN
    UL-I @ ARGC @ < IF UL-I @ ARGV@ C@ 45 = ELSE 0 THEN
  WHILE
    UL-I @ ARGV@ 1+ C@                       ( letter )
    DUP 72 = IF DROP -1 UL-HARD? ! ELSE
    DUP 83 = IF DROP -1 UL-SOFT? ! ELSE
      UL-SELECT 0= IF
        S" ulimit: bad option" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 2 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
      THEN
    THEN THEN
    UL-I @ 1+ UL-I !
  REPEAT
  UL-I @ ARGC @ < IF
    UL-I @ ARGV@ UL-PARSE-VALUE UL-APPLY
  ELSE
    UL-REPORT
  THEN ;

' DO-WAIT         S" wait"     BUILTIN
' DO-ULIMIT       S" ulimit"   BUILTIN
' DO-GETOPTS      S" getopts"  BUILTIN
' DO-READ         S" read"     BUILTIN
' DO-FORTH        S" forth"    BUILTIN
' DO-COLON        S" :"        BUILTIN
' DO-TEST         S" test"     BUILTIN
' DO-BRACKET-TEST S" ["        BUILTIN
' DO-CD           S" cd"       BUILTIN
' DO-PWD          S" pwd"      BUILTIN

\ ==================================================================
\ true, false, echo, printf (Iteration 269)
\ ==================================================================
\ Programs until now: a fork and an exec, about 800 microseconds, where
\ dash spends one to three (DASH-COMPARISON.md). echo follows the
\ echo scripts here have always run, /bin/echo, which is also bash's:
\ options -n -e -E, escapes only with -e. printf follows POSIX and
\ GNU: %s %b %c %d %i %u %o %x %X %%, flags - 0 + space, width and
\ precision (either may be *), the format reused while arguments remain.

: /STRING ( c-addr u n --- c-addr' u' )   ROT OVER + ROT ROT - ;

: DO-TRUE ( --- )    0 LAST-STATUS ! ;
: DO-FALSE ( --- )   1 LAST-STATUS ! ;

\ ---- backslash escapes, for echo -e, printf's format and %b ----
VARIABLE EA            \ the string being written
VARIABLE EU
VARIABLE ESC-STOP?     \ \c: nothing more is written
VARIABLE ESC-MODE      \ 0: printf's format (\NNN, \"); 1: echo and %b (\0NNN)
VARIABLE OV
VARIABLE ON

: EC ( i --- c | -1 )   DUP EU @ < IF EA @ + C@ ELSE DROP -1 THEN ;
: OCTAL-RUN ( i max --- )   \ OV: the value of up to max octal digits at i; ON: how many
  0 OV !  0 ON !
  0 ?DO
    DUP I + EC DUP 48 - 8 U< 0= IF DROP LEAVE THEN
    48 - OV @ 8 * + OV !  1 ON +!
  LOOP DROP ;
: HEXV ( c --- n | -1 )
  DUP 48 - 10 U< IF 48 - EXIT THEN
  32 OR DUP 97 - 6 U< IF 87 - EXIT THEN
  DROP -1 ;
: HEX-RUN ( i max --- )
  0 OV !  0 ON !
  0 ?DO
    DUP I + EC HEXV DUP 0< IF DROP LEAVE THEN
    OV @ 16 * + OV !  1 ON +!
  LOOP DROP ;

: EMIT-ESCAPE ( i --- i' )   \ i is at a backslash
  DUP 1+ EC
  DUP -1 = IF DROP 92 EMIT 1+ EXIT THEN
  DUP 97 = IF DROP 7 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 98 = IF DROP 8 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 99 = IF DROP -1 ESC-STOP? ! 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 101 = IF DROP 27 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 102 = IF DROP 12 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 110 = IF DROP 10 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 114 = IF DROP 13 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 116 = IF DROP 9 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 118 = IF DROP 11 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 92 = IF DROP 92 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  DUP 120 = IF
    DROP DUP 2 + 2 HEX-RUN
    ON @ IF OV @ EMIT 2 + ON @ + ELSE 92 EMIT 1+ THEN EXIT
  THEN
  ESC-MODE @ IF
    DUP 48 = IF DROP DUP 2 + 3 OCTAL-RUN OV @ 255 AND EMIT 2 + ON @ + EXIT THEN
  ELSE
    DUP 48 - 8 U< IF DROP DUP 1+ 3 OCTAL-RUN OV @ 255 AND EMIT 1+ ON @ + EXIT THEN
    DUP 34 = IF DROP 34 EMIT 2 + EXIT THEN
  THEN
  DROP 92 EMIT 1+ ;                          \ unknown: the backslash stays

: EMIT-ESCAPED ( c-addr u --- )   \ until the end, or a \c
  EU !  EA !  0
  BEGIN DUP EU @ < ESC-STOP? @ 0= AND WHILE
    DUP EC 92 = IF EMIT-ESCAPE ELSE DUP EC EMIT 1+ THEN
  REPEAT DROP ;

\ ---- echo ----
VARIABLE EO-I
VARIABLE EO-NONL?
VARIABLE EO-ESC?

: ECHO-OPTION? ( c-addr --- f )   \ - followed only by n, e and E
  DUP C@ 45 <> IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  1+ DUP C@ 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  BEGIN DUP C@ WHILE
    DUP C@ DUP 110 = OVER 101 = OR SWAP 69 = OR 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
    1+
  REPEAT DROP -1 ;

: DO-ECHO ( --- )
  0 EO-NONL? !  0 EO-ESC? !  0 ESC-STOP? !  1 ESC-MODE !
  1 EO-I !
  BEGIN EO-I @ ARGC @ < IF EO-I @ ARGV@ ECHO-OPTION? ELSE 0 THEN WHILE
    EO-I @ ARGV@ 1+
    BEGIN DUP C@ WHILE
      DUP C@ DUP 110 = IF -1 EO-NONL? ! THEN
      DUP 101 = IF -1 EO-ESC? ! THEN
      69 = IF 0 EO-ESC? ! THEN
      1+
    REPEAT DROP
    1 EO-I +!
  REPEAT
  BEGIN EO-I @ ARGC @ < ESC-STOP? @ 0= AND WHILE
    EO-I @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN
    EO-ESC? @ IF EMIT-ESCAPED ELSE TYPE THEN
    1 EO-I +!
    EO-I @ ARGC @ < ESC-STOP? @ 0= AND IF 32 EMIT THEN
  REPEAT
  EO-NONL? @ ESC-STOP? @ OR 0= IF CR THEN
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

\ ---- printf ----
VARIABLE PF-ARG        \ the next argument
VARIABLE PF-STATUS
VARIABLE PF-LEFT
VARIABLE PF-ZERO
VARIABLE PF-PLUS
VARIABLE PF-SPACE
VARIABLE PF-WIDTH
VARIABLE PF-PREC       \ -1: none
VARIABLE PF-START      \ where the conversion being read began
VARIABLE PF-UPPER      \ %X
CREATE NUL-BYTE CSTR," "
256 BUFFER: PF-BUF     \ a formatted number
VARIABLE PF#

: NEXT-ARG ( --- c-addr u )   \ the next argument, or an empty string
  PF-ARG @ ARGC @ < IF
    PF-ARG @ ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN  1 PF-ARG +!
  ELSE
    SH-NL 0
  THEN ;

: PF-BAD-NUMBER ( c-addr u --- )
  S" printf: " ERR-TYPE ERR-TYPE S" : invalid number" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  1 PF-STATUS ! ;

VARIABLE PN-A
VARIABLE PN-U
: PARSE-PF-NUMBER ( c-addr u --- n )
  \ decimal, 0NNN octal, 0xNN hex, or 'c / "c for a character's code
  2DUP PN-U ! PN-A !
  BEGIN DUP IF OVER C@ WS? ELSE 0 THEN WHILE 1 /STRING REPEAT
  DUP 0= IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  OVER C@ DUP 39 = SWAP 34 = OR IF
    DUP 1 > IF DROP 1+ C@ ELSE 2DROP 0 THEN EXIT
  THEN
  1 >R                                       \ the sign
  OVER C@ 45 = IF R> DROP -1 >R 1 /STRING ELSE
  OVER C@ 43 = IF 1 /STRING THEN THEN
  10 >R                                      \ the base
  DUP 1 > IF OVER C@ 48 = IF
    OVER 1+ C@ 32 OR 120 = IF R> DROP 16 >R 2 /STRING ELSE R> DROP 8 >R 1 /STRING THEN
  THEN THEN
  0 ROT ROT                                  ( value c-addr u )
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    OVER C@ HEXV DUP 0< OVER R@ < 0= OR IF
      DROP PN-A @ PN-U @ PF-BAD-NUMBER
      2DROP R> DROP R> * EXIT
    THEN
    >R ROT R> SWAP R@ * + ROT ROT
    1 /STRING
  REPEAT
  2DROP R> DROP R> * ;

: PF-DIGITS ( i --- i' n )   \ a decimal number in the format
  0 BEGIN OVER EC DUP 48 - 10 U< WHILE 48 - SWAP 10 * + SWAP 1+ SWAP REPEAT
  DROP ;

: PF-C, ( c --- )   PF# @ 1+ ENSURE-BUFFER PF-BUF  PF-BUF PF# @ + C!  1 PF# +! ;
: PF-PAD ( c-addr u --- )   \ written in the field's width
  PF-WIDTH @ OVER - 0 MAX
  PF-LEFT @ IF >R TYPE R> SPACES ELSE SPACES TYPE THEN ;

: PF-INTEGER ( n base signed? upper? --- )
  PF-UPPER !  >R >R                          ( n  R: signed base )
  BASE @ SWAP R> BASE !                      ( oldbase n  R: signed )
  0 PF# !
  R@ IF DUP 0< IF 45 PF-C, NEGATE ELSE
    PF-PLUS @ IF 43 PF-C, ELSE PF-SPACE @ IF 32 PF-C, THEN THEN
  THEN THEN
  R> DROP
  0 <# #S #>                                 ( oldbase addr len  R: upper )
  \ the precision is a minimum number of digits
  PF-PREC @ OVER - 0 MAX 0 ?DO 48 PF-C, LOOP
  \ zero padding goes after the sign, when there is no precision
  PF-ZERO @ PF-LEFT @ 0= AND PF-PREC @ 0< AND IF
    PF-WIDTH @ PF# @ - OVER - 0 MAX 0 ?DO 48 PF-C, LOOP
  THEN
  0 ?DO
    DUP I + C@ PF-UPPER @ 0= IF DUP 65 - 26 U< IF 32 + THEN THEN PF-C,
  LOOP DROP
  BASE !
  PF-BUF PF# @ PF-PAD ;

: PF-NUMBER-ARG ( --- n )   NEXT-ARG PARSE-PF-NUMBER ;

: PF-CONVERSION ( i --- i' )   \ i is at a %
  DUP PF-START !
  1+ DUP EC 37 = IF 37 EMIT 1+ EXIT THEN
  0 PF-LEFT !  0 PF-ZERO !  0 PF-PLUS !  0 PF-SPACE !  0 PF-WIDTH !  -1 PF-PREC !
  BEGIN
    DUP EC DUP 45 = OVER 48 = OR OVER 43 = OR OVER 32 = OR SWAP 35 = OR
  WHILE
    DUP EC
    DUP 45 = IF -1 PF-LEFT ! THEN
    DUP 48 = IF -1 PF-ZERO ! THEN
    DUP 43 = IF -1 PF-PLUS ! THEN
    32 = IF -1 PF-SPACE ! THEN
    1+
  REPEAT
  DUP EC 42 = IF
    1+ PF-NUMBER-ARG DUP 0< IF NEGATE -1 PF-LEFT ! THEN PF-WIDTH !
  ELSE
    PF-DIGITS PF-WIDTH !
  THEN
  DUP EC 46 = IF
    1+ DUP EC 42 = IF 1+ PF-NUMBER-ARG ELSE PF-DIGITS THEN
    DUP 0< IF DROP -1 THEN PF-PREC !
  THEN
  DUP EC
  DUP 115 = IF DROP                          \ %s
    NEXT-ARG PF-PREC @ 0< 0= IF PF-PREC @ MIN THEN PF-PAD 1+ EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 98 = IF DROP                           \ %b
    EA @ >R EU @ >R
    1 ESC-MODE !  NEXT-ARG EMIT-ESCAPED  0 ESC-MODE !
    R> EU ! R> EA !
    1+ EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 99 = IF DROP                           \ %c: a NUL for an empty argument
    NEXT-ARG ?DUP IF 1 MIN ELSE DROP NUL-BYTE 1 THEN PF-PAD 1+ EXIT
  THEN
  DUP 100 = OVER 105 = OR IF DROP  PF-NUMBER-ARG 10 -1 0 PF-INTEGER 1+ EXIT THEN
  DUP 117 = IF DROP  PF-NUMBER-ARG 10 0 0 PF-INTEGER 1+ EXIT THEN
  DUP 111 = IF DROP  PF-NUMBER-ARG 8 0 0 PF-INTEGER 1+ EXIT THEN
  DUP 120 = IF DROP  PF-NUMBER-ARG 16 0 0 PF-INTEGER 1+ EXIT THEN
  DUP 88 = IF DROP  PF-NUMBER-ARG 16 0 -1 PF-INTEGER 1+ EXIT THEN
  DROP
  S" printf: " ERR-TYPE
  EA @ PF-START @ +  OVER 1+ EU @ MIN PF-START @ -  ERR-TYPE
  S" : invalid conversion specification" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
  1 PF-STATUS !  -1 ESC-STOP? !
  DROP EU @ ;

: PF-FORMAT ( c-addr u --- )   \ the format, once
  EU !  EA !  0 ESC-MODE !
  0
  BEGIN DUP EU @ < ESC-STOP? @ 0= AND WHILE
    DUP EC DUP 92 = IF DROP EMIT-ESCAPE ELSE
      37 = IF PF-CONVERSION ELSE DUP EC EMIT 1+ THEN
    THEN
  REPEAT DROP ;

: DO-PRINTF ( --- )
  ARGC @ 2 < IF
    S" printf: usage: printf format [arguments]" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    2 LAST-STATUS !  -1 VALUE-FAILED? !      \ the status the caller reports EXIT
  THEN
  0 PF-STATUS !  0 ESC-STOP? !  2 PF-ARG !
  BEGIN
    PF-ARG @ >R
    1 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN PF-FORMAT
    \ again while arguments remain and the last pass used some
    ESC-STOP? @ 0=  PF-ARG @ ARGC @ < AND  PF-ARG @ R> > AND 0=
  UNTIL
  PF-STATUS @ LAST-STATUS ! ;

' DO-TRUE         S" true"     BUILTIN
' DO-FALSE        S" false"    BUILTIN
' DO-ECHO         S" echo"     BUILTIN
' DO-PRINTF       S" printf"   BUILTIN

VARIABLE KEEP-REDIRECTS?   \ exec with no command: its redirections stay (END-REDIRECT)

\ ---- exec and hash (Iteration 270) ----
: DROP-ARGV0 ( --- )   \ the command's words without its name
  ARGC @ 1 ?DO
    I ARGV@  ARGV I 1- CELLS + !
    I ARGV-QUOTED + C@  ARGV-QUOTED I 1- + C!
  LOOP
  ARGC @ 1- ARGC !  0 ARGV ARGC @ CELLS + ! ;

: DO-EXEC ( --- )
  \ Without a command, the redirections stay (END-REDIRECT keeps them).
  \ With one, it replaces the shell: found as any command is, executed
  \ as a program even if a builtin has the name - as bash and dash do.
  ARGC @ 2 < IF -1 KEEP-REDIRECTS? !  0 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  DROP-ARGV0
  ARGV @ DUP CSTRLEN HAS-SLASH? IF 0 ELSE ARGV @ LOCATE-COMMAND THEN CMD-RESOLVED !
  RUN-CHILD ;                                \ EXECVE flushes the output first

: DO-HASH ( --- )
  \ hash: the remembered paths; hash -r: forget them; hash NAME...:
  \ look them up now. (dash's forms.)
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ARGC @ 1 = IF
    CC-CHECK
    0 BEGIN DUP CC# @ < WHILE
      DUP CC-TEXT + DUP CSTRLEN + 1+        ( off path )
      DUP DUP CSTRLEN TYPE CR
      DUP CSTRLEN + 1+ CC-TEXT - NIP
    REPEAT DROP EXIT
  THEN
  1 ARGV@ S" -r" ROT STR0= IF 0 CC# ! EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 1 ?DO
    I ARGV@ LOCATE-COMMAND 0= IF
      S" shell: hash: " ERR-TYPE I ARGV@ ERR-CSTR S" : not found" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
      1 LAST-STATUS !
    THEN
  LOOP ;

' DO-EXEC         S" exec"     BUILTIN
' DO-HASH         S" hash"     BUILTIN

\ ---- trap, kill, umask, times, local (Iteration 271) ----
CREATE SH-DASH  CSTR," -"
: ALL-DECIMAL? ( c-addr u --- f )
  DUP 0= IF 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  OVER + SWAP ?DO I C@ 48 - 10 U< 0= IF UNLOOP 0 EXIT THEN LOOP -1 ;
: STRING-PREFIX? ( c-addr u prefix pu --- f )
  ROT OVER < IF DROP 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  TUCK COMPARE 0= ;
: PARSE-SIGNED ( c-addr u --- n )
  DUP IF OVER C@ 45 = IF 1 /STRING PARSE-DECIMAL NEGATE EXIT THEN THEN
  PARSE-DECIMAL ;

\ signal names 0..31, space-separated; 0 is EXIT
CREATE SIG-NAMES-TEXT
  S" EXIT HUP INT QUIT ILL TRAP ABRT BUS FPE KILL USR1 SEGV USR2 PIPE ALRM TERM STKFLT CHLD CONT STOP TSTP TTIN TTOU URG XCPU XFSZ VTALRM PROF WINCH IO PWR SYS "
  DUP C, HERE SWAP DUP ALLOT MOVE
: SIG-NAME ( n --- c-addr u | 0 0 )   \ the nth name
  DUP 0< OVER 31 > OR IF DROP 0 0 EXIT THEN
  SIG-NAMES-TEXT COUNT                      ( n a u )
  ROT 0 ?DO 32 SCAN 1 /STRING LOOP          ( a' u' )
  2DUP 32 SCAN NIP - ;

64 BUFFER: SIG-UPPER
: PARSE-SIG ( c-addr --- n | -1 )   \ a number, or a name with or without SIG
  DUP C@ 48 - 10 U< IF
    \ PARSE-DECIMAL takes both of them: the NIP here ate one of the
    \ CALLER's stack items, and the 2DROP DROP below one too many.
    \ `trap 'x' 2` wrecked the shell's own stack, and the damage showed
    \ up as a return stack overflow in whatever ran next; by name it
    \ never went down this path (Iteration 291).
    DUP CSTRLEN 2DUP ALL-DECIMAL? IF PARSE-DECIMAL DUP 64 > IF DROP -1 THEN EXIT THEN
    2DROP -1 EXIT
  THEN
  DUP CSTRLEN 60 MIN                        ( a u )
  0 ?DO DUP I + C@ DUP 97 - 26 U< IF 32 - THEN SIG-UPPER I + C! LOOP
  CSTRLEN 60 MIN SIG-UPPER SWAP             ( upper u )
  2DUP S" SIG" STRING-PREFIX? IF 3 /STRING THEN
  32 0 DO
    2DUP I SIG-NAME COMPARE 0= IF 2DROP I UNLOOP EXIT THEN
  LOOP
  2DROP -1 ;

: SQUOTED. ( c-addr --- )   \ as a single-quoted shell word
  39 EMIT
  BEGIN DUP C@ WHILE
    DUP C@ 39 = IF S" '" TYPE 34 EMIT 39 EMIT 34 EMIT 39 EMIT ELSE DUP C@ EMIT THEN   \ '"'"' as dash writes it
    1+
  REPEAT DROP
  39 EMIT ;

: LIST-TRAPS ( --- )
  TRAP-SLOTS 0 DO
    I TRAP@ ?DUP IF
      S" trap -- " TYPE
      DUP -1 = IF DROP S" ''" TYPE ELSE SQUOTED. THEN
      32 EMIT
      I SIG-NAME ?DUP IF TYPE ELSE DROP I 0 <# #S #> TYPE THEN CR
    THEN
  LOOP ;

VARIABLE TR-ACTION
: TRAP-SET ( sig --- )   \ TR-ACTION: "-" default, "" ignore, else a command
  DUP TRAP-FREE
  TR-ACTION @ S" -" ROT STR0= IF
    DUP IF 0 SIGNAL-ACTION DROP ELSE DROP THEN EXIT
  THEN
  TR-ACTION @ C@ 0= IF
    -1 OVER TRAP!
    DUP IF 1 SIGNAL-ACTION DROP ELSE DROP THEN EXIT
  THEN
  \ KILL and STOP cannot be caught; bash and dash both take the trap
  \ quietly and let it never fire, so the failure is not reported
  \ (Iteration 291).
  DUP IF DUP 2 SIGNAL-ACTION DROP THEN
  TR-ACTION @ DUP CSTRLEN 1+ DUP ALLOCATE DROP DUP >R SWAP MOVE
  R> SWAP TRAP! ;

: TRAP-BAD ( c-addr --- )
  S" shell: trap: " ERR-TYPE ERR-CSTR S" : bad trap" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL  1 LAST-STATUS ! ;

: DO-TRAP ( --- )
  \ trap                 list the traps
  \ trap action cond...  set them: action "-" resets, "" ignores
  \ trap cond / trap N...  reset (a lone condition, or numbers first)
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  ARGC @ 1 = IF LIST-TRAPS EXIT THEN
  1 ARGV@ S" --" ROT STR0= IF DROP-ARGV0 THEN
  ARGC @ 1 = IF LIST-TRAPS EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ 2 =  1 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN ALL-DECIMAL? OR IF
    SH-DASH TR-ACTION !  1
  ELSE
    1 ARGV@ TR-ACTION !  2
  THEN
  ARGC @ SWAP ?DO
    I ARGV@ PARSE-SIG DUP 0< IF DROP I ARGV@ TRAP-BAD ELSE TRAP-SET THEN
  LOOP ;

VARIABLE KILL-SIG

: DO-KILL ( --- )
  \ kill [-s name | -name | -n] pid...; kill -l [status]
  0 LAST-STATUS !  15 >R  1
  ARGC @ 2 < IF
    R> 2DROP S" shell: kill: usage: kill [-s sig | -sig] pid... | -l [status]" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
    2 LAST-STATUS !  -1 VALUE-FAILED? !      \ the status the caller reports EXIT
  THEN
  DUP ARGV@ S" -l" ROT STR0= IF
    R> 2DROP
    ARGC @ 2 = IF
      32 1 DO I SIG-NAME TYPE I 31 < IF 32 EMIT THEN LOOP CR EXIT
    THEN
    2 ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-DECIMAL DUP 128 > IF 128 - THEN
    SIG-NAME ?DUP IF TYPE CR ELSE DROP 1 LAST-STATUS ! THEN EXIT
  THEN
  DUP ARGV@ S" -s" ROT STR0= IF
    1+ DUP ARGC @ < IF DUP ARGV@ PARSE-SIG ELSE -1 THEN R> DROP >R 1+
  ELSE DUP ARGV@ C@ 45 = IF
    DUP ARGV@ S" --" ROT STR0= IF 1+ ELSE
      DUP ARGV@ 1+ PARSE-SIG R> DROP >R 1+
    THEN
  THEN THEN
  R> DUP KILL-SIG !  0< IF
    DROP S" shell: kill: bad signal" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  DUP ARGC @ < 0= IF DROP S" shell: kill: no process given" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 2 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  ARGC @ SWAP ?DO
    \ a %n job specifier names the job's process group (Iteration 322)
    I ARGV@ KILL-JOB-PID ?DUP IF NEGATE ELSE I ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN PARSE-SIGNED THEN
    KILL-SIG @ KILL IF
      S" shell: kill: " ERR-TYPE I ARGV@ ERR-CSTR S" : no such process" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
      1 LAST-STATUS !
    THEN
  LOOP ;

: .OCT4 ( n --- )   BASE @ >R 8 BASE ! 0 <# # # # # #> TYPE R> BASE ! ;
: .PERMS ( bits --- )   \ rwx of the low three bits
  DUP 4 AND IF 114 EMIT THEN DUP 2 AND IF 119 EMIT THEN 1 AND IF 120 EMIT THEN ;
VARIABLE UM-PERM
VARIABLE UM-SKIP   \ 1 when `-S` precedes the operand
VARIABLE UMS-A        \ the cursor into the symbolic mask
VARIABLE UMS-WHO      \ which of u, g, o this clause is about
VARIABLE UMS-OP       \ =, + or -
VARIABLE UMS-PERM     \ the bits it names

: UMS-WHO-BITS ( c --- bits|0 )
  DUP 117 = IF DROP 448 EXIT THEN            \ u
  DUP 103 = IF DROP  56 EXIT THEN            \ g
  DUP 111 = IF DROP   7 EXIT THEN            \ o
      97  = IF      511 EXIT THEN            \ a
  0 ;

: UMS-PERM-BITS ( c --- bits|0 )
  DUP 114 = IF DROP 292 EXIT THEN            \ r
  DUP 119 = IF DROP 146 EXIT THEN            \ w
      120 = IF       73 EXIT THEN            \ x
  0 ;

: UMS-SPREAD ( n --- bits )   \ one field's 0..7 into all three
  DUP 6 LSHIFT  OVER 3 LSHIFT OR  SWAP OR ;

: UMS-FROM-WHO ( c --- bits | -1 )
  \ `g=u` copies u's CURRENT permissions rather than naming letters
  \ (XCU `chmod`, which `umask` follows). This was rejected as a bad
  \ mask until Iteration 379.
  DUP 117 = IF DROP UM-PERM @ 6 RSHIFT 7 AND UMS-SPREAD EXIT THEN
  DUP 103 = IF DROP UM-PERM @ 3 RSHIFT 7 AND UMS-SPREAD EXIT THEN
      111 = IF      UM-PERM @        7 AND UMS-SPREAD EXIT THEN
  -1 ;

: UMS-OP-CHAR? ( c --- f )
  DUP 61 = OVER 43 = OR SWAP 45 = OR ;

: UMS-APPLY ( --- )
  UMS-PERM @ UMS-WHO @ AND                   ( bits )
  UMS-OP @ 61 = IF                           \ =
    UM-PERM @ UMS-WHO @ INVERT AND OR UM-PERM ! EXIT
  THEN
  UMS-OP @ 43 = IF UM-PERM @ OR UM-PERM ! EXIT THEN
  INVERT UM-PERM @ AND UM-PERM ! ;           \ -

: UMASK-SYMBOLIC ( c-addr --- ok? )   \ u=rwx,g=rx,o= and the +/- forms
  \ A clause is who-letters, then one or more op-and-permission groups:
  \ `u=r+w` is two groups, which the previous version read as one and
  \ refused (Iteration 379).
  UMS-A !
  BEGIN
    0 UMS-WHO !
    BEGIN UMS-A @ C@ UMS-WHO-BITS ?DUP WHILE
      UMS-WHO @ OR UMS-WHO !  1 UMS-A +!
    REPEAT
    UMS-WHO @ 0= IF 511 UMS-WHO ! THEN
    UMS-A @ C@ UMS-OP-CHAR? 0= IF 0 EXIT THEN
    BEGIN UMS-A @ C@ UMS-OP-CHAR? WHILE
      UMS-A @ C@ UMS-OP !  1 UMS-A +!
      UMS-A @ C@ UMS-FROM-WHO DUP -1 <> IF
        UMS-PERM !  1 UMS-A +!
      ELSE
        DROP  0 UMS-PERM !
        BEGIN UMS-A @ C@ UMS-PERM-BITS ?DUP WHILE
          UMS-PERM @ OR UMS-PERM !  1 UMS-A +!
        REPEAT
      THEN
      UMS-APPLY
    REPEAT
    UMS-A @ C@ 44 = WHILE 1 UMS-A +!
  REPEAT
  UMS-A @ C@ 0= ;

: DO-UMASK ( --- )
  0 LAST-STATUS !  0 UM-SKIP !
  0 UMASK DUP UMASK DROP                    ( mask )
  ARGC @ 1 = IF .OCT4 CR EXIT THEN
  \ `-S` with an operand sets the mask and prints nothing: the option
  \ asks for symbolic OUTPUT, and there is no output when a mask is
  \ given (XCU `umask`; Iteration 379).
  1 ARGV@ S" -S" ROT STR0= ARGC @ 2 = AND IF
    INVERT 511 AND
    S" u=" TYPE DUP 6 RSHIFT .PERMS  S" ,g=" TYPE DUP 3 RSHIFT .PERMS
    S" ,o=" TYPE .PERMS CR EXIT
  THEN
  ARGC @ 2 > IF S" -S" 1 ARGV@ STR0= IF 1 UM-SKIP ! THEN THEN
  UM-SKIP @ 1+ ARGV@ DUP C@ 48 - 8 U< IF
    NIP 0 SWAP
    BEGIN DUP C@ WHILE
      DUP C@ 48 - DUP 8 U< 0= IF 2DROP DROP S" shell: umask: bad mask" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
      ROT 8 * + SWAP 1+
    REPEAT DROP
    511 AND UMASK DROP EXIT
  THEN
  SWAP INVERT 511 AND UM-PERM !
  UMASK-SYMBOLIC IF UM-PERM @ INVERT 511 AND UMASK DROP ELSE
    S" shell: umask: bad mask" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 1 LAST-STATUS !
  THEN ;

: .MS ( ms --- )   \ 0m0.012s
  60000 /MOD 0 <# #S #> TYPE 109 EMIT
  1000 /MOD 0 <# #S #> TYPE 46 EMIT
  0 <# # # # #> TYPE 115 EMIT ;
: DO-TIMES ( --- )
  CPU-TIMES 2SWAP SWAP .MS 32 EMIT .MS CR SWAP .MS 32 EMIT .MS CR
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

\ local: a function's variable, restored when the function returns (dash)
256 BUFFER: LOCALS
VARIABLE LOCALS#
: STRDUP ( c-addr u --- block )   \ NUL-terminated heap copy
  DUP 1+ ALLOCATE DROP DUP >R SWAP DUP >R MOVE R> R@ + 0 SWAP C! R> ;
: LOCAL-PUSH ( x --- )
  LOCALS# @ 1+ CELLS ENSURE-BUFFER LOCALS  LOCALS LOCALS# @ CELLS + !  1 LOCALS# +! ;
: LOCAL-POP ( --- x )   -1 LOCALS# +!  LOCALS LOCALS# @ CELLS + @ ;

: LOCALS-RESTORE ( mark --- )
  BEGIN LOCALS# @ OVER > WHILE
    LOCAL-POP LOCAL-POP                     ( name old | name 0 )
    DUP IF 2DUP SET-SHVAR ELSE OVER REMOVE-SHVAR THEN
    ?DUP IF FREE DROP THEN
    FREE DROP
  REPEAT DROP ;

: DO-LOCAL ( --- )
  0 LAST-STATUS !
  FUNC-DEPTH @ 0= IF
    S" shell: local: not in a function" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 1 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT
  THEN
  ARGC @ 1 ?DO
    I ARGV@ DUP CSTRLEN 2DUP 61 SCAN NIP -  ( a namelen )
    2DUP VALID-NAME? 0= IF
      DROP S" shell: local: " ERR-TYPE ERR-CSTR S" : bad variable name" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL
      1 LAST-STATUS !
    ELSE
      2DUP STRDUP                           ( a n name )
      DUP LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP IF DUP CSTRLEN STRDUP ELSE 0 THEN   ( a n name old )
      LOCAL-PUSH DUP LOCAL-PUSH             ( a n name )
      >R + DUP C@ IF 1+ R@ SWAP SET-SHVAR ELSE DROP THEN R> DROP
    THEN
  LOOP ;

\ set with no arguments: the variables, sorted, quoted for reuse
256 BUFFER: SV-ORDER
: SVO-NAME ( k --- c-addr )   CELLS SV-ORDER + @ SHVAR-NAME-SLOT ;
: SV-LESS? ( j k --- f )
  SVO-NAME DUP CSTRLEN ROT SVO-NAME DUP CSTRLEN 2SWAP COMPARE 0< ;
: LIST-VARIABLES ( --- )
  SHVAR-COUNT @ CELLS 1 MAX ENSURE-BUFFER SV-ORDER
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 0 ?DO I SV-ORDER I CELLS + ! LOOP
  \ `n 1 ?DO` with n = 0 would run through every number: guarded
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 1 > IF SHVAR-COUNT @ 1 DO
    I BEGIN
      DUP 0> IF DUP DUP 1- SV-LESS? ELSE 0 THEN
    WHILE
      DUP CELLS SV-ORDER + DUP 1 CELLS - 2DUP @ SWAP @ ROT ! SWAP !
      1-
    REPEAT DROP
  LOOP THEN
  SHVAR-COUNT @ 0 ?DO
    I SVO-NAME DUP CSTRLEN TYPE 61 EMIT
    I SVO-NAME LOOKUP-VAR ?DUP IF SQUOTED. ELSE S" ''" TYPE THEN CR
  LOOP ;

